    <node id="De Iside et Osiride">
      <data key="d0">De Iside et Osiride</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">De Iside et Osiride is a work by Plutarch discussing Egyptian deities and related etymologies, including the name Chem.&lt;SEP&gt;De Iside et Osiride is a treatise by Plutarch discussing Egyptian mythology and religious beliefs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95fcd0021c644a0db5d36e039f705056</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Typho">
      <data key="d0">Typho</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Typho (or Set) is the brother of Osiris known for his conflict with Osiris, representing chaos and the sea.&lt;SEP&gt;Typho is a mythic Egyptian figure, brother of Osiris and Isis, known for jealousy and killing his brother to usurp the throne, possibly symbolizing chaos, the sea, or the sun.&lt;SEP&gt;Typho is a mythological brother of Osiris and Isis, associated with jealousy, treason, and destruction in Egyptian and Mayab mythology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7dc5cd694c8e04568651d4ad21b8623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Osiris">
      <data key="d0">Osiris</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Osiris is a prominent and ancient Egyptian deity primarily associated with the afterlife, death, resurrection, and divine judgment. As the god of the dead and the ruler of the Amenti— the region of the underworld where souls are judged— Osiris plays a central role in Egyptian mythology concerning the journey of the soul after death. Depicted as the king of the Amenti, Osiris adjudicates the fate of souls based on their deeds during their mortal lives, embodying themes of justice and moral accountability.

In mythological narratives, Osiris is famously involved in stories of his death, dismemberment, and subsequent resurrection, symbolizing renewal and eternal life. His mythological cycle often depicts him as the brother of Typho (Set), and his death is a pivotal event that leads to his reanimation, which underscores his association with resurrection and immortality. Osiris's influence extends beyond the realm of death; he is also associated with the inundation of the Nile, which was vital for agriculture and fertility in ancient Egypt.

In addition to his underworld functions, Osiris is invoked in contexts related to love, divine favor, and childbirth, emphasizing his role in fertility and life-giving aspects. He is often depicted in mythological stories as the ruler of the West and the dead region of Amenti, symbolizing the transition from mortal life to the divine afterlife. Overall, Osiris embodies death, resurrection, fertility, and divine justice, making him one of the most significant deities in ancient Egyptian religion and mythology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a6047d3b960e8aa1f1e7a19add07e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7dc5cd694c8e04568651d4ad21b8623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a944ace9f08bbbd03f30787f1ce4bc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1210480b5c05b18ce59cb21e5b063e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0c76d7dabe39a2ef5714186a6126fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19630553a201da235d9c697d18820ca5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610584</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Isis">
      <data key="d0">Isis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Isis is a prominent Egyptian goddess deeply embedded in ancient Egyptian mythology and religious practices. She is traditionally recognized as the wife and sister of Osiris, with her mythology central to stories involving the birth of Horus and various intrigues, including those involving the deity Nephthis. Isis is associated with motherhood, fertility, magic, and nature, often depicted with wings and a vulture totem, symbols that emphasize her protective and nurturing qualities.

Throughout history, Isis has been invoked as a divine figure offering aid in childbirth and mercy during labor, highlighting her role as a protector of mothers and children. Her worship extended beyond Egyptian borders, notably being celebrated by Roman women during elaborate festivals lasting several days, during which she was honored through sacrifices and rituals. In mythological texts, Isis is sometimes equated with figures like Diana Lucina, reflecting her association with motherhood and the moon.

In addition to her mythological and religious significance, Isis was involved in divine mysteries and temple worship, where she was revered as a deity connected to divine knowledge and magical practices. She was also invoked in contexts of romantic deception, where her divine attributes were personified to serve as a pretext or symbol during such situations. The worship of Isis was linked to Egyptian religious practices related to other deities like Io, further integrating her into the broader pantheon and religious rituals. Her influence persisted through various cultural interactions, notably within Roman religious festivals, making Isis a universally recognized goddess symbolizing motherhood, magic, and divine protection across different civilizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7dc5cd694c8e04568651d4ad21b8623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1210480b5c05b18ce59cb21e5b063e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1af75b0ed4f2d647b015eaa8b0eac561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0c76d7dabe39a2ef5714186a6126fc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c9d540a7609b6ba4c69dd1651e5329e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-891695fdfbd534c521def8ccdb70c56d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c65528b916ab8f0ecae0b5c8df37467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19630553a201da235d9c697d18820ca5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610582</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aak">
      <data key="d0">Aak</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Aak is a turtle brother of Chaacmol and Moo, involved in mythological stories of jealousy and betrayal, killed by spears.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995</data>
