    <node id="Sun">
      <data key="d0">Sun</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sun is a central celestial body that plays a significant role across various ancient cultures and mythologies. It is often revered as a divine entity, symbolizing truth, divine power, and knowledge. In ancient Egyptian and Maya traditions, the Sun is depicted as the supreme god worshipped by these civilizations, embodying divine authority and power. The Sun's significance is evidenced by its symbolic representations, such as hieroglyphs in Egyptian culture and references in Mayan mythology, where it is associated with divine illumination and authority. The Maya specifically measured the Sun's position and declination, highlighting its importance in their astronomical and calendrical systems.

In mythology, the Sun is often depicted as an enemy to the Serpent, symbolizing the struggle between cosmic forces of light and darkness. It also held a role in mythological events where it was believed to hide its face, signifying periods of darkness or divine concealment. Furthermore, the Sun was thought to possess the power to cause extreme heat and oppression if its rays were to “cleave the earth,” representing both its nurturing light and potentially destructive force. Overall, the Sun is universally recognized as a star that provides essential light and heat to the Earth, serving as a vital sustainer of life and a powerful symbol of divine authority in various cultural contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sun is a star that the comets travel around and want to learn about, including its nature and significance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sun is a luminous celestial body that influences the movement of celestial objects, radiating heat and light and affecting their behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sun is depicted as consuming comets in a fantastical context, causing the comet to be forever closed.&lt;SEP&gt;The sun's flickering rays indicate daytime and good weather during the journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d60c6202de29d519891205dac45f966a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a944ace9f08bbbd03f30787f1ce4bc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90130c63183d6c112f655e298f1be97a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ae1d708e454e0e6bf3e72d6ca49fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4958bc3b034dd7b59c1c0df2fab2b8d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542ef42b55daca6f5424b791e6bd2765&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c40e9d9d6bed631bcdf0022bea513804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67b83f16f525fc1670d5a9242f36cd37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b84f4e93e76fa38e1e70383218e0788b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e8ad11358294f1889279ffec7807bc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26eeb163c449b14e147fabcfe98a220a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87bd881119189a688bfde0ea81ce77c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615495</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Serpent">
      <data key="d0">Serpent</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Serpent is a symbol and totem of Kukulcan, often depicted covered with feathers in Maya iconography.&lt;SEP&gt;The serpent containing the bust of Cay symbolizes divine or mythological elements, especially in relation to the effigy and the city ruins.&lt;SEP&gt;The Serpent represents a rival or inimical force to the Sun in Egyptian and Mayab mythologies.&lt;SEP&gt;A serpent is seen coiled nearby, representing a protecting spirit ready to defend Moo.&lt;SEP&gt;The sluggish serpent is depicted with specific symbolism in Egyptian mythology, possibly representing the asp or related to sacred animals with multiple heads, associated with drowsiness or mortally venomous sting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-964858730c8a8dc2179e59d3cb07c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19630553a201da235d9c697d18820ca5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aareris">
      <data key="d0">Aareris</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Aareris is a brother of Osiris, Isis, Typho, and Nephthis, and acts as a peacemaker in mythology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606668</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hindostan">
      <data key="d0">Hindostan</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Hindostan is a geographic region in Asia, historically associated with advanced civilizations and architectural development.&lt;SEP&gt;Hindostan (India) is associated with advanced architecture such as towers and temples.&lt;SEP&gt;Hindostan, another term for Northern India, exhibits religious rites involving symbols comparable to those in the American-Maya culture.&lt;SEP&gt;Hindostan is an Asian region with religious rites, including worship symbols similar to those found elsewhere.&lt;SEP&gt;Hindostan is referenced as a region where Maya traditions and customs have persisted and been modified over time, including specific practices and religious customs.&lt;SEP&gt;Hindostan refers to the Indian subcontinent where some gods from MAYAB are believed to have come from, indicating cultural and religious connections.&lt;SEP&gt;Hindostan (India) is referenced as a place related to the origin stories of Maya and other historical links.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b75bf9652f1afa0e3e6a35dbcd6728e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-964858730c8a8dc2179e59d3cb07c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f83c27282913cff5172f6cf0dfa303eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="India">
      <data key="d0">India</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">India, especially the regions of Hindostan and Ceylon, are linked to ancient architectural monuments and cultural developments.&lt;SEP&gt;India (Hindostan) is cited as a place where the Maya custom of carrying children astride and recording vows with hand impressions still exists.&lt;SEP&gt;India was where David Inglis served in the army and participated in significant military conflicts.&lt;SEP&gt;India is the country where Mrs. Inglis and her family lived and where Inglis fought in the Mutiny.&lt;SEP&gt;India is the country where Elsie was born and spent her childhood, characterized by plains and hills.&lt;SEP&gt;India is the place where Elsie’s brother Ernest died.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9d85bd579f0f4db276bf050b7458944&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-229b0d933abdd5cd0fec83a0fe5e865e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb6a025623f47200c8c812a059793950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c9efbd12b4170d1e884f967c9273e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e67cfa2aae65293abe96f3dae9b1d3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607217</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rama">
      <data key="d0">Rama</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Rama is a legendary hero in the Ramayana, depicted as fighting for his wife and associated with the lands of the Mayas.&lt;SEP&gt;Rama is referenced in connection with the Maya tradition of excluding foreigners and executing those who enter their territories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pourandara">
      <data key="d0">Pourandara</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pourandara is a deity who killed Maya with a thunderbolt out of jealousy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
