    </node>
    <node id="Hur (Hula)">
      <data key="d0">Hur (Hula)</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Hur City, also called Hula, is the primitive city founded by Maya tribes near the Euphrates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606670</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Khaldi">
      <data key="d0">Khaldi</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Khaldi is the name the Maya tribes gave to themselves, probably referring to their intrenched city.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606670</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Magi">
      <data key="d0">Magi</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Magi were a learned and influential group, possibly descendants or related to the Maya, established in Babylon.&lt;SEP&gt;The Magi are the learned men identified as Maya, regarded as architects, mathematicians, and astronomers.&lt;SEP&gt;The Magi are learned men among the Chaldees, their name meaning magician or learned man, related etymologically to Maya.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-964858730c8a8dc2179e59d3cb07c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a22b4bcd91dd2d8b9fa5db8f58d88ab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606670</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rab-Mag">
      <data key="d0">Rab-Mag</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Rab-Mag is the chief or old man title used for Maya leaders associated with their religious and scholarly roles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606670</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urukh">
      <data key="d0">Urukh</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Urukh is a king whose signet cylinder shows that the Maya dressed similarly to figures depicted in Yucatan murals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606670</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Moo">
      <data key="d0">Moo</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Moo is a prominent figure within Maya mythology and religious practices, recognized as both a goddess and a mythological character. She is frequently associated with divine phenomena, lighting fires on her altar, and is represented symbolically as a macaw, a totem animal that later was honored and worshipped in Izamal. Moo is also linked to celestial and fiery imagery, often associated with the sun and the serpent, and appears in various murals and sculptures that depict her involvement in mythic themes of love, enmity, and tragedy.

In mythological narratives, Moo's story interweaves familial and romantic relationships. She is identified as the wife of the warrior Chaacmol and also as his sister, a relationship that defies conventional social norms within mythic contexts. She is described as the sister of Aac (or Aak), whom she married to Chaacmol, highlighting elements of mythological complexity involving kinship and union. Moo's relationships are entangled with stories of betrayal, jealousy, and violence, reflecting the tumultuous nature of divine and mortal relationships in Maya mythology.

Additionally, Moo is revered beyond her mythic stories as a divine figure associated with the goddesshood. She is worshiped among Maya tribes and later honored as the Queen of Chichen, where she was venerated after her death, emphasizing her importance in religious practices. Her worship involved divine phenomena, with her appearing as a macaw and lighting fires on her altar, symbolizing her connection to the sun and fiery elements. Her depiction in murals and sculptures often emphasizes her role as a protector and a figure of divine authority, embodying powerful symbols of femininity, celestial influence, and mythic conflict.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa4a04bbf52d96486efcb2e364d70456&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d60c6202de29d519891205dac45f966a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-964858730c8a8dc2179e59d3cb07c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b549627ac943f03650e7d7278845faf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606673</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chichen">
      <data key="d0">Chichen</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Chichen is an ancient city of notable archaeological significance, primarily associated with the Maya civilization in the Yucatan Peninsula. It is renowned for its rich array of monuments, murals, and distinctive architecture that reflect various religious and cultural shifts over its extensive history. The site features elaborate structures including temples, bas-reliefs, and murals that depict mythological scenes, religious practices, and attire, offering valuable insights into Maya society and spirituality.

Additionally, Chichen has garnered attention for its inscriptions, which intriguingly include characters resembling Egyptian, Chaldean, and Etruscan scripts, suggesting possible cultural exchanges or influences beyond Mesoamerica. The murals at Chichen also depict figures with diverse features, such as African characteristics, indicating a potential link to broader cultural or ethnographic interactions. The city is often mentioned alongside other significant sites like Uxmal, emphasizing its prominent role within Mayan cultural history.

Historically, Chichen was destroyed by Nahualts, a detail that highlights its turbulent history. Its archaeological records include murals portraying mythological scenes and individuals with deformities like Moo, which underscore complex religious symbolism and societal perceptions. Overall, Chichen stands out as a key archaeological site that encapsulates the religious, artistic, and possibly intercultural dimensions of ancient Maya civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa4a04bbf52d96486efcb2e364d70456&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-469abfadf4d8fb7475eafd715164f218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90130c63183d6c112f655e298f1be97a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0986a653a68c393fd5247a90b35adc69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chaacmol">
      <data key="d0">Chaacmol</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Chaacmol is a significant figure within ancient Maya culture, depicted extensively in murals, sculptures, and relics found across sites such as Chichen-Itza and Uxmal. He is associated with warfare, often portrayed as a warrior or deity involved in battles, and is connected to martial symbols like shields with green spots representing totems, emphasizing his role as a god of war and protector of his followers. In some representations, Chaacmol is linked to funerary practices, with archaeological remains indicating that he was a figure of importance, possibly an ancient ruler or warrior whose remains were deposited in stone urns within mausolea decorated with elaborate sculptures.

The iconography associated with Chaacmol includes depictions of blue-painted figures and mask-like statues reflecting religious and ritual symbolism, sometimes involving sacrifices. He appears in murals portraying mythic themes, such as land of the West, and is sometimes represented as a spotted tiger or leopard, underscoring his mythic or divine nature. Numerous artifacts suggest his connection to ancient Mayan ceremonial life, including funerary chambers and relics indicating his reverence extended to ancestral worship and divine protection.

Additionally, Chaacmol is linked to a mythic narrative involving familial and political conflicts, such as being killed by Aac amid jealousy and treachery among Mayan tribes. He is also associated with high-ranking priesthood, with records mentioning a brother of Aak and Moo, who served as a high priest or pontiff involved in mythological stories of divine intervention. In some accounts, Chaacmol is connected to superstition and totemic protection, with symbols of death by spear reflecting ancestral beliefs. Overall, Chaacmol emerges as a multifaceted figure embodying war, religion, ancestor veneration, and mythic storytelling within the ancient Maya civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa4a04bbf52d96486efcb2e364d70456&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a6047d3b960e8aa1f1e7a19add07e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b991502e22e90fb761c5591cdca0b88e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d60c6202de29d519891205dac45f966a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08ffcfc6ec928be11797840c2e48ac2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-964858730c8a8dc2179e59d3cb07c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f65c2b617b2b75df32a484e145b034fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-469abfadf4d8fb7475eafd715164f218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a944ace9f08bbbd03f30787f1ce4bc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7d407dd88d4f39cbd7f09e60b40325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90130c63183d6c112f655e298f1be97a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f83c27282913cff5172f6cf0dfa303eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b549627ac943f03650e7d7278845faf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0986a653a68c393fd5247a90b35adc69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Carians">
      <data key="d0">Carians</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Carians are an ancient people from Asia Minor, whose language and customs are linked to Maya by historical and linguistic comparisons.&lt;SEP&gt;Carians is a reference to an ancient people whose cultural links are suggested to be connected with African and Maya traditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Carians are a group historically located in Asia Minor, who at some point settled and established themselves there after spreading terror among surrounding populations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Carians are an ancient people believed to have established themselves in Asia Minor, possibly descended from Maya tribes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a22b4bcd91dd2d8b9fa5db8f58d88ab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0986a653a68c393fd5247a90b35adc69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uxmal">
      <data key="d0">Uxmal</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Uxmal is a renowned archaeological site located in the Yucatán Peninsula that showcases the grandeur of ancient Maya civilization through its well-preserved ruins, temples, palaces, and sculptures. It was a densely populated and highly advanced city, serving as a significant cultural and religious center. The site features notable architectural elements such as a grand staircase, sacrificial altar, and mythological sculptures, reflecting the Maya's artistic and religious ingenuity.

Uxmal became the capital of the region following the destruction of Chichen Itza by invaders, and it is associated with symbols of the Nahuatl religion. The site contains inscriptions and frescoes dating from the Maya period, some of which have intrigued researchers due to their unusual inclusion of symbols and signs that resemble Egyptian, Chaldean, and Etruscan characters, suggesting possible ancient intercultural exchanges or symbolic significance. Among its monuments, there are references to the "land of the four mouths," indicating a theme of four-fold division, and symbols linked to natural forces and the sun.

The archaeological discoveries at Uxmal include relics of Nahuatl phallic worship and ritualistic practices, along with preserved red hand imprints on wall surfaces. The site also holds significant religious artifacts, such as a high priest effigy and a figure associated with Cay, and features its coat-of-arms with symbols of the land of the sun. Additionally, the site includes monuments emphasizing the number seven, represented through mounds and turrets, and symbols of the natural world and cosmic forces.

Overall, Uxmal stands as an important testament to Maya architectural, artistic, and spiritual achievements, embodying a complex cultural landscape rich in symbolism, religious practice, and historical significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06e492003a084ec018b849a310a4e995&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65e48b610f2a391366b1dc455dacc28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa4a04bbf52d96486efcb2e364d70456&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b991502e22e90fb761c5591cdca0b88e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e19091b4b68c6f4b29d836f6d5f11c0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d60c6202de29d519891205dac45f966a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7dc5cd694c8e04568651d4ad21b8623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cf3140092a745267effea6e9a53aa65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a944ace9f08bbbd03f30787f1ce4bc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7d407dd88d4f39cbd7f09e60b40325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
