    </node>
    <node id="Mayas">
      <data key="d0">Mayas</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mayas are an ancient indigenous civilization from Central America, primarily concentrated in regions such as Yucatan and surrounding areas. They are renowned for their complex cities, monumental architecture, and contributions to archaeology, highlighting their status as a highly advanced and enduring civilization. The Mayas developed sophisticated writing systems that employed phonetic, symbolic, and ideographic signs, allowing them to document historical, astronomical, and cultural knowledge.

Culturally, the Mayas believed in the immortality of the soul and the concept of reincarnation after death. Their burial practices often involved placing riches and trade implements in tombs and sometimes abandoning their houses following death, reflecting complex ritual practices related to their spiritual beliefs. They also engaged in various ritualistic acts, including potential acts of ritual cannibalism such as eating hearts, and customs like filing teeth, which held cultural and symbolic significance.

The Mayas demonstrated remarkable advancements in astronomy and divination, parallel to practices observed in ancient Egypt, including the division of the year into cycles based on celestial observations. Their number system was notably built on a base of five and twenty, with the number seven frequently appearing in their architecture and symbolic representations, indicating a rich mathematical and symbolic tradition.

Mythologically, the Mayas share certain narrative themes with other ancient cultures, such as stories about culture heroes resembling Osiris, which underscores the deep spiritual and mythic aspects of their civilization. Their narrative and religious practices reflect a complex worldview centered around cosmic cycles, deities, and the afterlife.

Overall, the Mayas are portrayed as a great and powerful civilization, characterized by their advanced scientific, architectural, and spiritual achievements. They possess a rich cultural heritage, with their language, customs, and historical identity continuing to be recognized by the native peoples of America and studied for their profound influence on world history and archeology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ba6980ac19f47019ce87374bfbf5be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa4a04bbf52d96486efcb2e364d70456&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a6047d3b960e8aa1f1e7a19add07e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e19091b4b68c6f4b29d836f6d5f11c0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53318967e1e0c7a354ac926405083d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7dc5cd694c8e04568651d4ad21b8623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-964858730c8a8dc2179e59d3cb07c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90130c63183d6c112f655e298f1be97a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0986a653a68c393fd5247a90b35adc69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc</data>
