    <node id="Mayo_lo">
      <data key="d0">Mayo_lo</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mayo_lo appears to be a city or site associated with the Mayas, possibly a variation of MAYAB.&lt;SEP&gt;Mayo_lo is a city in Africa whose name resembles Maya, suggesting a cultural or historical link.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0986a653a68c393fd5247a90b35adc69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="African inhabitants">
      <data key="d0">African inhabitants</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">The inhabitants of Equatorial Africa have customs similar to the Mayas, including making offerings over graves and sacrificing slaves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="African slaves">
      <data key="d0">African slaves</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">Slaves are said to be slain over graves of kings and chieftains as offerings, with their spirits believed to join the spirits of the deceased.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Maya tombs">
      <data key="d0">Maya tombs</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Maya tombs contained riches, implements of trade or profession, and sometimes blood sacrificed during funerals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="African houses">
      <data key="d0">African houses</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">African inhabitants abandon their houses or villages after death, believing spirits of the deceased linger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Musical instruments">
      <data key="d0">Musical instruments</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mayas used drums like the Tunkul and Zacatan in religious festivals and dances.&lt;SEP&gt;The text references many Roman and Greek musical instruments, such as _tibia_, _cornu_, _tuba_, _lituus_, _tympanum_, and _cymbalum_.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613877</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tunkul">
      <data key="d0">Tunkul</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A cylindrical drum hollowed from a tree trunk, played by striking tongues with rubber balls, producing loud sound heard over miles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Zacatan">
      <data key="d0">Zacatan</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A drum hollowed from a tree trunk, with stretched skin, played by beating on the skin, used in religious and ceremonial contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ngoma">
      <data key="d0">Ngoma</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of African war drum, formed from hollow logs, sometimes nine feet long, used to call clans together.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fan drum">
      <data key="d0">Fan drum</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A drum similar to the Zacatan, made of hollowed wood with stretched skin, played with sticks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606689</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="fetish houses">
      <data key="d0">fetish houses</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Huts or shrines containing skulls, idols, or statues, located at village entrances or sacred sites in Yucatan and Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skulls">
      <data key="d0">Skulls</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Skulls of ancestors or idols are placed in fetish houses or shrines, serving as spiritual symbols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="small idols">
      <data key="d0">small idols</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Small idols are placed at village entrances, roads, and temples, representing gods of love, dancers, or other deities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="African dogs">
      <data key="d0">African dogs</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">African dogs are described as small, lean, with long ears, long muzzle, curly tail, short hair, and yellowish color, used as watch dogs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yucatan dogs">
      <data key="d0">Yucatan dogs</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Native dogs of Yucatan, similar in breed to African dogs, with long ears, muzzle, and tail.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="African warriors">
      <data key="d0">African warriors</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">African warriors believe that wearing a charmed leopard skin makes them spear-proof.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inhabitants of Equatorial Africa">
      <data key="d0">Inhabitants of Equatorial Africa</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">They practice customs similar to the Mayas, including graves offerings, sacrifices, and house abandonment after death.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Du Chaillu">
      <data key="d0">Du Chaillu</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Du Chaillu was a traveler who described African drums and rituals, and observed native dogs and warriors' beliefs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stanley">
      <data key="d0">Stanley</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Stanley was a traveler who reported observations of African drums and cultural practices related to danger and clan calling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="African towns">
      <data key="d0">African towns</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">African towns contain fetish houses, skulls, idols, and are important centers for ancestral and spiritual worship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a987c9c6b375c5a920c9be05f99a27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Merida">
      <data key="d0">Merida</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Merida is a city in Yucatan known for poor drainage and large populations, leading to environmental issues and health problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cozumel">
      <data key="d0">Cozumel</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Cozumel is an island in Yucatan where people should theoretically only die of old age or accidents due to its healthy environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mangroves">
      <data key="d0">Mangroves</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Mangroves grow along the coast in the lowlands, forming dense thickets with crooked roots that create natural barriers.&lt;SEP&gt;Mangroves are salt-tolerant trees growing along lakeshores in Yucatan, forming natural barriers and providing habitat for waterfowl.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a980d05b1d871a902a88d111d028517</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Snipe">
      <data key="d0">Snipe</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Snipe is a medium-sized bird inhabiting the lakes and coastal regions of Yucatan.&lt;SEP&gt;Snipe is a character who is feeling blue and disconsolate, observing his surroundings with cynicism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3def92aa574a943cf57899f915c3bcae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612527</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Crane">
      <data key="d0">Crane</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Crane is a large bird found among the lakes and mangroves of Yucatan.&lt;SEP&gt;A crane is used to handle and transport ingots into and out of the soaking pits, enabling large-scale operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8158e0db68586c52c3a94ca3b5a762c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flamingos">
      <data key="d0">Flamingos</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Flamingos are brightly colored birds that inhabit the lakes of Yucatan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sea Gulls">
      <data key="d0">Sea Gulls</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Sea gulls are diverse waterfowl seen in Yucatan's lakes and coastal areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606690</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelican">
      <data key="d0">Pelican</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelican is a large water bird that frequents the lakes and coasts of Yucatan.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pelican screams and flaps her wings during the discussion about honey origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab5a2da379766b69f7bf3153cb0f2c47</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764608171</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sea Birds">
      <data key="d0">Sea Birds</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Numerous sea birds and waterfowl, including snipe, crane, flamingos, and gulls, inhabit the small lakes and coastal wetlands.&lt;SEP&gt;Sea birds refer collectively to various bird species, including snipe, crane, flamingos, gulls, and pelicans, inhabiting Yucatan's lakeside environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Sea birds fly around the rocks of the Devil's Frying-pan, suggesting a rich seabird presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a980d05b1d871a902a88d111d028517&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9947d8e5a23dab49c88d36cae1db57a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615527</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Climate">
      <data key="d0">Climate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The climate in Yucatan is mild, with temperatures averaging around 80°F, rising in July and August, seldom falling below 65°F in December.&lt;SEP&gt;Yucatan's climate is mild and healthy due to sea breezes, characterized by consistent temperatures and high humidity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8d3ba708724915d4c95066561027635</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Fever">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Fever</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Yellow fever is a disease endemic to Yucatan, appearing in epidemic forms especially after heavy rains.&lt;SEP&gt;A disease that attacks Rose while in New Orleans, leading to her death.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-297e2cf3fdd658f56fcb73ec4f707e74</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611986</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Malaria">
      <data key="d0">Malaria</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Malaria is a disease associated with the region's moisture and decomposition of organic matter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fevers">
      <data key="d0">Fevers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Intermittent fevers, including endemic yellow fever, are common health issues in Yucatan related to environmental factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rainy Season">
      <data key="d0">Rainy Season</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The rainy season in Yucatan, especially if excessive, often leads to outbreaks of diseases such as smallpox and yellow fever.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="D rains">
      <data key="d0">D rains</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Heavy rains in Yucatan contribute to poor drainage and increase health risks like epidemics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vegetation">
      <data key="d0">Vegetation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Yucatan's vegetation is luxuriant due to moisture, with many large trees whose roots grow on the surface and penetrate stone crevices.&lt;SEP&gt;Vegetation in Yucatan varies seasonally, with lush growth after rains and barrenness during dry months.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Soil">
      <data key="d0">Soil</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The soil in Yucatan varies, with arable loam suitable for crops, and red clay supporting cultivation of sugar cane and yuca.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1556645e734e01c75f40b96c3891db6a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sugar Cane">
      <data key="d0">Sugar Cane</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sugar cane is cultivated on fertile soil in parts of Yucatan for producing sweet products.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yuca Root">
      <data key="d0">Yuca Root</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Yuca root is a staple crop grown in Yucatan, used for producing starch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Trees">
      <data key="d0">Trees</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Large trees of significant size grow in Yucatan, often from slabs or stones, seeking moisture.&lt;SEP&gt;Trees are mentioned as sources of leaves to be plucked, symbolizing nature's resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Trees are perennial plants with roots, branches, leaves, and sometimes produce fruits or seeds, forming the foundational flora in various ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64ce64b0a51b17a08eb9a938fb755d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8493bf9cd90b8f6b1a63947e3d6ecb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613891</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Seasons">
      <data key="d0">Seasons</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Yucatan experiences distinct dry and wet seasons, with vegetation changing accordingly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dry Season">
      <data key="d0">Dry Season</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">From January to June, during which trees lose leaves, and the land appears parched.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rain">
      <data key="d0">Rain</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Refreshed landscape and vegetation renewal occur with the first showers in June.&lt;SEP&gt;Rain is a weather phenomenon occurring as the characters go outside, causing delays.&lt;SEP&gt;Rain is a weather phenomenon causing rainfall, which wets Gallegher's clothes and contributes to his muddy condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bae0a02b7ee1aadc768d0246ed69ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8cf348102921436615f31eec7112c69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1030072f07138e17f0700081a9158b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612493</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stagnant Waters">
      <data key="d0">Stagnant Waters</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Stagnant waters are formed due to poor drainage in Yucatan, providing breeding grounds for disease-carrying miasmas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Floods">
      <data key="d0">Floods</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Floods occur during the rainy season, contributing to environmental unsanitary conditions and health epidemics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a838c08843884c0f3a0816d2b39bddaf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
