    <node id="Deity">
      <data key="d0">Deity</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deity refers to gods worshipped in different cultures where hand imprints serve as symbols of prayer and vows.&lt;SEP&gt;The deity inhabiting the grove is a divine presence believed to dwell in the sacred space.&lt;SEP&gt;The sacred name of the Deity is inscribed on cork bark tied to the enchanted Argan trees to break spells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b75bf9652f1afa0e3e6a35dbcd6728e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64ce64b0a51b17a08eb9a938fb755d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33cad941dca515ea1feb8e87a23c7dfb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614526</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mayab">
      <data key="d0">Mayab</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mayab is a country whose name is derived from its stony soil's absorbent properties, connecting it to ancient Maya culture and language.&lt;SEP&gt;Mayab, in the context of the text, is associated with shared religious and cultural symbols with other regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mayab is an ancient or traditional name for the Maya region, and also a name for learned men’s libraries associated with Maya civilization.&lt;SEP&gt;Mayab is a region characterized by highly porous soil, with its name derived from its water-absorbing properties, and associated with the Maya inhabitants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b75bf9652f1afa0e3e6a35dbcd6728e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-964858730c8a8dc2179e59d3cb07c3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-469abfadf4d8fb7475eafd715164f218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eea61287f7c7aac52c315ee2cca003fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90130c63183d6c112f655e298f1be97a</data>
