    <node id="City of Chichen">
      <data key="d0">City of Chichen</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Chichen is an ancient city known as the city of holy and learned men, with symbols of worship distinct from Uxmal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Can">
      <data key="d0">Can</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Can is a founding family of the cities Uxmal and Chichen, worshiping deities symbolized by a mastodon's head.&lt;SEP&gt;Can is a high priest at Uxmal, depicted among ruins, associated with the serpent motif and the effigy found in the city.&lt;SEP&gt;Can is a divine figure within the myth, contained within the serpent and linked to the effigy of Cay, symbolizing origin and divine power.&lt;SEP&gt;Can is identified as the founder of Uxmal and Chichen cities, and the father of Cay, linking him to early city establishment.&lt;SEP&gt;Can is a figure whose spirit is a serpent, and is the progenitor or totem of the people associated with Moo and the Mayab culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7d407dd88d4f39cbd7f09e60b40325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f47d3b712e0953b518a70453701f3ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fire">
      <data key="d0">Fire</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The term "Fire" encompasses both its symbolic and practical significance across different contexts. Historically, fire was worshipped by the Maya and was represented hieroglyphically in their script, indicating its profound spiritual importance. It was also associated with the Chaldeans and Hindoos, reflecting its central role in various religious traditions and cultural practices. In a practical and modern context, fire frequently refers to destructive incidents that require emergency response. For example, fires can break out unexpectedly, leading to chaos, widespread damage, injuries, and even the collapse of structures such as hotels and surrounding buildings. Such incidents often originate from sources like lamps in laundry areas, with dense smoke complicating evacuation efforts and causing guests to rush out in their night clothes.

Fires can consume multiple buildings, including hotels and other infrastructures, resulting in extensive property damage. Additionally, fire symbolizes destruction in a more metaphorical or cultural sense, as seen in references to flames consuming the Poet or representing the flames of a pyre, signifying both physical destruction and spiritual purification or transition. There are also instances where fire appears as a natural or activity-related phenomenon, such as flickering flames near a clay-pit that provide light or indicate ongoing activity at a site.

Overall, "Fire" embodies both its historical and cultural significance as a worshipped and symbolic element, as well as its role in causing and representing destruction through accidental or incidental occurrences, highlighting its multifaceted nature.&lt;SEP&gt;A clear fire reaching 18 feet in height, demonstrating the intensity of flames humans can tolerate.&lt;SEP&gt;The fire was built by Tom for camping during the night on their journey.&lt;SEP&gt;The fire in the study sets a warm scene and is a backdrop to the contemplative mood of the young man.&lt;SEP&gt;The fire is a central feature in the story where Toto and friends gather and warm themselves.&lt;SEP&gt;A bright hearth that provides warmth and ambiance for the gathering.&lt;SEP&gt;Fire is involved in the environment near the fireplace and causes the mice to feel cold after experiencing the fiery heat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd305fbce6d35b0ef2d81f49f11311ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf33d450055327352cd4545502529ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bf8943ee8dd238feb8fd1d1ea67c1d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b75c5f4f7c0385a783e34fd555b4a0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29eddb27a800eedb331c3ac515bfc570&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19d80edbff78a673a3b603b37c60a64a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c3e181a56877f38400ce12e934ff548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0ab09362082e7df27936fc84baa9ce3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b50f15854f0d7a894e3abd39a81c4ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59ad55bacf8e991f7ec32f1bd324e6cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-308dc1f4e23e0e8a33feabd4ae43b18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a19e01a71eb685d36416d4d22bde8d62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca7afa2615c5868318d15a4ab2c224c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d3e57d404581a83d442385fc9e650dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c99f9b60b678b965df35f51c42867839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b60d4926e5d4ac6ded55aa7e91fca4c3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613422</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Invasions">
      <data key="d0">Invasions</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasions from the west, particularly by Nahualts, influenced Yucatec religion and architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Symbols">
      <data key="d0">Symbols</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Symbols are engravings and sculptures on Yucatec monuments representing deities, animals, and religious beliefs.&lt;SEP&gt;The symbols on the placard resemble a cartouche, representing cultural or spiritual significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Symbols are ritual objects such as a rice measure, an abacus, a mace carved with a dragon and phoenix, used in ceremonial displays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7dc5cd694c8e04568651d4ad21b8623&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d338a477bdce675ba495c45901f2f4f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c3cc3199e9836aa61baa142bf1c2aae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611206</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Maya Idols">
      <data key="d0">Maya Idols</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Maya idols were few, small, often buried to prevent desecration, and represented their gods and religion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Deities">
      <data key="d0">Deities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deities are gods worshipped by the Maya, including Moo, Chaacmol, Kukulcan, and others, associated with various symbols and practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Deities refer to divine beings in mythology, involved in stories of vengeance, fertility, and destruction.&lt;SEP&gt;Deities are gods that the speaker references in context of blessings, curses, or divine rites.&lt;SEP&gt;Deities refer to gods worshiped in Roman religious practices, often involved in prayers and sacrifices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f54377361394a10337ee324609f05a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3a6fd5e17721f4573f70b6578a7f23a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52ca25c65db54cb74a2c1bd788c198c8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610610</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kukulcan">
      <data key="d0">Kukulcan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Kukulcan is a culture hero and deity, depicted as a winged serpent, worshiped by the Maya, Mexicans, and Quiches.&lt;SEP&gt;Kukulcan, also known as Quetzalcoatl, is a prominent deity and figure in Mesoamerican mythology associated with Maya and Aztec cultures.&lt;SEP&gt;Kukulcan is also the name of a prominent Maya deity often associated with the pyramid at Chichen Itza.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-469abfadf4d8fb7475eafd715164f218</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mexicans">
      <data key="d0">Mexicans</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mexicans worship Kukulcan as the feathered serpent deity.&lt;SEP&gt;Mexico, home to the Aztecs, where various ancient musical instruments such as pipes and flutes have been discovered.&lt;SEP&gt;Mexicans are indigenous people who made and used the _teponaztli_, a percussion instrument, and developed other musical contrivances.&lt;SEP&gt;Indigenous people of Mexico known for using bells called _yotl_.&lt;SEP&gt;Mexicans, in the context of the text, refers to the people of Mexico who had their own songs for historical record, war, love, and sacred chants for religious observances.&lt;SEP&gt;The Mexicans employed the conch trumpet in their religious observances, similar to other Asian practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-863ecd34d59b90a44bfad0a61b39cd89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93589ec60a74676e9a13006653f3b90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82544a13bf6799b0efc4d2f2a5e62661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73ed7c284b3a9d9caa2237f4d2af971d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quiches">
      <data key="d0">Quiches</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The Quiches worship Kukulcan as Cucumatz.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wall Carvings">
      <data key="d0">Wall Carvings</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Wall carvings depict the emblems of deities, such as Kukulcan, and ceremonial vestments made of feathers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cucumatz">
      <data key="d0">Cucumatz</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Cucumatz is a deity worshiped by the Quiches as Kukulcan, the feathered serpent and culture hero.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bagre">
      <data key="d0">Bagre</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bagre is a silurus fish mentioned in the text, indicating a specific type of fish with cultural or symbolic significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Niblu">
      <data key="d0">Niblu</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Niblu is a Maya name for a fish, associated with thanksgiving in the context of the name Nipru.&lt;SEP&gt;Niblu is identified as the thanksgiving fish, implying a cultural or ritual significance associated with this fish.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7d407dd88d4f39cbd7f09e60b40325</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Egyptian characters">
      <data key="d0">Egyptian characters</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Egyptian characters inscribed on the mastodon head symbolize ancient Egyptian mysticism and connection to divine symbols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TZAA">
      <data key="d0">TZAA</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">TZAA is an inscription on the mastodon head meaning "that which is necessary," reflecting symbolic or mystical significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BELIS">
      <data key="d0">BELIS</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">BELIS is depicted as a goddess, the wife of Bel-nipru, representing female power and an important deity, known as the Great Goddess.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bel-nipru">
      <data key="d0">Bel-nipru</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Bel-nipru is the consort of BELIS, associated with divine power and possibly linked to Mesopotamian deities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mulita">
      <data key="d0">Mulita</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mulita is a name signifying “many here” or “many in me,” representing the mother of many gods in mythological context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Enuta">
      <data key="d0">Enuta</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Enuta signifies “I am here before anybody,” indicating a maternal and primordial aspect as the mother of gods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HEA">
      <data key="d0">HEA</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">HEA is a divine figure or name associated with water and knowledge, linked to the god of knowledge and science, Oannes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hoa">
      <data key="d0">Hoa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Hoa, also called Oannes, is a god who brought civilization to Mesopotamia, depicted as half man, half fish, associated with water and teachings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oannes">
      <data key="d0">Oannes</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oannes is a mythological figure sent from the Indian Ocean to bring civilization and knowledge to Mesopotamia, described as half fish, half man.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dav-Kina">
      <data key="d0">Dav-Kina</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dav-Kina is depicted as the wife of Hoa, meaning “chief lady” or “the mistress,” associated with divine feminine power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tab-Kin">
      <data key="d0">Tab-Kin</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tab-Kin signifies “rays of the sun,” symbolizing enlightenment, civilization, and illumination brought by Hoa and Dav-Kina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sin">
      <data key="d0">Sin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sin, also called Hurki, is the moon deity associated with the moon, the month, and the shining or brightness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hurki">
      <data key="d0">Hurki</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Hurki, also known as Sin, is the Maya moon god, related to the sun and moon, and associated with architecture and strength.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Zin">
      <data key="d0">Zin</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Zin signifies extension or stretching, representing the universe’s expansion in Maya symbolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Zinil">
      <data key="d0">Zinil</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Zinil refers to the extension of the universe, symbolizing cosmic expansion or universality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bel-Zuna">
      <data key="d0">Bel-Zuna</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bel-Zuna is a title for Hurki/Sin, meaning “lord of the building,” symbolizing architecture, strength, and fortification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="U">
      <data key="d0">U</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">U is a Maya term signifying moon and is associated with a city where Hurki/Sin had a great temple, named Ur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="San">
      <data key="d0">San</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">San, or Sansi, is the Sun God, ruler of fire and day, illuminating the heavens and earth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Zamal">
      <data key="d0">Zamal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Zamal signifies dawn or morning in Maya, representing the beginning of the day and the sun’s light.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vul">
      <data key="d0">Vul</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Vul, also called Iva, is the prince of the powers of the air, associated with the tempest, thunderbolt, and wind.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Iva">
      <data key="d0">Iva</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Iva (Vul) is the deity of wind and air, wielding the thunderbolt, symbolizing weather and atmospheric power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hiba">
      <data key="d0">Hiba</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hiba in Maya means to rub or chafe, associated here with the tempest and atmospheric phenomena.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hul">
      <data key="d0">Hul</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hul is an arrow in Maya symbolism, representing the god of rain and atmospheric phenomena, depicted with a flaming sword.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ishtar">
      <data key="d0">Ishtar</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ishtar, or Nana, is the Chaldean Venus, associated with desire and celestial phenomena.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nana">
      <data key="d0">Nana</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nana means the great mother, representing the maternal figure in Maya culture.&lt;SEP&gt;Nana is a goddess linked to Venus and desire, associated with celestial and mythological symbolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ix">
      <data key="d0">Ix</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ix is a feminine article in Maya, signifying “she,” used in names or titles indicating femininity or divine feminine traits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tac">
      <data key="d0">Tac</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tac or Tal is a verb signifying desire or craving, used here to describe the goddess's desire to satisfy wants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606703</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ixtal">
      <data key="d0">Ixtal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ixtal signifies the desire to satisfy physical or material wants, related to the goddess’s attributes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606703</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ena-Te">
      <data key="d0">Ena-Te</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ena-Te suggests “first here” or “before anybody,” symbolizing primacy and origin, associated with maternal aspects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80579f75badf4bd09adb3a115b482e00</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606703</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Monuments of Yucatan">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Monuments of Yucatan</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The ancient monuments of Yucatan include architectural structures and inscriptions that have been deciphered to understand Maya civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606703</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chaldeans">
      <data key="d0">Chaldeans</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">The Chaldeans were an ancient civilization whose monuments and mode of building are compared to those of the Maya.&lt;SEP&gt;Chaldeans refer to an ancient civilization associated with the creation myth, ruled by a woman in the story of world creation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606703</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Babylon">
      <data key="d0">Babylon</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Babylon is an ancient city, mentioned as a place where the Maya or their learned representatives, the Magi, established themselves.&lt;SEP&gt;Babylon was an ancient city known for its architecture, including the tower of Babel and the Birs-i-nimrud.&lt;SEP&gt;Babylon was an ancient city associated with the presence of the deity Ra and the development of cuneiform writing.&lt;SEP&gt;Babylon was an ancient city where the native name was _ka-ra_, meaning city of the infinite truth.&lt;SEP&gt;Babylon is a historical place of exile for many Jews, which the Jews of the Atlas did not experience or relate to.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d7d407dd88d4f39cbd7f09e60b40325&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a22b4bcd91dd2d8b9fa5db8f58d88ab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4996254e95ccc6b78afba2bb6b96949</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614577</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ur">
      <data key="d0">Ur</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ur, also called Hur, was the capital of the primitive Chaldees during the time of Abraham.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606703</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Herodotus">
      <data key="d0">Herodotus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Herodotus was an ancient historian who described funeral sacrifices among the Scythians, providing historical context for similar practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Herodotus is an ancient historian noting language similarities between the Carians and Maya.&lt;SEP&gt;Herodotus was an ancient Greek historian who described ceilings in Egyptian buildings and tombs that resemble the brick vaults found in Yucatan.&lt;SEP&gt;Herodotus was an ancient Greek historian whose works include descriptions of customs and general historical accounts, including information about Roman women and their practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Herodotus was an ancient Greek historian who documented the large influence of Lydia on Greek culture and traditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Herodotus was an ancient Greek historian who described musical instruments, including the lyre, in his writings.&lt;SEP&gt;Herodotus was an ancient Greek historian who mentioned peoples of North Africa, often relaying fables or interesting facts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a22b4bcd91dd2d8b9fa5db8f58d88ab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc0c02df8cb82b091fb73a066662a32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9ab58d75e0d33f2fb45c933ce510e99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Coffins">
      <data key="d0">Coffins</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Coffins found in Yucatan are arranged side by side in artificial mounds, similar to those in Chaldea.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
