    <node id="Chaldean Mode of Building">
      <data key="d0">Chaldean Mode of Building</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Chaldeans built their sacred edifices with mounds of superposed platforms, similar to Mayan architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965b5b39cb58a29f56a43495ef18b6ff</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606703</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Her Name">
      <data key="d0">Her Name</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The name of the Maya goddess, composed of "ix" (she) and "tac" (to have a desire to satisfy a want), meaning "she who desires to satisfy a corporal inclination."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba43616fff0b8112616e172e23cfa8d7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606703</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thalatth">
      <data key="d0">Thalatth</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thalatth is a woman in Chaldean creation myths, associated with ruling over monstrous animals in darkness and water.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606704</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thalassa">
      <data key="d0">Thalassa</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Thalassa is a Greek term for the sea, used to describe a mythological woman associated with water and darkness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606704</data>
