    <node id="Urkuh">
      <data key="d0">Urkuh</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Urkuh was a great architect and king of the Chaldees, credited with planning ancient Mesopotamian monuments resembling Maya construction styles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606704</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HUK">
      <data key="d0">HUK</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">HUK is a Maya word meaning to make everything, used as part of the etymology of Urkuh’s name, indicating construction or making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606704</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LUK">
      <data key="d0">LUK</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">LUK is a Maya word meaning mud, combined with HUK to form Urkuh, signifying "he who makes everything of mud."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4953aba767e12ab2c6cc0f2d20adf220</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606704</data>
