    <node id="King">
      <data key="d0">King</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The term "King" encompasses a diverse range of meanings and representations across different contexts. Primarily, it refers to a monarch or ruler, symbolizing leadership, authority, and social rank. In historical and cultural settings, a king is conceived as a traditional monarch who embodies stability and sovereignty; for instance, a king who was alive during a narrator's childhood exemplifies such a role, representing conventional authority within a societal framework.

Furthermore, a king can be depicted as an active political figure involved in governance and diplomacy. For example, a king who appointed the narrator's lord as an ambassador played a part in political affairs, and was the monarch expected to come and adjourn Parliament, highlighting his role in state governance. In official and ceremonial contexts, kings are often central figures, seen arriving in formal attire, such as a plain riding-suit, and participating in official matters like hunting events or royal entertainments with nobles and dignitaries.

In the realm of personal interactions, a king may be portrayed as someone who notices and admires individuals, such as expressing admiration for a princess's eyes and reacting to her behavior, such as attempting to end her restriction on making only three remarks. This depiction emphasizes a more personal or narrative-driven aspect of the king’s character.

Apart from human monarchs, the term "King" also denotes a sacred ancient Chinese musical instrument used in religious and imperial contexts. This instrument, made from sonorous stones or specific materials, holds religious significance and is regarded as sacred within its cultural tradition.

Additionally, "King" is encountered in literary and historical texts, such as Samuel Pepys's diary, where it references the monarchy and courtly society of 17th-century England. Here, the king is associated with societal hierarchy, theater, and courtly entertainment, often present at plays, royal festivities, and societal gatherings involving noble figures and courtiers.

In summary, "King" can refer to a traditional monarch, a political leader actively engaged in governance and societal events, a personal figure observed in narratives, a sacred musical instrument from Chinese culture, or a historical reference from English court life. Each context highlights different facets of the entity, from leadership and authority to cultural and ceremonial significance.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers generally to legendary legendary monarchs involved in Tintagel's myth and history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90130c63183d6c112f655e298f1be97a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abefe66985061e17bec49f1898419196&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-586a7611f02b1deff7ed6b0d6bdd65af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03560b66e85f3d2c8750033a19ea401c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-506f129a6a5da52f9bd4973893d49e50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-203f252ae42306fa9f42171f8e2f0f23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e746cb1e34dcaeb58ddb7736aa554abf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88362a658523273b693a56c70000ab09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-291c8a0f74d4df3d7cf1b446e90edd31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40dae678ded1ef4fdb119e4518ba93d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80d293af5e7df136bc3f73cb98ae3fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f974464e9d58658b4b7ed99efd30cae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c40a925861c89aa50e0fdf703fdaf56f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615518</data>
