      <data key="d0">Hindoos</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">Hindoos shared similar beliefs in reincarnation and spiritual continuity, related to their religious practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Hindoos are an example of a non-European group that has developed historical feelings of resentment and criticism toward European colonizers and their actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d6d693b5da179ac238c1380743ccaf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764608159</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Romans">
      <data key="d0">Romans</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Romans were an influential ancient civilization primarily based in the city of Rome, known for establishing a vast empire that extended across Europe, North Africa, and parts of Spain. They occupied and governed numerous territories, including North Africa, where they referred to some indigenous tribes as Moors or Mauri, and engaged in numerous interactions and conflicts with local peoples and neighboring civilizations, such as the Carthaginians. 

Culturally, the Romans inherited and adapted various traditions from earlier civilizations, notably deriving their notions of tragedy from the Greeks and being influenced by Lydia. They adopted numerous cultural elements, including musical instruments like lyres and the hydraulic organ, from Greece, Egypt, and Western Asia, which played a significant role in their musical culture. Roman funeral rites included offerings and sacrifices, reflecting their religious and ritualistic practices, which were similar to those described in other contexts and cultures.

In social and culinary spheres, the Romans used terms like "pincerna," which referred to roles or entities associated with hospitality or culinary functions within their society. Their influence extended beyond their immediate era, shaping European culture through their musical, military, and social practices. Overall, the Romans are recognized for their military prowess, cultural achievements, and extensive territorial control, which left a lasting legacy influencing subsequent civilizations across Europe and the Mediterranean region.&lt;SEP&gt;The Romans were an ancient civilization whose presence in Cornwall signifies historical influence and early civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc0c02df8cb82b091fb73a066662a32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de69a3d99803079ed2ffea77329d6210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e7feca07c634684d8111b02a41fcae1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a3a1103e1d6efafaf897eb994dbaa44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbd510e5f844be534e59040af85f777&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2a4525b14048f3353d702b98f67ccda</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615485</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scythians">
      <data key="d0">Scythians</data>
      <data key="d1">culture</data>
      <data key="d2">Scythians are referenced as having sacrificed slaves at funerals, according to Herodotus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Statue">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Statue</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A statue representing a deceased person, symbolizing the spirit's continued existence, is a significant artifact in the funeral customs described.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood">
      <data key="d0">Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood is a vital bodily fluid with multiple essential functions in human physiology as well as cultural symbolism. It contains nitrogenous substances crucial for transporting nutrients and supporting metabolic processes, including transporting food particles from the intestines to the heart and distributing oxygen to tissues. Blood circulates throughout the body, carrying waste matter away from cells to be expelled or processed, notably transporting waste products to the lungs where they are purified through respiration. Additionally, blood plays a key role in regulating body temperature by transporting heat generated from internal metabolic activities to different parts of the body.

Beyond its biological functions, blood holds significant symbolic and cultural meanings. It signifies heritage and kinship, emphasizing common descent and the importance of ancestral lineage in forging national consciousness. In ritual contexts, such as funerals, manifestations of blood—such as dried blood surrounding a funeral slab—can indicate sacrifices or rituals performed during burial ceremonies, exemplified by its association with Chaacmol. Furthermore, blood is also depicted metaphorically and poetically, symbolizing human emotions like shame and other intense feelings. Overall, blood serves as both a cornerstone of physiological life and a powerful symbol in cultural and ritual practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f5b2f7d138338b628491cb8bacb7fe79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d6d693b5da179ac238c1380743ccaf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91ae32cc554dc390c60bd5c873778659&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71d99b7f78b8977afbaeb638a1b23512&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ec816dcf44e2fe3a4afba7c6692bba3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-306d5de2eae0665c92137b63fb6d96d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c40e9d9d6bed631bcdf0022bea513804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b1925755a5c65f004018f45749c7912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476f39fcc8c0c7619821c2b1143970d6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612179</data>
      <data key="d6" />
