    <node id="Colors">
      <data key="d0">Colors</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b991502e22e90fb761c5591cdca0b88e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb4eb8f1e4cf05e01dcfb95969a6bb79</data>
      <data key="d2">Maya artists used vegetable colors, ochres, and cinnabar for decorations on sculptures and walls.&lt;SEP&gt;Colors such as green, red, azure, and white represented different factiones of charioteers, with additional colors like gold and purple added later.</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610604</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Temple">
      <data key="d0">Temple</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b991502e22e90fb761c5591cdca0b88e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d338a477bdce675ba495c45901f2f4f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1c80cac977c30ca82e1b9af698fb201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-667660461b3e82515e6fd43c059be5a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fea727469cc8526b5cd257ed6247a09</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicte assisted her brother Cay in temple rituals, and her name appears in inscriptions at Uxmal.&lt;SEP&gt;The temple, with cymbals and lamplight, is a spiritual place that influences the scene's atmosphere.&lt;SEP&gt;The Temple, also called the House of Gold, is a sacred Chinese religious site housing the Golden Dragon idol.&lt;SEP&gt;The Temple is the sacred place where the Golden Dragon idol is kept, protected by guards and guardians.&lt;SEP&gt;The temple is the site where the sacrifice and ritual involving breaking a flute occur during ascent.</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613939</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Goddess">
      <data key="d0">Goddess</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b991502e22e90fb761c5591cdca0b88e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-002cf134516b6f6702e5dfec7413096b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a22b4bcd91dd2d8b9fa5db8f58d88ab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1c921bbb2e03c67dfc84088fb4c60fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa28345d39e77952274cbff7d1c3944e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442f3a60382a33a67c2d1a47639643cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-432add00f9047dd14d52a61f532cf54d</data>
      <data key="d2">Zuhuy-Kak is a goddess of maidens, worshiped after her apotheosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The Goddess of Love (implied as Cupid or similar) is portrayed as a patroness and confidant linked to love and poetic inspiration.&lt;SEP&gt;Goddess refers to divine female figures in mythology, including Atalanta and Diana, associated with qualities like swiftness and prowess.&lt;SEP&gt;The goddess is symbolized by the procession and the sacred offerings.</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mausoleum">
      <data key="d0">Mausoleum</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b991502e22e90fb761c5591cdca0b88e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f65c2b617b2b75df32a484e145b034fe</data>
      <data key="d2">Chaacmol's mausoleum contains sculptures and relics representing his importance and respect among the Mayas.&lt;SEP&gt;Chaacmol's mausoleum had interior apartments, bas-reliefs, and frescoes depicting his life and customs.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764606722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
