    <node id="Serbia">
      <data key="d0">Serbia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Serbia is a country in the Balkans that played a central role during World War I, serving as a focal point of wartime military and medical efforts. It was a nation heavily impacted by conflict, experiencing invasion, retreat, and significant hardships such as famine, epidemics—including typhus in 1915—and widespread suffering among its population. The country was characterized by active battles, strategic passes, and large movements of soldiers and civilians during the war, reflecting its importance on the Balkan front in the broader military campaign.

During this tumultuous period, Serbia also became a significant site for international medical aid and charitable work. Elsie Inglis, a notable activist and medical professional, traveled to Serbia to undertake humanitarian efforts, particularly with the Scottish Women’s Hospitals. She was involved in establishing and supporting hospital services amidst the chaos, including working directly in hospital settings under bombing and shellfire. Her activities underscored Serbia’s role as a recipient of international aid aimed at alleviating wartime suffering. Inglis’s association with Serbia highlights her contribution to its medical services and symbolizes the country's enduring connection to humanitarian efforts, resilience, and hope amidst adversity.

Additionally, Serbia’s historical significance extends beyond wartime to include its national identity and suffering due to epidemics like typhus, which devastated the population in 1915. The country’s landscape, strategic importance, and the resilience of its people—both soldiers and families—are integral to its identity. Overall, Serbia during World War I was a land marked by conflict, suffering, international aid, and the remarkable efforts of individuals like Elsie Inglis, who dedicated themselves to serving its people in their time of greatest need.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b424c5c152bf0f5c8eb62283ff8b04ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64db1dd7d0712bd0b11ae20de546f3e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-255e055413333e0db5092afb8deec482&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32f709c5563ea114d4fd6bb560a0d87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7f3fc74bc960c00595a6361c3449b8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fc5bb98f8d283a62db5e74bbfa9071&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdf24e1910d1ceb68fb8c77bc4b2ee5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4be564c5fa39fff8a20f1369b2fa02d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce963d77a022a25ba3a2d2ad400fa56c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b81ae10df52932fd952fbc03a861bd8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d37436bbfc5c51f8d3f5eadaa7be7704&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2678b978dddcb10f74685afcc33826d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6540245da04893916f916cbd8b74aa3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-615fd00e3494a05f5434700852bd4911&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-563025dcd3d25d4d2626b41bcc0a84e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aa2387fc90852af29634d0f93b3c46b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb2443dd1abc31bd06002ccb7a44c963</data>
