    <node id="Mary">
      <data key="d0">Mary</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The entity "Mary" refers to multiple individuals across various contexts and relationships. One description identifies Mary as a person to whom Elsie writes a letter, in which she describes winter conditions at the hospital, suggesting her role in personal correspondence involved with health or community insights. Another description states that Mary was the name of Theophilus Stickney’s third daughter, linking her to the Stickney family and highlighting her position within their lineage.

Additionally, Mary is described as Angeline's sister, emphasizing familial ties and naming practices that involve sharing the same name among siblings. She is also identified as the daughter of Moses Stickney, who married Theophilus Stickney, and they settled in Jaffrey, New Hampshire, establishing her as part of a specific family and geographical context. Further descriptions depict Mary as a relative who visited Cambridge, provided care for young children, and engaged in family correspondence, suggesting her involvement in caregiving and familial communication.

There are also references to Mary as a sister whose death preceded her own serious illness, indicating a family narrative of loss influencing her life. Other descriptions mention her recent death and imminent burial, portraying her as a recently deceased individual. In a different context, Mary is associated with Miss Mary, a character capable of gasping for breath and fluttering her wings in distress during certain antics, possibly describing a pet or an allegorical figure involved in a story.

Furthermore, Miss Mary is characterized as a story character who expresses gratitude and shares a close bond with a raccoon and other animals, implying a narrative role of companionship and affection. Finally, Mary is also described as one of Madam’s friends who has enjoyed her visit and is part of a group of merry companions, suggesting her presence in social or festive settings.

Overall, "Mary" in these descriptions encapsulates a diverse set of individuals—from family members and personal correspondents to story characters and social companions—spanning different timelines, relationships, and contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Mary is a woman in Cornwall, involved in marketing and related to the house party at Mullion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7643380e1b94787722b1984480288f4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-761dc75dfaa658aca91981a9ec83d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d483764952d7db72ab6b8abd0b9b597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9153b876a7591a7e83884937254c80d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccd32b7ed6773eeb16d88857c35f0af9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63ada5b526daab8bb42bced4bd5ee488&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddd5ecb41e87808c4d5b933250c73c2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385599a16e6adec90c4a4bae922a43d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6778bb1c4c17f23f0cc54a25751bc2f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e99224c8c1fbad67820c6ae2260eff2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678eca288ef7c7d22d1d4c106619b015&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88bb0742ed8d4804fc66811c924845ad</data>
