    <node id="Science">
      <data key="d0">Science</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8e1105db92c26143035157a3a806604b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c60f4b4b14ecad06c16f793619f89752&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9153b876a7591a7e83884937254c80d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8442dd0527e82eb09ee5eeff266010ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3573c74971fb594d0887556481e6476&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d98dca421f9ad7bdbf89c28e679535b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2131d24a8dbb5fbb7841a0c8eb97f9f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f42ca0e751524db1f61362471e59be14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9567484ec2e94de37ab803e0df4f01d</data>
      <data key="d2">Science is the systematic pursuit of knowledge about the natural world and the physical universe. It encompasses the study of fundamental phenomena, such as electricity, and extends to broader philosophical implications, including its influence on morality and social duties. Historical figures like Sir James Crichton-Browne are associated with scientific concepts such as primordial protoplasm, highlighting its role in understanding biological and physical processes. 

Science is also viewed as a human endeavor involving precise methodologies, where personal judgment can be measured relative to standardized benchmarks, emphasizing its empirical nature. Throughout history and culture, science has been regarded as a source of knowledge and enlightenment, inspiring efforts like the regeneration of mankind and promoting human understanding. It is often integrated with religious beliefs without inherent conflict, illustrating its multifaceted influence across different domains.

Furthermore, science underpins philosophical outlooks and contributes to moral understanding, though it can also generate confusion and indecision regarding social responsibilities. Ultimately, science is seen as human handiwork used to counteract natural powers, forming the foundation of modern comprehension of the universe and our place within it.</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
