    <node id="Angeline Stickney">
      <data key="d0">Angeline Stickney</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Angeline Stickney was a multifaceted individual known for her contributions as a teacher, author, and social activist during the mid-19th century. She was dedicated to her education and professional development despite facing financial and societal challenges, demonstrating perseverance, gentleness, and religious sincerity. As an educator and student at McGrawville, she was recognized for her academic achievements, political activism, and commitment to the women's suffrage movement, reflecting her strong dedication to social reform and equality.

In addition to her professional pursuits, Angeline Stickney was an accomplished writer who composed literary works during her college years. Her writings were characterized by sincerity, religious sentimentality, and unwavering loyalty to her alma mater. She also engaged in personal narratives, including courtship and marriage stories, notably wearing the "bloomer" costume—a symbol associated with religious and cultural identity and gender reform during that era.

Personal letters from August 1855 reveal her efforts to seek health, employment, and spiritual growth. Despite experiencing health weaknesses, she was mentally strong and demonstrated resilience. Ultimately, Angeline Stickney married Asaph Hall, with whom she shared her life. Her life story encompasses her roles as an educator, writer, activist, and a woman navigating societal expectations while maintaining her ambitions and principles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ddae83cf32cd2eddf4f6f5e5f7b1de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7b901adf8679c3923b1589b8a4a5c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d00c65bf79fd492a5b18e73d5714807&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59c37387ba8758c27d10bbea3fdf809b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ae8a29bf4f3c8add60aa8ad2364f52c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18fc234e4ccd21f6ffe31638a97054a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bed6738b6d7b4e2dc22c8db0e48f9a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9edfe0fb8fa0058acfbbae0026363f1c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
