    </node>
    <node id="Lyman Hall">
      <data key="d0">Lyman Hall</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lyman Hall was a signer of the Declaration of Independence and Governor of Georgia, descendant of John Hall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c23711e6a6e44b422911f2bdf57475a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="David Hall">
      <data key="d0">David Hall</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">David Hall was an original proprietor of Goshen, Conn., killed in battle near Lake George.&lt;SEP&gt;David Hall is an ancestor whose remains are thought to be in an unknown grave.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c23711e6a6e44b422911f2bdf57475a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9eb39f6855ba70b44490f834cf645715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ethan Allen">
      <data key="d0">Ethan Allen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ethan Allen was an American Revolutionary figure, friend of Asaph Hall’s ancestor, associated with the capture of Ticonderoga.&lt;SEP&gt;Ethan Allen is a historic figure remembered by the narrator during the journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c23711e6a6e44b422911f2bdf57475a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9eb39f6855ba70b44490f834cf645715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wallingford">
      <data key="d0">Wallingford</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Wallingford is a town in Connecticut where John Hall and the ancestors of Asaph Hall originated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c23711e6a6e44b422911f2bdf57475a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Union">
      <data key="d0">Union</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The union referred to as a "glorious union" is described as a collective entity that is actually a collection of robbers and pirates, symbolizing a corrupt or oppressive federation.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to the United States forces fighting during the Civil War, initially reported to have won the first Battle of Bull Run.&lt;SEP&gt;The Union was the primary opposing force during the Civil War, composed of northern states fighting to preserve the United States.&lt;SEP&gt;The Union refers to the United States government and its supporters during the Civil War.&lt;SEP&gt;Union refers to the Northern states' forces in the Civil War, represented by the Second Minnesota regiment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Union is the northern faction in the Civil War, opposed to the Confederacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bf8943ee8dd238feb8fd1d1ea67c1d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b435924602432e6d898c0d4eced2d0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2440d11a9db0e7c5ec428e134dc47e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-253bf03f6503d8e5da2098445ef9b630&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb007a78a7e771d39efbeb580b179f2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611974</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Robbers">
      <data key="d0">Robbers</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Robbers are depicted as individuals who steal liberty, representing unlawful oppressors within the union.&lt;SEP&gt;A band of fierce, black-bearded men armed with various weapons who confront the princess in the forest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-506f129a6a5da52f9bd4973893d49e50</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613424</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pirates">
      <data key="d0">Pirates</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">Pirates are depicted as individuals who commit acts of theft and piracy, symbolizing violent oppressors within the union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbd510e5f844be534e59040af85f777</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Commissioner">
      <data key="d0">Commissioner</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The commissioner is an appointed official responsible for deciding the future of fugitive slaves, with financial incentives influencing decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fugitive">
      <data key="d0">Fugitive</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The fugitive is a person fleeing slavery seeking freedom, whose future is subject to decision by the commissioner.&lt;SEP&gt;The fugitive is a character who is being sought by Masterton, possibly an absconding manager or someone involved in clandestine activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df25ca25a175761aaaa6532ccedf415&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b6e87fa71178c4ad2bb90380a5b1eac</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613002</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Slavery">
      <data key="d0">Slavery</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Slavery is described as an unjust practice involving holding men in bondage and related to the buying and selling of slaves.&lt;SEP&gt;Slavery is portrayed as a system that the union seeks to sustain but is argued to be overturned by dissolution of the union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18fc234e4ccd21f6ffe31638a97054a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Deut. 23, 15 &amp; 16">
      <data key="d0">Deut. 23, 15 &amp; 16</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Biblical passages cited as the law of God opposing the fugitive slave law.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mrs. Emily Judson">
      <data key="d0">Mrs. Emily Judson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mrs. Emily Judson is referenced as a figure in a piece of sentimental writing about her demise.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Earths Angels">
      <data key="d0">Earths Angels</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Earth’s angels represent noble souls dedicated to oppressed humanity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764607643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Poetry">
      <data key="d0">Poetry</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Poetry is discussed as a literary form that originates from the deep, mysterious recesses of the human soul, with enduring materials and origin.&lt;SEP&gt;She possessed a poetic soul and maintained religious feeling and faith throughout her life.&lt;SEP&gt;Poetry is described as artificial language used to express unreal sentiments, with an implication of future evolutions towards true ideas.&lt;SEP&gt;Poetry is both the subject and medium of the text, reflecting on the art, its constraints, and divine inspiration.&lt;SEP&gt;Poetry is a form of artistic expression that is exempt from death and can preserve the fame of poets throughout eternity.&lt;SEP&gt;A piece of poetry about cows and herds, involving quotations and poetic descriptions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9153b876a7591a7e83884937254c80d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b7cbaacbda1b565d7c16cf842522b6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15ba2e01ff70de4541cc6b1d7ee24441&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da34ec44714215c53ff16985a5645248&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f42ca0e751524db1f61362471e59be14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c781927a13f05b8bf3cc68dedd0ed839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94a58ce86fabb001a8e0add34c5408f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f54377361394a10337ee324609f05a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa28345d39e77952274cbff7d1c3944e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b49e205c56dbae8d49f65c119fc9144a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc0c02df8cb82b091fb73a066662a32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-540d8a1b5f98027bff4df2b8e31b99b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3a6fd5e17721f4573f70b6578a7f23a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e879d2a9ee2a96c5c34084b6728bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f7adebfdec41855a49926f0ef0c9df6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb2935b8030ab293e3b353c463c7bc94</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613432</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Astronomy">
      <data key="d0">Astronomy</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Astronomy is shown as a science revealing the wonders of the Creator, emphasizing the grandeur and majesty of the universe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe2e999d42ae24fd88802c534f3fd8d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b7cbaacbda1b565d7c16cf842522b6c</data>
