    <node id="Cupid">
      <data key="d0">Cupid</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Cupid is the Roman god of love, associated with desire, romantic attraction, and affection. He is traditionally depicted as a young boy or winged youth, often with wings of gold, emphasizing his swift and playful nature. Cupid is frequently shown wielding a bow and quiver of arrows, which symbolize the power to inspire love and passion in individuals. These arrows are often depicted as causing desire and romantic longing, highlighting Cupid's role as a vessel of love's whims and unpredictability.

In mythological and artistic representations, Cupid's arms—such as flames and bows—serve as symbols of love and passion, often associated with enduring emotions and fervor. He is portrayed as influencing the feelings of gods and mortals alike, compelling subjects like poets to write about love or tormenting individuals with unrequited affection. His influence extends into poetic and literary contexts, where he is depicted as making whimsical or unpredictable love arrangements, emphasizing the fickle and wavering nature of love. Furthermore, Cupid's influence can be seen as extending through his step-son, Mars, with whom he shares associations of arms and conflict, subtly linking desire with action and aggression.

Overall, Cupid embodies the fleeting, passionate, and sometimes mischievous qualities of love, acting as both a divine figure and a symbol of desire's unpredictable power across mythology, art, and literature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ae8a29bf4f3c8add60aa8ad2364f52c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c781927a13f05b8bf3cc68dedd0ed839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a40ad400e6c46c8451bc02d5c90eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30bc679e676a49c813bf82844d9cb331&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8a704560f9aa2b54ceb8b9a6fd26a53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e60239d7bab05fbce9d289bd08db986&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94a58ce86fabb001a8e0add34c5408f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62d9981c359a23239322d1ef69f6eabb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
