    <node id="The House">
      <data key="d0">The House</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The House is a multifaceted entity encompassing various buildings and locations with distinct characteristics and significance. Primarily, it is described as a two-story brick dwelling that was expanded to three stories in 1876, featuring diverse architectural details, interior furnishings, and a surrounding yard. Often depicted as a white-washed private residence, The House includes features such as a veranda and serves as a home where inhabitants engage in daily routines, such as preparing for bed and enjoying dinner with family members and animals, creating a cozy and intimate environment.

Beyond its primary function as a private home, The House also functions as a symbolic or metaphorical space within different narratives. It can represent safety and refuge, providing security for characters, or serve as a hiding place used for clandestine activities. For example, Raegen hides there to escape law enforcement, highlighting its role as a secure, secretive location with features like narrow stairs and locked doors that evoke confinement and secrecy. The House is situated near significant landmarks, such as a distant light prompting approach, and is often located in remote, guarded areas, as in the residence of Miss Flora, which is described as being in an isolated and protected location. It may also feature notable surrounding elements, such as large gardens and high walls, especially within certain towns, where characters like Eugenia and Bray conduct reconnaissance from its vicinity.

In addition to residential and symbolic functions, The House is involved in strategic or social contexts, acting as a venue for gatherings, dinners, and scheming activities. It can be a setting for planned social events or intimate conversations among visitors, residents, or characters engaged in plotting. Moreover, The House can be associated with property ownership and legal considerations, as in cases where Sir G. Lane discusses renting a property, indicating its role within property law and ownership disputes.

Furthermore, in specific instances, The House is depicted as a darker, gloomy residence, such as the location where Eileen is brought after her abduction, emphasizing its use as a place of captivity or foreboding atmosphere. It is also portrayed as a residence that the narrator maintains, used for various activities and daily upkeep, reflecting its functional significance across different contexts.

Overall, The House is a versatile and layered concept that serves as a private home, a safe haven, a clandestine hideout, a strategic property, and a social hub, with its portrayal adapting to the narrative or contextual needs expressed across various descriptions.&lt;SEP&gt;The house is filled with foreign curiosities and experimental inventions, serving as the residence of the male character who shows guests around.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c65610bff2058547e45414d7690ba36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd506e2b4ab4403262e3cf611653609b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feaab40fd1e6e5870b9322a9a5d595b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b764a3bbaaf870d7b877a3d4dd4fea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e245282ab23d3c2de534d9a0d931dd59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c292374d14be4a74f9ce167fd44fac75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61d5043e124aeb47926ce7d455548eaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e5a1f05827b42a336baccdd4933d843&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-398ac320ee8a237290740b6caa93b301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c46a50cbc624aabf5a4e28b143cd34f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a96c6566abc9d6c8f4e98b738e911a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3612e7c996c6ca62cbb14dfd7b5263&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b24f2b68a542e5127bd458a14221bb2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39786c205992e5e0d95dca55454814f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95bebed720934a7d07e65bb3901bf810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b994957c96ad60e44faa19f24d657e7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2923fc0c238f18810f053d6e390e261d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7588957ea7d271bfdda9ca4a517733ff</data>
