    <node id="Morality">
      <data key="d0">Morality</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Morality is a multifaceted concept primarily concerned with the principles, standards, and principles that govern human behavior, particularly distinguishing between what is considered right and wrong. It encompasses both societal and personal dimensions, serving as a measure of conduct, involving duties individuals owe to one another and the standards of good and evil, praise, and blame. Morality often functions as a normative framework that guides individual actions and societal norms, with debates surrounding its true foundations and the basis for virtue, which can sometimes be misunderstood or misapplied, especially in social and political contexts.

Discussions of morality also address its role in concepts such as moral currency, moral swindlers, and integrity, highlighting its importance in evaluating character and ethical standing. The principles of morality are critically examined, especially in contexts where societal values are degraded or challenged, emphasizing the importance of virtuous conduct. In addition, morality is linked to emotional and behavioral stability, as extreme states like frenzy, madness, and violence are sometimes associated with moral and emotional instability, reflecting the complex relationship between moral feelings and human actions.

Historically, morality has been recognized as a vital aspect of societal cohesion, with particular significance noted in places like France where it is regarded as a crucial societal need. Overall, morality functions as a fundamental ethos guiding both individual morality and collective societal standards, though its application and interpretation can vary, leading to ongoing debates about its true basis and proper expression in various social and political spheres.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-625804364e635f703f6cec4378f9af4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d98dca421f9ad7bdbf89c28e679535b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9481c4e9169907102e1a6a63fadb365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b35cd39246570e8cc51cc541f1b742f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf1500eca47c9a930e310cc4b31eb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e35b84d5e9534312cd2edd202ad1eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66a11e3fdc0678e4dcf84b4d05935cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a861d56558288f1ebda11fb012fb783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2131d24a8dbb5fbb7841a0c8eb97f9f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91102a79f5efa1a15145abb192d5b4a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59e1a5f4174321002296136e39cacfb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c6ddbee998a17d32743b8b0df30530b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Morals">
      <data key="d0">Morals</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Morals are discussed alongside morality as words whose meanings have become diminished or distorted over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Morals are specific actions or conduct that are generally accepted as right or wrong, often used in the context of social standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Morals are described as the social norms and formal standards, often conflated with ethics, but subject to perversion and misinterpretation.&lt;SEP&gt;Morals involve the ethical qualities of the mistress that the speaker finds corrupt or unprincipled.</data>
