    <node id="Church">
      <data key="d0">Church</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-184fcee92f8ae5f8c3132b64ac949ef4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe367bc72a82be3c2cacfc81ba72e719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb71f32b0ab08e17e4d6cb83be9c380c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ce275f5f1e049ce82f2c5c025f7d225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80d293af5e7df136bc3f73cb98ae3fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5836d0924804112ead175776cdcd9ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de865f683f08a0b7587135564fd925d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f749ef842a6b7afcee9121c9350e439&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d4b3e608aee39b08d0a23ee7e08f470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a0cc4adb1d182cba3feb57248b5f290</data>
      <data key="d2">The entity "Church" encompasses a range of roles and representations across different contexts. Historically, the church is depicted as a modest and humble building, described metaphorically as a "shabby barn," reflecting its physical modesty in certain settings. In the context of political and revolutionary movements, the church is represented by bishops and clergy who played a significant role in opposing revolutionary activities, issuing warnings against rebellion, and maintaining social order. It also served as a gathering site for rebels and was involved in episodes of fighting and shooting, where both soldiers and civilians were present, indicating its proximity to conflict zones.

Furthermore, the church substantially influenced the opposition of French-Canadian people to rebellion, highlighting its social and cultural significance in shaping community sentiments. The church is also depicted as an important venue for personal and community events, such as wedding ceremonies, with arrangements made for such occasions within its premises. In addition to its religious functions, multiple mentions refer to attending church services—whether at one's own church or other locations like Clerkenwell Church—serving as places for worship, hymns, prayers, and Sunday morning activities within villages.

Within specific stories and regional settings, the church also functions as a site for special services, such as thanksgiving during harvest festivals, emphasizing its role in community celebration and social cohesion. Overall, "Church" in these descriptions is presented both as a physical place integral to religious life and community gatherings, and as a vital social institution involved in political, cultural, and personal domains across different historical and geographical contexts.</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615495</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
