    </node>
    <node id="Sentimental Journey">
      <data key="d0">Sentimental Journey</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sentimental Journey is a literary work authored by Laurence Sterne that has significantly contributed to his literary reputation and the development of the sentimental movement. Published in London in 1768, just before Sterne’s death, the novel quickly gained popularity among contemporary readers and literary circles. Its themes revolve around innocence, sentimental relationships, and human emotions, often illustrated through episodes involving fidelity, natural scenery, and reflections on superficial travel and human reflection. The work features characters, such as Yorick, and episodes that highlight sensibility and emotional expression, often employing literary devices like papers for wrapping trade articles as motifs.

Sentimental Journey's influence extended beyond England, notably inspiring German literary figures like Herder and Wieland. It played a pivotal role in establishing Sterne’s presence in Germany, where translations and editions, including influential versions published around 1772, sparked enthusiasm for Sterne's style and themes. The novel's popularity in Germany coincided with the broader interest in travel and sentimental literature, prompting imitation and discussion among literary scholars and writers. Its translation, annotations, and critical editions—such as those revised by Benzler—further amplified its reach and impact.

Furthermore, Sentimental Journey is recognized for its stylized narrative, which served as a model for imitating Sterne’s tone and thematic motifs in other works. The novel's episodes involving animal fidelity, sensibility, and reflections on human nature contributed to its role as a foundational text influencing the sentimental movement and travel literature. Notably, it influenced writers such as Schummel and Goethe, with the latter drawing inspiration from Sterne’s approach to emotional expression during his travels. Its influence extended into various European literary adaptations, imitations, and discussions, solidifying its status as a significant work that connected Sterne with broader emotional and literary currents in 18th-century Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5e7f1d14d418ffd32ee04aefb91e7331&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb6830a00197a03b02eae670e7f2e9ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b527db1f11e9eeff8a9a251b1ec766ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31affcd51684d5b249dcc7331131c006&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c3f247ede6e2e4cbb7778c1b56fd58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302bb08717ae1ac7bbe814edbfc98875&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b57f8b57daf11b165b8852ab7e62eb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bed52ff145a82972c1126c22e4c660b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed9ce3f9f758b6eff5d2e71c4d139d78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f11d94df1514c9134e03d19c4744e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3dcaffe826a353c7b1a0eff516a2419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3633c2240cf930228d1d94b3be682bf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c22d20d40b43b3d2c1dbb21d56b7f2c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f84f8d10490e85138ed83f5d3bbf9fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb9f823daea4acf22b2dce0ec0b834ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7eb91ef19d9479c183ea94881f5798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23201d16810e4a370ffadc0b018cb013&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f99047222f973cdfa74a4aaa41bc1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c29ec3cd76c13c7da9bd30bfc253b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-573f9ee936a1b21e04721d34c3cc59f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7be859800282f20899485aabb0c91c80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c128264faa77a054077d4c6bfd355759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c9c0013c86c9a2d0424cec3204472c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9eb63434ade7863dbb529206940c54f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42f3a2f0a754041c29f091e82271385c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce38d5ecc64e3430779a09866429a260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-835b2b68b00b2bd674ad666036ecbc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95bd5a42403a89f556cf4ccd4b82fd6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1ddd79524e91ba84eecbc707c7a2b7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ecb8725d9b49ee2bda6cf5f19b07499</data>
