    <node id="Goethe">
      <data key="d0">Goethe</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe was a prominent and influential German writer, poet, dramatist, and philosopher whose extensive body of work significantly shaped European literature. Celebrated for his genius and literary contributions, Goethe received admiration and acknowledgment from contemporaries and later scholars alike. He was known to have expressed appreciation for authors such as Laurence Sterne, referencing Sterne’s influence on his development as a writer and in his travel literature, as well as engaging with Sterne’s “Koran” and other works, which played a part in the German reception of Sterne’s writings.

Goethe's relationship with Sterne’s oeuvre extended to acceptance and admiration, with evidence suggesting that he considered Sterne's works authentic and influential. He also referenced Sterne’s “Yorick” in his critiques and discussions, highlighting Sterne's impact on literary observation and critique of false ideals. Goethe’s conversations, letters, and critical reviews often include mentions of Sterne, demonstrating a close intellectual connection and reverence.

As a literary critic, Goethe played a crucial role in evaluating contemporary works, such as Schummel’s publications, where his reviews contributed to their recognition. He was involved in discussions surrounding literary authenticity, imitation, and originality, sometimes commenting on satirical or parody materials like Schummel’s “thistle” from Yorick’s grave. Additionally, Goethe commented approvingly on various literary volumes and pairings, such as those of Charis and Johann, indicating his engagement with literary themes and figures.

Furthermore, Goethe engaged in correspondence with prominent figures like Friedrich Schiller, particularly concerning satirical writings such as the Xenien. Towards the end of his life, he explicitly recognized Sterne’s significance within the literary landscape. His influence extended beyond literary critique to broader philosophical and cultural discussions, including his comments on sentimentality, imitation of works like “Werther,” and the importance of observation and critique in developing artistic and literary acumen.

Overall, Johann Wolfgang von Goethe remains a central figure in German literature whose admiration for Sterne and engagement with literary criticism, influence, and reception have left a profound mark on European cultural history. His work and letters reflect an ongoing dialogue with the themes of influence, authenticity, and literary appreciation, underscoring his enduring legacy as a literary giant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-458f12b07f966802c9353e65d5abc548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b931a16c9a56c8f63f44e1fc34f0594&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-073c32eb7cfcec94cf8952d9901d1c9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb3d3d51e372c48bb1c3585adf76c5fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d16d254ebf3b608d2707f3d59145e0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56a19306fc0bdd6d75f01267c814f30c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-096d82a8b687771312b327300d1f1171&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f84f8d10490e85138ed83f5d3bbf9fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58047f97c3f5cb05bd0a35cf0a18f86d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12e58d009015748875e7d97f6b72f688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc64252e1565a88b1d7729211672d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10af28895017a86626bcf11657f1ec8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9c0367c62a6ef1f6fa6379da0c02c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c29ec3cd76c13c7da9bd30bfc253b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc9514ef0c01ef6384fdf0e7549eeccb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-559b00cb6dc756396bfc420ba8cc5cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5c097c91ca9891428d1433e3642e46f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003097ce0b9615e69c58849744d9a337&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea440613a1c400f54c6088d2b80f02cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c128264faa77a054077d4c6bfd355759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58d87bc060205995efa9baf4bddb1a07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9616c39c6806c71b17146575a3dbfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-835b2b68b00b2bd674ad666036ecbc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1ebe193334de57430520a945aa53a53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00bff87303bf89315a4f5f9c478e3b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609009</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lessing">
      <data key="d0">Lessing</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lessing was a prominent German literary figure known for his admiration of Laurence Sterne and his work, particularly "Tristram Shandy." Throughout his career, Lessing expressed a deep devotion to Sterne, acknowledging his influence on German literature and demonstrating ongoing respect for Sterne's genius as a novelist and stylist. As an author, dramatist, scholar, and critic, Lessing engaged with Sterne's works both academically and personally, often discussing Sterne in the context of literary history and criticism.

In addition to his admiration for Sterne, Lessing may have drawn inspiration from Cervantes and possibly influenced or interacted with efforts related to the translation of Sterne's "Shandy," although concrete evidence of his active involvement in such translation projects remains undocumented. Lessing was also in possession of notable literary works such as Burton’s "Anatomy of Melancholy," from which Sterne might have borrowed wisdom, indicating an indirect influence on Sterne's character and ideas.

His role extended beyond mere admiration; Lessing was an influential scholar and critic whose assistance is recognized in literary translations, notably Bode’s translation efforts related to Sterne’s works. He engaged in literary conversations, such as with Sara Meyer, and is mentioned in connection with German literary history, emphasizing his significance in this domain. While Lessing regarded Sterne fondly, he was not significantly influenced by Sterne’s style in his later works but maintained a respectful regard for Sterne's literary genius.

References to Lessing appear in various historical texts, including a citation from 1885, and within discussions of literary influence and history concerning Sterne. Although he was not listed as a subscriber to certain publications, his connection to Sterne's circle and literary legacy remains noteworthy. Overall, Lessing’s legacy is marked by his admiration of Sterne, his critical insights, and his role in fostering German engagement with Sterne’s works and the broader Romantic literary tradition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-458f12b07f966802c9353e65d5abc548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c3f247ede6e2e4cbb7778c1b56fd58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb3d3d51e372c48bb1c3585adf76c5fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3dcaffe826a353c7b1a0eff516a2419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb9f823daea4acf22b2dce0ec0b834ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84307003973059440e3b98bafcdbef48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc64252e1565a88b1d7729211672d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c29ec3cd76c13c7da9bd30bfc253b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc9514ef0c01ef6384fdf0e7549eeccb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1ddd79524e91ba84eecbc707c7a2b7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d15e55fa6a03a4ae9a73fae85bc97ec2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4a45157e34604d157e56225b4b8622b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9616c39c6806c71b17146575a3dbfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9eb63434ade7863dbb529206940c54f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a362f2b4122dbc620cf476357f1ece</data>
