    <node id="Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek">
      <data key="d0">Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek was a prominent German literary review magazine established in 1765, operating in Berlin and Stettin until 1792. It played a significant role in shaping literary discourse by publishing reviews, critiques, and commentary on prominent authors and translations of the period. The publication is notably recognized for its positive assessments of Laurence Sterne’s works, praising his deviation from conventional homiletic discourse and highlighting his ability to reveal the good and beautiful in ordinary themes. It reviewed collections of Sterne’s letters and translations, emphasizing the influence of Sterne’s sentimental style and praising his literary contributions.

Furthermore, the Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek engaged in detailed critical discussions about translations of Sterne’s work, including evaluations of Bode’s and Mittelstedt’s interpretations, displaying its commitment to literary scholarship and interpretation. The periodical also discussed broader themes of sentimentalism and meanderings inspired by Sterne’s influence. Additionally, it recognized the importance of contemporaneous satire, notably praising Timme’s satire and calling him "Our German Cervantes," thereby acknowledging the broader satirical and literary landscape of the time.

Overall, the Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek was a vital publication dedicated to literary review and critique, with a particular focus on Sterne and related German translation efforts, contributing significantly to German literary culture in the late 18th century.</data>
