    <node id="Wieland">
      <data key="d0">Wieland</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Wieland was a prominent German writer, poet, and critic whose work significantly contributed to the dissemination and appreciation of British literature in Germany. Influenced by English authors such as Richardson, Fielding, and Sterne, Wieland played a vital role in fostering literary connections between Britain and Germany. His admiration for Sterne was particularly notable; he was an early enthusiast of Sterne's "Sentimental Journey" and considered translating Shandy, actively encouraging others like Bode to do so. Wieland's engagement with Sterne’s ideas extended to adopting stylistic features and analyzing Sterne’s influence in his own writings, although he was not merely an imitator. 

He was involved in translating and interpreting Shakespeare’s works, with his translations influencing contemporary figures such as Goethe. Wieland's literary opinions, often liberal and progressive, appeared in various writings and letters, reflecting the emotional currents of the period. He was also associated with the publication _Teutscher Merkur_, where he contributed and cited Sterne and Yorick, the latter being a character associated with Sterne's works. Wieland's admiration for Sterne was sometimes expressed critically, as in satirical works referencing Yorick and sentimental themes, illustrating the complex relationship he had with Sterne's literary ideas.

Additionally, Wieland's name was sometimes falsely used to endorse editions, such as an unauthorized use related to Zückert’s translation, which he publicly repudiated. He was a subscriber to Bode's translation of Sterne’s "Shandy," emphasizing his support for bringing Sterne’s works into the German cultural sphere. Historically, Wieland was connected with various literary endeavors, including editing editions of Unterhaltungen and engaging with the literary debates of his time. His influence extended to scholars studying Sterne’s impact on German literature, and his own letters reveal the emotional and intellectual currents of the Enlightenment period. Overall, Wieland remains a key figure in bridging British and German literary traditions, renowned for his admiration of Sterne and his contributions to German literary history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b527db1f11e9eeff8a9a251b1ec766ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302bb08717ae1ac7bbe814edbfc98875&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed9ce3f9f758b6eff5d2e71c4d139d78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d16d254ebf3b608d2707f3d59145e0ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef7a5904617eb5f54e94c5ea84fd0b1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f84f8d10490e85138ed83f5d3bbf9fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58047f97c3f5cb05bd0a35cf0a18f86d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12e58d009015748875e7d97f6b72f688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c29ec3cd76c13c7da9bd30bfc253b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5c550c793c251f99502adc3abd77fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ccff1ea6ecc4a7b158250715bcedc0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc9514ef0c01ef6384fdf0e7549eeccb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f77f5d39cb0cceb82bc5a1d37faf560f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-559b00cb6dc756396bfc420ba8cc5cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5306de19c5ab05feb0d4d09c44ca2fc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c128264faa77a054077d4c6bfd355759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c9c0013c86c9a2d0424cec3204472c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1ddd79524e91ba84eecbc707c7a2b7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d15e55fa6a03a4ae9a73fae85bc97ec2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9616c39c6806c71b17146575a3dbfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bba52ca6db0e619b578b5cf52494e654&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00bff87303bf89315a4f5f9c478e3b5</data>
