    <node id="Böttiger">
      <data key="d0">Böttiger</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">C. A. Böttiger was a prominent scholar and critic known for his insightful commentary on literary works and their reception. He suggested that Laurence Sterne may have influenced Gotthold Ephraim Lessing's development of the term "empfindsam," highlighting Böttiger's interest in the interconnections between key literary figures and their contributions. Böttiger also analyzed various translations and editions of Sterne’s writings, noting their impact and the different receptions they garnered over time.

Furthermore, Böttiger emphasized Lessing’s role in the literary landscape and made specific statements regarding Sterne’s influence on later writers. He reported on Lessing’s possession of Burton’s “Anatomy of Melancholy,” indicating Böttiger’s engagement with Sterne-related literary influences and actions. Additionally, Böttiger commented on the work of other scholars, such as Schink, sometimes portraying Schink’s work as a variation or continuation of Stevenson’s contributions, thereby situating Böttiger as a critic attentive to the evolution and reinterpretation of literary texts and authors.

He also recognized the significance of sentimental travelers inspired by Sterne, praising Bode’s translation for demonstrating their excellence and the power of Sterne’s influence. Overall, Böttiger’s extensive references and analyses reflect his importance as a scholar dedicated to understanding and interpreting the nuances of literary history, influence, and translation within the context of Sterne, Lessing, and related authors.</data>
