    <node id="Herder">
      <data key="d0">Herder</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Johann Gott Herder was a prominent German philosopher, literary critic, and scholar active during the late 18th century. He extensively engaged with German literature, aesthetics, and cultural development, offering critical opinions on humor, aesthetic values, and the nature of imitation in literature. Herder was particularly known for his critique of slavish copying, emphasizing the importance of originality while also reevaluating the role of imitation as a positive influence in cultural evolution.

Herder’s scholarly work included detailed discussions on the German literary scene, especially in relation to the reception and translation of foreign works. He examined Wieland’s efforts in translating Laurence Sterne’s and other authors’ works, demonstrating his interest in the cross-cultural exchange of literary ideas. Herder valued Sterne and Swift highly, expressing admiration for their contributions to literature, and he actively participated in the German reception of Sterne’s works, subscribing to translations and commenting on their significance.

A key aspect of Herder’s influence was his role in guiding and shaping literary figures such as Goethe. He was instrumental in encouraging Goethe’s early engagement with Sterne during Goethe’s time in Strasbourg, thus impacting the development of German literary appreciation. Herder maintained correspondence with Goethe, with notable letters exchanged between 1771 and 1773, reflecting his intellectual engagement and influence within the broader literary community.

Throughout his career, Herder was recognized as a dedicated critic and scholar, often cited in various texts, and associated with multiple pages of scholarly references. His work contributed significantly to the understanding of literary imitation, originality, and the aesthetic values associated with German and European literature of his era. Herder’s enduring influence is evident in his writings, correspondence, and his role in shaping the German literary landscape during the Enlightenment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58047f97c3f5cb05bd0a35cf0a18f86d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc64252e1565a88b1d7729211672d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3e15f8dcb69742a9caa28ee76e24abe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f99047222f973cdfa74a4aaa41bc1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c29ec3cd76c13c7da9bd30bfc253b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf5c550c793c251f99502adc3abd77fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc9514ef0c01ef6384fdf0e7549eeccb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3445af39103910094e6f39b4e9d594d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003097ce0b9615e69c58849744d9a337&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c128264faa77a054077d4c6bfd355759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c9c0013c86c9a2d0424cec3204472c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba9616c39c6806c71b17146575a3dbfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9eb63434ade7863dbb529206940c54f</data>
