    <node id="Mondelet">
      <data key="d0">Mondelet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mondelet was a member of the Executive Council, associated with political reforms and implicated in governance considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609472</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lord Aylmer">
      <data key="d0">Lord Aylmer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lord Aylmer was a governor accused by the Patriote press of actions akin to slaughter during a riot and cholera epidemic, and of inviting sick immigrants to decimate French Canadians.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Aylmer was the governor of Canada appointed in 1830, involved in revenue control negotiations.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Aylmer was a governor involved in the British response to the Ninety-Two Resolutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Aylmer was a governor of Canada whose relations with the Assembly made him less effective in resolving political issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da749e17a56c35554061ac541d67d08c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609472</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lord Goderich">
      <data key="d0">Lord Goderich</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lord Goderich was involved in financial proposals impacting the governance and political debates in Lower Canada around 1830.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Goderich was at the Colonial Office, instructed Lord Aylmer, and was involved in proposals regarding revenue control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da749e17a56c35554061ac541d67d08c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609472</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="F. A. Quesnel">
      <data key="d0">F. A. Quesnel</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">F. A. Quesnel was a moderate member of the Patriote party who introduced a bill to secure the independence of judges and was threatened by Papineau.&lt;SEP&gt;F. A. Quesnel is connected to Papineau in the historical context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609472</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Assembly">
      <data key="d0">Assembly</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The assembly in 1805 debated building prisons, which led to political conflict and racial tensions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Assembly was the legislative body involved in political disputes, expulsions, and manipulations, reflecting the tensions in Lower Canada's governance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Assembly was an elected legislative body that the Patriot party aimed to make more influential by subordinating the Executive and reforming the Legislative Council.&lt;SEP&gt;The Assembly refers to the legislative assembly in Upper Canada involved in debating supply and reforms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609472</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Legislative Council">
      <data key="d0">Legislative Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Legislative Council was the upper chamber in Lower Canada that often threw out measures passed by the Assembly and was mainly composed of English-born members.&lt;SEP&gt;The Legislative Council was the nominated upper house in Lower Canada's government, under control of the executive, and a focus of reform efforts to make it elective.&lt;SEP&gt;The Legislative Council was a nominated and reactionary body serving as a buffer between the governor's Executive Council and the Assembly, with plans for becoming an elective body.&lt;SEP&gt;The Legislative Council was a legislative body that rejected the bill of supply and was involved in the political conflicts in Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The Legislative Council was involved in the colonial governance structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609472</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Patriote">
      <data key="d0">Patriote</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Patriote party was a political organization active during the rebellion in Lower Canada, advocating for responsible government and representing French-Canadian interests.&lt;SEP&gt;The Patriote was a political movement advocating for reform and constitutional change in Lower Canada, becoming more revolutionary over time.&lt;SEP&gt;The Patriote party was a revolutionary political group advocating for the independence of Canada from British rule, emphasizing proud designs toward emancipation and republican government.&lt;SEP&gt;The Patriote movement was involved in the rebellion, with leaders like Thomas Storrow Brown and others fleeing or absent.&lt;SEP&gt;The _Patriote_ is a group of rebels involved in the rebellion, with many being killed or wounded, and their leaders fleeing or being captured.&lt;SEP&gt;The Patriote was a political movement active in 1837 in Lower Canada, a predecessor to the Parti Rouge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe367bc72a82be3c2cacfc81ba72e719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ea9058e9ed8c78aad18db9d953d662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Union Bill of 1822">
      <data key="d0">Union Bill of 1822</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Union Bill of 1822 was an abortive legislative attempt seen as an infringement on liberties by the Patriotes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="British government">
      <data key="d0">British government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The British government pursued a policy of conciliatory measures to maintain loyalty among French Canadians and their leaders during the late 18th and early 19th centuries.&lt;SEP&gt;The British government governed Lower Canada and was perceived as unresponsive to local grievances and reform efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;The British government attempted to settle financial difficulties in Canada in 1830.&lt;SEP&gt;The British government was the authority to which the Ninety-Two Resolutions were forwarded and involved in colonial policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The British government is the overarching authority issuing instructions through the Colonial Office and controlling colonial policies.&lt;SEP&gt;The British government is the governing authority whose unpopularity in Canada influenced the political context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da749e17a56c35554061ac541d67d08c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26bd7dcdcc38c1ce5338b530f0ab8694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian blood">
      <data key="d0">Canadian blood</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The phrase 'Canadian blood' was used by the _La Minerve_ press to describe the casualties during the riot.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian">
      <data key="d0">Canadian</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Canada is the country involved in the historical context of the rebellion and colonization.&lt;SEP&gt;Canadian refers to French Canadians or the broader colonial population involved in political disputes.&lt;SEP&gt;The term 'Canadian' refers to the national identity of the French Canadians involved in the riots and political conflicts.&lt;SEP&gt;The Canadians mentioned are involved in the rebellion, with one, Chenier, being shot down and attempting to escape from a window.&lt;SEP&gt;Canadian refers to the insurgents and rebels involved in the conflict, many of whom fought or were affected by the rebellion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6edfc8c6c5b4bb45e9a1b7ed41347d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fusillade">
      <data key="d0">Fusillade</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A fusillade of stones was thrown during the 1832 riot in Montreal, leading to troops firing on rioters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609475</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Massacre">
      <data key="d0">Massacre</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The term 'massacre' was used by the _La Minerve_ press to describe the violence during the riot.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b1afd400ed63eed6be44317b9ae3f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5b84432ad1a7600b31aab3ee4243129</data>
