    <node id="Gosford">
      <data key="d0">Gosford</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Gosford is a colonial governor who delivered a speech at the legislature in Quebec in 1835, advocating for reforms and attempting reconciliation between French and English inhabitants.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Gosford was a governor of the colony who attempted a conciliatory policy but faced opposition and ultimately resigned.&lt;SEP&gt;Gosford was a government official who opposed the formation of a loyalist rifle corps in Montreal, concerned it may worsen the situation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gosford was a key figure who remained in Canada during the rebellion until February.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Gosford served as governor of Canada, overseeing colonial affairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe367bc72a82be3c2cacfc81ba72e719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb71f32b0ab08e17e4d6cb83be9c380c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609476</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lord Gosford">
      <data key="d0">Lord Gosford</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lord Gosford was an Irish peer appointed as governor of Canada and chief royal commissioner to investigate and resolve disputes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lord Gosford was the governor of Quebec in 1835, who made efforts to address grievances and promote reconciliation between French and English populations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dac7fb6c5e874c886dbb8df30d7d6fd5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609476</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir Francis Bond Head">
      <data key="d0">Sir Francis Bond Head</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir Francis Bond Head was the lieutenant-governor of Upper Canada whose instructions caused conflict with Lord Gosford's policies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609476</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colonial Office">
      <data key="d0">Colonial Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Colonial Office was responsible for overseeing colonies like Canada and experienced multiple successive ministers during 1834-1835.&lt;SEP&gt;The Colonial Office was responsible for issuing instructions to colonial governors like Lord Gosford and Sir Francis Bond Head regarding governance policies in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609476</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quebec">
      <data key="d0">Quebec</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Quebec is a province in Canada where traces of Scottish-origin names and French-speaking habitants are found.&lt;SEP&gt;Quebec is a region involved in military defense in 1775 and later political events in Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;Quebec is the Canadian province where the legislative session discussed in the text took place.&lt;SEP&gt;Quebec is a Canadian province where Loyalist meetings were held to oppose Patriote meetings and influence public sentiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Quebec is the broader region where the rebellion in Lower Canada took place, with specific events happening in cities like Montreal and St Hyacinthe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe367bc72a82be3c2cacfc81ba72e719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353f63fa013a00ee4a62ffebc8c022a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609477</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Canada">
      <data key="d0">Upper Canada</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A Canadian province, mainly English-speaking, created in 1791, with equal political rights as Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;Upper Canada was a predominantly English province created in 1791, granted equal political rights with Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;Upper Canada is a historical region involved in the governance dispute with Lower Canada, as mentioned in the context of Sir Francis Bond Head's instructions.&lt;SEP&gt;Upper Canada was another region affected by the rebellions, contrasted with Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;Upper Canada was a British colony, later part of the union with Lower Canada, discussed in the context of political union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353f63fa013a00ee4a62ffebc8c022a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26bd7dcdcc38c1ce5338b530f0ab8694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609477</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Legislature">
      <data key="d0">Legislature</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The legislature refers to the legislative assemblies of Quebec and Upper Canada meeting to debate governance issues.&lt;SEP&gt;The legislature was convened to devise means to combat the outbreak and protect settlers.&lt;SEP&gt;The law-making body that in 1858 mandated ringing church bells during fire alarms.&lt;SEP&gt;The legislature was in session during the fire, and many members were among the hotel guests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd305fbce6d35b0ef2d81f49f11311ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5b84432ad1a7600b31aab3ee4243129</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609996</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reconciliation">
      <data key="d0">Reconciliation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reconciliation refers to the efforts to reconcile the French and English races in Canada, as promoted by Lord Gosford.&lt;SEP&gt;Reconciliation was a diplomatic goal sought by some colonial officials to resolve unrest peacefully.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f63400d8188502b67eda00c561659eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
