    <node id="Sir James Craig">
      <data key="d0">Sir James Craig</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir James Craig was a governor of Canada, whose regime marked the beginning of racial tensions and oppression against French Canadians.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir James Craig was a colonial administrator who declined to forward a resolution to the British Parliament in 1810, affecting the power dynamics in Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir James Craig was a governor whose actions during the repression of _Le Canadien_ and his policy decisions impacted racial relations and political stability in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir James Craig was a governor whose policies and actions influenced the political tensions and racial relations in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609487</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir John Coape Sherbrooke">
      <data key="d0">Sir John Coape Sherbrooke</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir John Coape Sherbrooke was a British administrator who supported conciliation, included Catholics in the Executive Council, and allowed Lower Canadian legislature to vote the entire revenue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609487</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="St Charles">
      <data key="d0">St Charles</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">St Charles is a significant village involved extensively in the conflicts that occurred in Canada, particularly during revolutionary and insurgent struggles. It served as a critical site where rebel forces gathered, prepared defenses, and held meetings, including those of Patriote leaders advocating for revolutionary causes. The village was a focal point for skirmishes and battles between the rebels and British forces, with efforts by the British military to disperse or defeat the insurgents, who had fortified their positions there.

Notably, St Charles was a strategic location targeted by military expeditions, including those led by Wetherall, and was associated with acts of bravery such as those demonstrated by Major Gugy, highlighting its importance in the military campaigns of the period. The site also functioned as a rallying point for rebel defenses and was the scene of significant fighting, culminating in the defeat of the rebellion, which was ultimately confirmed at this location. Additionally, Saint Charles was involved in political resistance, with figures like Papineau active in repudiating revolutionary efforts, indicating its role as a hub of both conflict and political activity during this turbulent time in Canadian history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353f63fa013a00ee4a62ffebc8c022a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f60868fc120dda4e8b845c8a6db7921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609487</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wolfred Nelson">
      <data key="d0">Wolfred Nelson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Wolfred Nelson was a prominent Canadian figure significantly associated with the Lower Canadian Rebellion of 1837-1838. As a key leader of the Patriote movement, Nelson was actively involved in organizing, inspiring, and supporting armed resistance against British rule in Lower Canada (present-day Quebec). He was a supporter of Louis-Joseph Papineau, a central figure in the patriotic struggle, and played an integral role in Patriot meetings, including organizing the gathering at St Ours. Nelson advocated for rebellion and encouraged patriotic actions, including advising Papineau to leave dangerous areas for safety.

During the uprising, Wolfred Nelson commanded rebel forces at St Denis, leading efforts to resist British authority. His leadership extended to direct participation in fighting and strategic planning. Unfortunately, Nelson was not fortunate in escaping the conflict; he was captured near the frontier and subsequently imprisoned for his involvement. Despite his imprisonment, Nelson remained a symbol of patriotism and resistance for the movement.

Beyond the rebellion, Nelson's political and public service contributions continued after his release. He returned to Montreal in 1842, became a member of parliament, and was later appointed inspector of prisons. He was also a brother of Robert Nelson, another notable figure in the Canadian independence movement. Nelson is recognized as a signatory of the declaration proclaiming Canadian independence, underscoring his lasting impact on Canada’s political history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-45d67b8f87397b56d105fc32a0b1f677&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6059f7b6685e9fbba6db55d93c65ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0099aaf0d6a5d5b602247be491e3fb1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe367bc72a82be3c2cacfc81ba72e719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb71f32b0ab08e17e4d6cb83be9c380c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f60868fc120dda4e8b845c8a6db7921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dac7fb6c5e874c886dbb8df30d7d6fd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6edfc8c6c5b4bb45e9a1b7ed41347d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26bd7dcdcc38c1ce5338b530f0ab8694</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609487</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lower-Canadian legislature">
      <data key="d0">Lower-Canadian legislature</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Lower-Canadian legislature was the elected body in Lower Canada that sought control over government revenue and challenged the authority of the Executive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609487</data>
