    </node>
    <node id="Union Bill">
      <data key="d0">Union Bill</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Union Bill was a proposed legislation in Westminster aimed at uniting Upper and Lower Canada, perceived as a threat to French-Canadian identity and autonomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609488</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="John Neilson">
      <data key="d0">John Neilson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">John Neilson was a Scottish editor and agent sent to London to oppose the Union Bill on behalf of French Canadians.&lt;SEP&gt;John Neilson was a Scottish editor of the Quebec Gazette, involved in opposing the bill and leading a deputation to England.&lt;SEP&gt;John Neilson supported Papineau initially but later opposed his actions, notably objecting to the expulsion of Mondelet and opposing resolutions for abolishing the Legislative Council.&lt;SEP&gt;John Neilson supported the Patriote cause and later distanced himself.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da749e17a56c35554061ac541d67d08c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609488</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir John Colborne">
      <data key="d0">Sir John Colborne</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir John Colborne was a prominent British military officer and administrator involved in various operations during periods of unrest in North America, particularly in Lower Canada. As a British military commander, he played a key role in planning and executing efforts to suppress rebellions, including the rebellion led by the Patriotes. He commanded substantial forces, often numbering over two thousand troops, to disperse revolting rebels and restore order.

Colborne was responsible for documenting the state of unrest, reporting on agitators and rebellious activities, and issuing strategic orders during the conflicts. His military leadership included advancing from Laprairie toward Napierville with a strong force and bringing reinforcements into Napierville following rebel retreats. He was actively involved in military operations that sometimes involved threats of extreme measures, such as threatening to burn the village of St Benoit, an action for which he was later censured due to the destruction caused.

In addition to his military duties, Colborne served as a governmental authority in Lower Canada during the rebellion. His administration included actions such as suspending judges and convening courts-martial for prisoners, reflecting his role in maintaining authority and order amidst chaos. Despite his leadership, he appeared hesitant to take immediate aggressive action against the Patriotes until other conflicts were resolved, indicating a cautious approach to military intervention.

Overall, Sir John Colborne’s legacy encompasses his multifaceted role as both a military commander actively engaged in suppressing rebellion and an administrative figure governing during a turbulent period in Canadian history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6059f7b6685e9fbba6db55d93c65ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6e90edbdd9d437566164aceb39d9b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb71f32b0ab08e17e4d6cb83be9c380c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349529243dbc83f8fcf9f0443bec18f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f60868fc120dda4e8b845c8a6db7921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ea9058e9ed8c78aad18db9d953d662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609488</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chateau Clique">
      <data key="d0">Chateau Clique</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Chateau Clique was a group of English-descended elites who held significant political power in Lower Canada and often opposed reforms proposed by French-Canadian politicians.&lt;SEP&gt;The 'Chateau Clique' was a powerful political group involved in conflicts with the Patriotes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Chateau Clique was a political group opposing the Patriotes and involved in governing Lower Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The Chateau Clique is a political group opposing the Patriotes and involved in regional conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609488</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imperial Exchequer">
      <data key="d0">Imperial Exchequer</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Imperial Exchequer was the treasury of the British government responsible for funding colonial expenditures, including those of Lower Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba0f247b33f556371c343cbd158638c2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609488</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Militia">
      <data key="d0">Militia</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The militia consists of military force members who were dismissed by an authority in the context of political conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;Militia refers to armed civilians or paramilitary forces involved in attacking and skirmishing with rebels.&lt;SEP&gt;The militia was called into service to protect settlements and respond to Sioux outbreaks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49dd4de896cb4d1aa7c0c3db3861fcd1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610021</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Odelltown">
      <data key="d0">Odelltown</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Odelltown is a village north of the border where Nelson and his men retreated during the conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;Odelltown is a location defended by Lieut.-Colonel Taylor against rebels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609489</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nelson">
      <data key="d0">Nelson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nelson is a military leader who commanded about a thousand men and led the retreat to Odelltown.&lt;SEP&gt;Nelson was one of the Generals of Buell's army, involved in strategic military operations during the battle.&lt;SEP&gt;Nelson is a historical figure whose opinion is cited regarding Morocco's political importance amidst European maritime conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45d67b8f87397b56d105fc32a0b1f677&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02b249095455416ac52a060862c213f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1b63bc45a96581a48ba223c8d48c07</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614591</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Richelieu">
      <data key="d0">Richelieu</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Richelieu district is a revolutionary area where Papineau and his lieutenants evaded arrest.&lt;SEP&gt;The Richelieu River is a geographical feature near Napierville and the site of a rebel retreat and subsequent loyalist attack.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f60868fc120dda4e8b845c8a6db7921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6059f7b6685e9fbba6db55d93c65ee</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609489</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir James Macdonell">
      <data key="d0">Sir James Macdonell</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir James Macdonell was a British officer involved in commanding forces and overseeing the burning of rebel and loyalist houses during the conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir James Macdonell was Colborne's second-in-command during the conflicts in Lower Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609489</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Royal Engineers">
      <data key="d0">Royal Engineers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Royal Engineers supported the British forces in operations including burning rebel-held villages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609489</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Glengarry Volunteers">
      <data key="d0">Glengarry Volunteers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Glengarry Volunteers were a militia unit supporting British operations against rebels, supporting at Beauharnois.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609489</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Beauharnois">
      <data key="d0">Beauharnois</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Beauharnois was a site of rebel assembly and subsequent military engagement where the village was partially burned.&lt;SEP&gt;Beauharnois was associated with defeats at Beauharnois, possibly a location or a person involved in conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609489</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Elzear Bédard">
      <data key="d0">Elzear Bédard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Elzear Bédard was a French-Canadian judge who attempted to challenge the court proceedings against rebel leaders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609490</data>
