    <node id="Canadian Rebels">
      <data key="d0">Canadian Rebels</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Canadian Rebels are insurgents involved in the 1838 rebellion, facing defeat and dispersal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Court-martial">
      <data key="d0">Court-martial</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The court-martial was a military tribunal established by Colborne to try rebel leaders after the rebellion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74232d6a8ea1ca9bbf02c4e6c8ef3d90</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wetherall">
      <data key="d0">Wetherall</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Wetherall is a British officer leading troops in the campaign against rebels.&lt;SEP&gt;Lieutenant-Colonel Wetherall led several companies of infantry, a troop of volunteer cavalry, and two field-guns, marching toward St Charles during the rebellion.&lt;SEP&gt;Lieutenant-Colonel Wetherall was advancing with troops toward St Charles, with his movements influenced by Gugy's advice and information.&lt;SEP&gt;Wetherall is described as a respectable old habitant who sent a message to the rebels, offering safe passage if they dispersed quietly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f60868fc120dda4e8b845c8a6db7921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brown">
      <data key="d0">Brown</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thomas Storrow Brown was appointed as commander of the rebel forces at St Charles, despite lacking military experience and suffering from injuries.&lt;SEP&gt;Brown is a military officer who initially assumed confidence, received a message from Wetherall, and later decided to retreat from engagement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="St Denis">
      <data key="d0">St Denis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Village where French-Canadian rebels tried to resist British forces.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is a strategic village involved in the rebel attack and later the scene of British retribution.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is a village defended by rebels resisting British military forces.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis was a location where the check administered to Colonel Gore's column took place during the rebellion.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is a location where Brown arrived after retreating from combat and where the engagement lasted less than an hour.&lt;SEP&gt;St Denis is a site of conflict where Charles Ovide Perrault was killed and where fighting and destruction occurred.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0099aaf0d6a5d5b602247be491e3fb1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353f63fa013a00ee4a62ffebc8c022a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f60868fc120dda4e8b845c8a6db7921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="O'Callaghan">
      <data key="d0">O'Callaghan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">O'Callaghan is a Patriote leader who also escaped across the Vermont border following the defeat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0099aaf0d6a5d5b602247be491e3fb1e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colonel Gore">
      <data key="d0">Colonel Gore</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonel Gore is the commander of the British troops involved in the engagement at the village.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonel Gore commanded a column at St Denis during the rebellion, where a check was administered that was considered the only victory of the rebel forces.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonel Gore is responsible for entering St Denis on December 1 and recovering artillery and wounded, later involved in acts of vengeance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0099aaf0d6a5d5b602247be491e3fb1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Moore's Corners">
      <data key="d0">Moore's Corners</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Moore's Corners near the Vermont border is a place where Patriote refugees gathered after the collapse at St Charles.&lt;SEP&gt;Moore's Corners is a place where insurgents encountered the Missisquoi Volunteers during the confrontation.&lt;SEP&gt;Moore's Corners is a place where Robert Bouchette sustained a serious wound.&lt;SEP&gt;Moore's Corners was a site of rebel defeat.&lt;SEP&gt;Moore's Corners is a geographical location associated with Edouard Rodier's activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349529243dbc83f8fcf9f0443bec18f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ea9058e9ed8c78aad18db9d953d662</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dr Cyrile Cote">
      <data key="d0">Dr Cyrile Cote</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dr Cyrile Cote is a Patriote refugee and member of the Lower Canada Assembly at Moore's Corners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Edouard Rodier">
      <data key="d0">Edouard Rodier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Edouard Rodier is a Patriote refugee and member of the Lower Canada Assembly at Moore's Corners.&lt;SEP&gt;Edouard Rodier is associated with events at Moore's Corners and other locations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ludger Duvernay">
      <data key="d0">Ludger Duvernay</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ludger Duvernay is a member of the Assembly and editor of _La Minerve_, among the Patriote refugees at Moore's Corners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dr Kimber">
      <data key="d0">Dr Kimber</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dr Kimber is a Patriote involved in the rescue of Demaray and Davignon, present at Moore's Corners.&lt;SEP&gt;Dr Kimber was involved at Moore's Corners during the conflict.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Robert Shore Milnes Bouchette">
      <data key="d0">Robert Shore Milnes Bouchette</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Robert Shore Milnes Bouchette is a descendant of a loyal and loyalist family, involved in the Patriote invasion attempt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir Guy Carleton">
      <data key="d0">Sir Guy Carleton</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir Guy Carleton was a British military and administrative leader instrumental during the American Revolutionary period, whose escape was facilitated by Bouchette's grandfather in 1775.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vermont">
      <data key="d0">Vermont</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Vermont is the border region where some Patriote leaders and refugees escaped or were captured during the rebellion.&lt;SEP&gt;Vermont is known for producing variety and high-quality marble, including black and brecciated types.&lt;SEP&gt;Vermont produced slate starting from 1852.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8daf6c6c70a3c63c2e7f57dbc969f7c7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Swanton">
      <data key="d0">Swanton</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Swanton is a geographical location from which enthusiastic ladies crossed the Canadian border with a military-style group.&lt;SEP&gt;Swanton is a small town on the American side of the boundary-line where Patriote refugees gathered after the collapse at St Charles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-92528df8108a5a90a2c2b6e9d79fa6a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349529243dbc83f8fcf9f0443bec18f2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="United States of America">
      <data key="d0">United States of America</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The United States of America is a country with a growing population, expected to surpass Great Britain within twenty years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Great Britain">
      <data key="d0">Great Britain</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Great Britain is a country whose institutions have been conferred on the two Canadas and whose population is being surpassed by the USA.&lt;SEP&gt;Great Britain was the overseeing sovereign authority that made decisions regarding the colony's governance.&lt;SEP&gt;Great Britain played a role in colonial policies and relations with French Canadians.&lt;SEP&gt;Great Britain is a key political entity involved in colonial governance and loyalty issues in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;Great Britain conducted treaties and diplomatic relations with Morocco.&lt;SEP&gt;A nation involved in treaties and relations with Morocco concerning trade, security, and political treaties.&lt;SEP&gt;Great Britain is a colonial power that captured Gibraltar and exerted influence over Moroccan ports and waters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73c170ba2d6f65788ae52ee29483e59d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1b63bc45a96581a48ba223c8d48c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67661a5e143ad1775de09087e2149acd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="British America">
      <data key="d0">British America</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">British America is a region that, in less than twenty years, will have a larger population than Great Britain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canada">
      <data key="d0">Canada</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Canada is the territory involved, historically transformed under British rule and influenced by French and British cultural and political factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Canada is the country discussed where it was divided into Upper and Lower Canada in 1791, with distinct political and cultural dynamics between English and French populations.&lt;SEP&gt;Canada, particularly its British colonies, is involved in constitutional and political developments discussed in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Canada was the British colony where Durham administered during the rebellion and issued his policies.&lt;SEP&gt;Canada is the region involved in the political conflict with various historical entities.&lt;SEP&gt;Canada is the country where Papineau was active in politics and returned to in 1845.&lt;SEP&gt;A country in North America where the subject was enjoying his freedom in the Queen's Dominion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-353f63fa013a00ee4a62ffebc8c022a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d3c806038738c5cf824708ed3cfff5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58ea9058e9ed8c78aad18db9d953d662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6059f7b6685e9fbba6db55d93c65ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb71f32b0ab08e17e4d6cb83be9c380c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da749e17a56c35554061ac541d67d08c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-308dc1f4e23e0e8a33feabd4ae43b18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-297e2cf3fdd658f56fcb73ec4f707e74</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611986</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ninety-Two Resolutions">
      <data key="d0">Ninety-Two Resolutions</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ninety-Two Resolutions are a set of political demands related to constitutional reform and responsible government in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions were a list of grievances by the Assembly calling for political reforms in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions are a formal statement or petition expressing grievances, introduced and advocated for redress.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions represented the demands of the Patriotes during the conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ninety-Two Resolutions are a set of demands associated with the Patriotes' political movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392528a8e340519b1401fbf1b71aad8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b195a27b43ec85da2d0175fa01cef0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d52a624a7e98a490d86c7ae612749e5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="House of Commons">
      <data key="d0">House of Commons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The House of Commons was involved in reviewing reports and reports concerning French-Canadian rights and policies.&lt;SEP&gt;The legislative body that reviewed reports and made recommendations concerning Quebec's governance.&lt;SEP&gt;The House of Commons is the legislative body of the UK that appointed a committee to inquire into Canadian grievances.&lt;SEP&gt;The House of Commons is the legislative body that debated a measure concerning the suspension of the constitution of Lower Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa1b94cc88676d252f09238e9d2fcd2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dac7fb6c5e874c886dbb8df30d7d6fd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da749e17a56c35554061ac541d67d08c</data>
