    </node>
    <node id="Jean Antoine Bouthillier">
      <data key="d0">Jean Antoine Bouthillier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jean Antoine Bouthillier was the official editor of _Le Canadien_, promoting moderate but militant French Canadian perspectives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pierre Bedard">
      <data key="d0">Pierre Bedard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pierre Bedard was a prominent member of the House of Assembly and on the staff of _Le Canadien_, defending French interests.&lt;SEP&gt;Pierre Bedard was an officer and a proprietor associated with the publication of _Le Canadien_, and was arrested and imprisoned during political unrest.&lt;SEP&gt;Father of Elzear Bedard, associated with the authorship of the Ninety-Two Resolutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Roman Catholic Priest">
      <data key="d0">Roman Catholic Priest</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Roman Catholic Priests were influential figures whose authority was targeted by Sir James Craig's regime.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reign of Terror">
      <data key="d0">Reign of Terror</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'Reign of Terror' was a period of oppressive governance under Sir James Craig aimed at suppressing French Canadian influence and loyalty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="French land laws">
      <data key="d0">French land laws</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">French land laws included rents and feudal burdens paid to the Seigneur and tythes to the Priest, shaping French Canadian social structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="House of Assembly">
      <data key="d0">House of Assembly</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The House of Assembly was the legislative body in Lower Canada, involved in debates over taxation and political conflicts.&lt;SEP&gt;The House of Assembly is the legislative body in Lower Canada where Papineau was elected and served as speaker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="French-Canadian majority">
      <data key="d0">French-Canadian majority</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The French-Canadian majority often carried votes to support their interests and oppose Anglo-American policies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="English minority">
      <data key="d0">English minority</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The English minority opposed French dominance and advocated for land taxes on landowners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="French law">
      <data key="d0">French law</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">French law involved land rents, feudal burdens, and tithes that French Canadians considered their own taxation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Feudal Burdens">
      <data key="d0">Feudal Burdens</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Feudal burdens refer to rents and tithes paid by French Canadians as part of their land laws, shaping social relations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b39e2cfb1e3f641af2dc7d617761e850</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Major Gugy">
      <data key="d0">Major Gugy</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Major Gugy advised Wetherall to halt and then took on a role as commissary, billeting men and horses.&lt;SEP&gt;Major Gugy was a brave officer who survived the conflict and was wounded during the showdown.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Montmagny">
      <data key="d0">Montmagny</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Montmagny was a village that experienced local agitation during the rebellion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Etienne P. Tache">
      <data key="d0">Etienne P. Tache</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Etienne P. Tache was a local agitator in Montmagny, later became a politician and prime minister of Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Church interdict">
      <data key="d0">Church interdict</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Church placed an interdict on Abbe Chartier, forbidding him from setting foot on Canadian soil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian soil">
      <data key="d0">Canadian soil</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Canadian soil refers to the territory of Canada, which some rebels fled from and where church authority was enforced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian church">
      <data key="d0">Canadian church</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The church supported the rebellion initially, but later placed an interdict on Abbe Chartier for his actions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian militia">
      <data key="d0">Canadian militia</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Militia forces, including troops led by Sir John Colborne, fought against the rebels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian insurgents">
      <data key="d0">Canadian insurgents</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The insurgents were rebels fighting in Lower Canada during the 1837 uprising.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5baf8115ec01000bf9bea26709c3f099</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bill of Supply">
      <data key="d0">Bill of Supply</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The bill of supply was a legislative measure for funding government expenses, which was rejected multiple times due to political disputes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sir George Gipps">
      <data key="d0">Sir George Gipps</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir George Gipps was a commissioner and later governor of New South Wales, associated with Canadian affairs during Gosford's tenure.&lt;SEP&gt;Sir George Gipps was a colonial official who remained until 1837, involved in the colonial governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609506</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian Reports">
      <data key="d0">Canadian Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The reports by commissioners opposed responsible government and suggested suspending the constitution if crises arose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Resolutions">
      <data key="d0">Resolutions</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The resolutions addressed financial issues, control of revenues, and power to pay expenses without Assembly approval.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dac7fb6c5e874c886dbb8df30d7d6fd5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian Government">
      <data key="d0">Canadian Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Canadian Government is the ruling authority overseeing the colony and involved in colonial governance conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Requêtes">
      <data key="d0">Requêtes</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Requêtes refer to petitions or claims made by the Patriotes demanding political reforms and autonomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Status of Responsible Government">
      <data key="d0">Status of Responsible Government</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The status of responsible government was an issue contested by the commissioners and colonial leaders, with commissioners opposing its adoption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Suspension of the Constitution">
      <data key="d0">Suspension of the Constitution</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The suspension of the constitution was considered as a possible response to colonial crises, possibly more acceptable than partial interference.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Unctuous Blandishments">
      <data key="d0">Unctuous Blandishments</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Unctuous blandishments were diplomatic efforts initially believed to win over discontented Canadians, later deemed a failure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Royal Commissioners">
      <data key="d0">Royal Commissioners</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Royal Commissioners were officials sent to investigate and report on the conditions in the colony, producing six reports.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ReForm">
      <data key="d0">ReForm</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reform refers to the political demands and proposals for changes made by the Patriotes for better governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0359fb0e1dc5215c33545ffddcd78876</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Patriote Party">
      <data key="d0">Patriote Party</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Patriote party was a political group advocating for an elective Legislative Council and opposing the existing nominated council.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aylmer">
      <data key="d0">Aylmer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Aylmer was a political figure under whom a coolness developed between Neilson and Papineau, indicating political or ideological differences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Louis Bourdages">
      <data key="d0">Louis Bourdages</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Louis Bourdages was Papineau's chief lieutenant, involved in proposing resolutions such as the abolition of the Legislative Council.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quesnel">
      <data key="d0">Quesnel</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Quesnel was a politician associated with the bill for the independence of judges, which Neilson rebuked Papineau for language used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Elzear Bedard">
      <data key="d0">Elzear Bedard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Elzear Bedard introduced the Ninety-Two Resolutions, which were a political platform of the Patriote party.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esau">
      <data key="d0">Esau</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A biblical figure used metaphorically to describe the hand behind the Ninety-Two Resolutions, implying deception or falsehood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Home government">
      <data key="d0">Home government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the British colonial authorities to whom the Ninety-Two Resolutions were addressed, expressing grievances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="English-speaking members">
      <data key="d0">English-speaking members</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Members of the Patriot party who supported Papineau, including Wolfred Nelson and others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fc668a337064f7982e9ba35184cf528</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
