      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The American border is a geographical reference where Canadian refugees gathered and where agitation for rebellion was carried out.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-26bd7dcdcc38c1ce5338b530f0ab8694</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Provisional government">
      <data key="d0">Provisional government</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The provisional government was a temporary authority led by Robert Nelson seeking Canadian independence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-26bd7dcdcc38c1ce5338b530f0ab8694</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Colonial Office">
      <data key="d0">The Colonial Office</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Colonial Office was the British administrative body responsible for colonial affairs in British North America, including issuing the Report on the Affairs of British North America.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-26bd7dcdcc38c1ce5338b530f0ab8694</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colonel Panet">
      <data key="d0">Colonel Panet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonel Panet was a militia officer who fought in the defense of Quebec in 1775 and served as speaker of the House of Assembly since 1792.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Craig">
      <data key="d0">Craig</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Craig was a governor who suppressed the newspaper _Le Canadien_, arrested its printer and proprietors, and took measures reflecting suspicion of French Canadians.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Taschereau">
      <data key="d0">Taschereau</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Taschereau was one of the proprietors of _Le Canadien_ who was arrested and imprisoned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blanchet">
      <data key="d0">Blanchet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Blanchet was one of the proprietors of _Le Canadien_ who was arrested and imprisoned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bedard">
      <data key="d0">Bedard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Bedard was an officer, mathematician, and proprietor imprisoned during political unrest and refused release until trial and acquittal.&lt;SEP&gt;Bedard, a mathematician and proprietor, was imprisoned for his association with _Le Canadien_ and refused release until acquitted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Habeas Corpus">
      <data key="d0">Habeas Corpus</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Habeas corpus was a legal measure applied to Bedard and his friends, which was refused by Craig during their imprisonment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609514</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="J. A. Panet">
      <data key="d0">J. A. Panet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">J. A. Panet was a veteran who held the office of speaker of the House of Assembly in Lower Canada since 1792 before resigning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aubert de Gaspé">
      <data key="d0">Aubert de Gaspé</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Aubert de Gaspé was a college friend of Papineau who remarked on his eloquence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71f776a39a2f6e419cd9e7dc482a99e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="British Government">
      <data key="d0">British Government</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The British government is responsible for colonial administration and political decisions affecting Canada during the period discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;The British Government is an international authority that originally entrusted its subjects' disputes to the Court of Morocco but faces issues with the Moroccan legal system.&lt;SEP&gt;The British Government offered recognition and some favor to Mr. Lucas for his efforts during the siege.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea2d548d08b4129bfc47eab34719160b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9af5b64614e29079b8b6802af2587be</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="William IV">
      <data key="d0">William IV</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">William IV was the king of Britain, whose constitutional principles influenced policy decisions regarding Canadian governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gugy">
      <data key="d0">Gugy</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Gugy was an English member of the Assembly who accused Papineau of intentions of civil war.&lt;SEP&gt;Major Gugy was the leader of the English party in the Assembly who instructed Wetherall to advance and attack the rebel position at St. Charles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Governing System">
      <data key="d0">Governing System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The governing system involved constitutional principles, legislative councils, and colonial administration challenges during the period discussed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reforms">
      <data key="d0">Reforms</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reforms refer to the political and constitutional changes sought by Canadian patriots and reformists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ae911c17e49d755ada12627d0a57c5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vaudreuil">
      <data key="d0">Vaudreuil</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaudreuil is a young barrister, considered to have considerable promise, and was a close follower and confidant of Papineau during the last sessions of the Lower Canada legislature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Perrault">
      <data key="d0">Perrault</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Perrault contributed many letters to _La Minerve_, a publication involved in political discourse during the time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chambly">
      <data key="d0">Chambly</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Chambly was a location where Wetherall's forces received instructions, and Major Warde embarked a company for St Hilaire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="St Hilaire">
      <data key="d0">St Hilaire</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">St Hilaire was where Wetherall's column reached the day after leaving Chambly, and where he obtained information about Gore's status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Debartzch">
      <data key="d0">Debartzch</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Debartzch was the owner of a manor-house fortified by the rebels, who fled to Montreal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86a0a44acd086e0907bf225584c3be7a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="British authorities">
      <data key="d0">British authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The British authorities expected strong opposition at St Charles, conducted military operations, and issued arrest warrants for Patriote leaders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Assembling Forces">
      <data key="d0">Assembling Forces</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The rebels at St Charles fortified their position and appointed Thomas Storrow Brown as their commander.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
