    </node>
    <node id="Major Warde">
      <data key="d0">Major Warde</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Major Warde was the commandant at Chambly who sent a company to reinforce Wetherall's forces at St Hilaire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a14313aff1307f7a4b44f484aaa9939a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="English Common Law">
      <data key="d0">English Common Law</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">English common law refers to the body of customary law originating in England that has been incorporated into the legal systems of many English-speaking countries, but has been almost completely excluded in the context of Canada according to the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Church of the Vanquished">
      <data key="d0">Church of the Vanquished</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Church of the Vanquished was established in Canada through legal enforcement of obligations upon Catholics to pay tithes, representing a religious institution intertwined with legal and social history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Roman Catholics">
      <data key="d0">Roman Catholics</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">Roman Catholics in Canada were granted privileges such as sitting in Canadian Assemblies but were prohibited from sitting in the House of Commons at Westminster.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian Assemblies">
      <data key="d0">Canadian Assemblies</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Canadian representative Assemblies created by the Act of 1791 provided political rights to French Canadians, including the use of the French language.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Act of 1791">
      <data key="d0">Act of 1791</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Act of 1791 was legislation that divided Canada into Upper and Lower Canada and established political rights and structures for each.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="English Members">
      <data key="d0">English Members</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">English members in the assembly represented British interests, generally preferred English language use, and opposed the use of French in official proceedings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="French Members">
      <data key="d0">French Members</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">French Canadian members of the assembly advocated for the recognition of the French language and emphasized their attachment to their cultural identity and loyalty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="French Language">
      <data key="d0">French Language</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The French language was a key issue in debates over its official recognition and use in the Canadian Assembly, symbolizing cultural and linguistic identity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f4557b5a58dc496c18ce0c8f8f2c88</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
