      <data key="d0">Linden Theatrical Company</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Linden Theatrical Company performed at the Athenaeum and suffered damages during the fire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dr. Schell">
      <data key="d0">Dr. Schell</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dr. Schell was a well-known physician who occupied a small frame building near the hotel and criticized city authorities.&lt;SEP&gt;Dr. Schell was trapped under the ruins of the hotel and was rescued unharmed after the collapse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="City Authorities">
      <data key="d0">City Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The city authorities were criticized by Dr. Schell for lax enforcement of laws.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Windstorm">
      <data key="d0">Windstorm</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A powerful windstorm struck the city, causing part of the hotel walls to fall and damaging nearby buildings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fire Department">
      <data key="d0">Fire Department</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The fire department responded quickly to the fire and rescue operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The fire department worked to combat the fire, relying on cisterns and pumping water from the river.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf33d450055327352cd4545502529ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d26c30cc3102d9679f3880d6b7c95b61</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="W.L. Wilson">
      <data key="d0">W.L. Wilson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">W.L. Wilson learned of the sick couple during the fire and arranged for their safe transportation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Old Post Office">
      <data key="d0">Old Post Office</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The old post office was located on the corner of Wabasha and Fifth Streets and was replaced by the Mansion House.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mansion House">
      <data key="d0">Mansion House</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mansion House was situated on Wabasha Street and was part of the early hotel fires.&lt;SEP&gt;The Mansion House was a popular resort built by Nicholas Pottgieser at a cost of $12,000, serving travelers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e857eaec34481e1ebe2d9949bee6999e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609995</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nicholas Pottgieser">
      <data key="d0">Nicholas Pottgieser</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicholas Pottgieser was the builder of the Mansion House.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609995</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hawkes">
      <data key="d0">Hawkes</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Hawkes was a guest at the Mansion House who accidentally shot his wife and was tried for murder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609995</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="International Hotel">
      <data key="d0">International Hotel</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Hotel, formerly the Fuller House, was a large five-story brick hotel built in 1856 on Seventh and Jackson streets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609995</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="A.G. Fuller">
      <data key="d0">A.G. Fuller</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A.G. Fuller built the Fuller House, later renamed the International Hotel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609995</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="William H. Seward">
      <data key="d0">William H. Seward</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A prominent Republican statesman who delivered a notable speech in Minnesota.&lt;SEP&gt;A historical figure whose speech was falsely fabricated and published as part of the bogus news.&lt;SEP&gt;William H. Seward stayed at the International Hotel during his trip to the West in 1860.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c49b770a2f437d2a1478f52b0f2747ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-253bf03f6503d8e5da2098445ef9b630</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609995</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gen. Pope">
      <data key="d0">Gen. Pope</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Gen. Pope was in command after the Battle of Wood Lake and reported the end of the Indian war.&lt;SEP&gt;General Pope commanded the department of the Northwest and declared the Indian war over after the battle.&lt;SEP&gt;Gen. Pope stayed at the hotel and was involved in the military activities against the Sioux.&lt;SEP&gt;Gen. Pope claimed that Porter was within sound of his guns at the Second Battle of Bull Run but remained inactive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078fa88c408ca99e058ba3c5129a1858&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49dd4de896cb4d1aa7c0c3db3861fcd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7096033ca5d17f317a3de07c28f9b0bc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609995</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gen. Sibley">
      <data key="d0">Gen. Sibley</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Gen. Sibley was a military leader who arrived at Fort Ridgely to command troops during the Sioux uprising.&lt;SEP&gt;Gen. Sibley led military operations moving toward Sioux engagements, including the Battle of Wood Lake.&lt;SEP&gt;General Sibley led the troops to Camp Release and was involved in negotiations with Indian prisoners.&lt;SEP&gt;Gen. Sibley was a military leader present at the hotel during the same period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49dd4de896cb4d1aa7c0c3db3861fcd1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7096033ca5d17f317a3de07c28f9b0bc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609995</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Samuel Mayall">
      <data key="d0">Samuel Mayall</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Samuel Mayall gained ownership of the hotel in 1861 and renamed it the International Hotel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
