    </node>
    <node id="Guests">
      <data key="d0">Guests</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Guests are individuals staying in the hotel, evacuating in night clothes during the fire.&lt;SEP&gt;Numerous guests, including legislators, were in the hotel during the fire, facing life-threatening danger.&lt;SEP&gt;The guests are hundreds or thousands of tiny creatures of various species, attending a festive gathering in the cave.&lt;SEP&gt;The Guests are large creatures who may sit on each other due to their size, causing concern for the host about politeness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf33d450055327352cd4545502529ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ce275f5f1e049ce82f2c5c025f7d225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91c4a284dc9ce506ed9cd886b83df824&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91c775951405f76d18f8b6972a487cd4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613375</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fire Alarm">
      <data key="d0">Fire Alarm</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The fire alarm was turned on to alert guests and firemen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609996</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Firemen">
      <data key="d0">Firemen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Firemen are volunteers or professionals responsible for extinguishing fires in pioneer days, with a history dating back hundreds of years.&lt;SEP&gt;Firefighters responsible for fighting fires, often overwhelmed during large fires and sometimes assisted by police.&lt;SEP&gt;Firemen responded immediately to rescue guests and fight the hotel fire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd305fbce6d35b0ef2d81f49f11311ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40ca577fc1aab8ffff670ab327655dda</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609996</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wabasha">
      <data key="d0">Wabasha</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Wabasha was another location affected during the Sioux outbreak.&lt;SEP&gt;Sioux chief, involved in negotiations, and the father of Wabasha (the county and street are named after him).&lt;SEP&gt;Sioux chief, son-in-law of Wabasha, who was condemned but not found guilty of participating in massacres.&lt;SEP&gt;Sioux chief and father-in-law of Wabasha, involved in the events surrounding prisoners’ confessions.&lt;SEP&gt;Wabasha is a street in the city where the Emmert house and old post office were located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7096033ca5d17f317a3de07c28f9b0bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a3ab772f4c7d1211b5007a6c92e23da</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609996</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fifth Street">
      <data key="d0">Fifth Street</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Fifth Street is a street in the city where the old post office and Mansion House stood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3b2fb8cc8d028b5cd15ca31eb42884f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764609996</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
