    <node id="Corinna">
      <data key="d0">Corinna</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Corinna is a multifaceted female figure prominent in classical literature and poetic tradition, whose representations span a range of themes including beauty, modesty, inspiration, and personal relationships. She is often characterized as a woman of beauty and modesty, as described in poetic scenes and elegies, highlighting her aesthetic appeal and humble demeanor. In some accounts, Corinna is depicted as a poetess renowned for her literary talent and lyric excellence, inspiring poets and being associated with fame and poetic achievement.

She also appears as a muse or symbol of inspiration, having warmed the narrator’s genius and being invoked in poetic contexts to represent artistic and romantic ideals. Her role as an illustrious poetess, possibly historical or legendary, underscores her significance in literary history. Additionally, Corinna is referenced as someone who learned to deceive her keeper and escape using her own verse, indicating a legendary or romantic narrative involving wit and poetic cunning.

Beyond literary and poetic themes, Corinna is portrayed as a woman who faced obstacles and demonstrated triumph, sometimes being the owner of a mistress or involved in contexts of protection and victory. She is associated with personal relationships characterized by longing and complex dynamics, such as a tyrannical mistress whose dominance causes suffering, and a recipient of messages requiring reading and replies, highlighting social and communicative aspects.

Corinna also appears in contexts related to travel and danger, being advised against her voyage to Baiæ and associated with sea travel and perilous journeys. In some descriptions, she is linked to symbols of companionship and loss, exemplified by the narrative of a parrot she once owned that has now died. She is referenced in connection with social roles like the 'ornatrix' or 'tiring woman,' indicating involvement with dress or attire, and sometimes referenced in the context of farewell speeches, suggesting her presence in various literary and cultural moments.

Historically, the Roman poetess Corinna is known for her extravagance and viciousness, potentially wearing fine Coan cloth, which adds a layer of personal or stylistic detail to her character. These diverse portrayals depict Corinna as a complex figure embodying artistic inspiration, personal resilience, social roles, and poetic legacy within Greek and Roman literary traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1210480b5c05b18ce59cb21e5b063e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ba3706ec176b6f1bdc3b92e3affac80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-432add00f9047dd14d52a61f532cf54d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b49e205c56dbae8d49f65c119fc9144a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d18197681a28270c1f146277731c3f51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1cbc70bf76a9f17da10b1c67a4da593&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4958bc3b034dd7b59c1c0df2fab2b8d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de69a3d99803079ed2ffea77329d6210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a88a2ba3aaa4f2f07b382ccde92f86d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3acd5a0f264338faea85dbb5eea34f00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa28345d39e77952274cbff7d1c3944e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62d9981c359a23239322d1ef69f6eabb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442f3a60382a33a67c2d1a47639643cf</data>
