    <node id="Jupiter">
      <data key="d0">Jupiter</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jupiter is a multifaceted entity encompassing both mythological deity and celestial body, and each aspect contributes to its diverse significance. As a prominent figure in Roman mythology, Jupiter is recognized as the king of gods, embodying divine authority, power, and celestial leadership. He is often associated with the heavens and exerts control over divine and natural forces, notably wielding fire, lightning, and divine authority. In mythological narratives, Jupiter is known for transforming into various forms such as a bird (swan), gold, or a bull, often in contexts involving divine intervention or mythic symbolism, with notable stories involving a virgin upon him. 

Jupiter’s role extends to divine punishment and retribution, as he is depicted as an actor in myths involving divine justice, such as the myth involving Endymion and the mythological father of Tityus. Additionally, he is associated with nurturing aspects, as seen in references to his mythic nurture of Jove in Crete and favor towards individuals like Iasius. 

In Roman culture, Jupiter was a central figure in religious practices; games and celebrations were often dedicated to him following Roman military successes, emphasizing his role in public and state affairs. He was also referenced metaphorically and symbolically in literary and poetic contexts, such as the "Triumph of Cupid," where he appears as a divine figure representing authority and power. 

Aside from his mythological and religious significance, Jupiter is also the name of a planet in the Solar System. Its planetary nature is referenced alongside mythological elements, such as the myth of violence involving Ceres, suggesting a symbolic or allegorical connection between the celestial body and the divine figure's attributes. Overall, Jupiter encapsulates a complex blend of divine power, mythological symbolism, cultural reverence, and celestial presence, making it a notable and multifaceted figure in both ancient myth and modern astronomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b6cab761408cb58a952d84a4e27dce81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1af75b0ed4f2d647b015eaa8b0eac561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c781927a13f05b8bf3cc68dedd0ed839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ae1d708e454e0e6bf3e72d6ca49fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-094a55bf6bce40e6d7aa0cbb6247afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79465f6fd525b49edfa758554ce7718c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a40ad400e6c46c8451bc02d5c90eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4315ba5dfb6f6d19c3b64e08d29cdc1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24655aa74002f9c9a1716299b6bb40bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-714b6c3790dfcbb71e477248f2e13f46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64676dc63a3bd3d7dc906d1feffc619e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442f3a60382a33a67c2d1a47639643cf</data>
