    <node id="Laomedon">
      <data key="d0">Laomedon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Laomedon is a Trojan king and the father of Ilia, associated with mythological stories involving tears and divine interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Laomedon was a mythological king from whom a mythological descendant, through Ascanius, is said to have descended.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5cee73398d032581450496a09223dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610589</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Streams">
      <data key="d0">Streams</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Streams are flowing bodies of water described in mythological and poetic contexts, including various rivers such as Inachus, Xanthus, Peneus, Achelous, and others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d13dcdd4ee9b4b632310a1a196718c4b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610589</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inachus">
      <data key="d0">Inachus</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Inachus is a mythological river referenced in relation to the rivers discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;Inachus is a mythological river god associated with flowing with love for Melie, the Bithynian Nymph.&lt;SEP&gt;Inachus is a mythological river, addressed in poetic apology, one of the rivers mentioned along with others.&lt;SEP&gt;Inachus is a river located in Argolis, Peloponnesus, associated with ancient Greek mythology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d13dcdd4ee9b4b632310a1a196718c4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5cee73398d032581450496a09223dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610589</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melie">
      <data key="d0">Melie</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Melie is a Bithynian Nymph who is associated with love for river gods like Inachus.&lt;SEP&gt;Melie is a Nymph loved by Neptune and mother of Amycus, associated with Bithynia in Asia Minor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610589</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Xanthus">
      <data key="d0">Xanthus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Xanthus is a mythological river god who was attracted to the Arcadian maiden.&lt;SEP&gt;Xanthus is a rivulet near Troy, mentioned in connection with Creüsa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610590</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arcadian Maid">
      <data key="d0">Arcadian Maid</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Arcadian maiden is a mythological figure mentioned in relation to Xanthus, indicating love and longing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610590</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peneus">
      <data key="d0">Peneus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Peneus is a river god who concealed Creüsa, betrothed to Xanthus, in the lands of the Phthiotians.&lt;SEP&gt;Peneus is a river in Thessaly, associated with the Naïad Creüsa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610590</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Creüsa">
      <data key="d0">Creüsa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Creüsa is a mythological figure betrothed to Xanthus, concealed by Peneus.&lt;SEP&gt;Creüsa is a mythological figure, granddaughter of Laomedon, and associated with the descent lineage.&lt;SEP&gt;Creüsa is a Naïad associated with Peneus and mother of Hypseas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5cee73398d032581450496a09223dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610590</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Asopus">
      <data key="d0">Asopus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Asopus is a river god associated with Thebe, who received five daughters from Mars.&lt;SEP&gt;Asopus is a river god associated with Metope's children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610590</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thebe">
      <data key="d0">Thebe</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Thebe is a mythological city associated with Asopus and Mars, and the parent of five daughters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610590</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Achelous">
      <data key="d0">Achelous</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Achelous is a river god with horns, which are said to have been broken away by Hercules.&lt;SEP&gt;Achelous is a river god and opponent of Hercules in the contest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610590</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Apollo">
      <data key="d0">Apollo</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Apollo is a prominent deity originating from Greek mythology, with significant associations encompassing a broad spectrum of domains including beauty, the sun, artistic inspiration, music, prophecy, and moral order. As a Greek god, Apollo is often depicted as a multifaceted figure whose divine influence extends over the skies and human morality. He is symbolized in statues linked to poetry and wealth, emphasizing his role in artistic and material prosperity.

Apollo is notably connected with the temple where statues of Danaus and his daughters were housed, illustrating his importance within religious worship and divine representation. His relationship with artistic inspiration is further highlighted through references to poetry and musical vows, notably in connection with the mythological figure of the Lesbian damsel and figures such as Linus, whose death was marked by the exclamation "Ælinon"—a shout associated with Apollo's poetic and prophetic attributes.

Additionally, Apollo's attributes include the bow and arrow, symbolizing his martial and hunting skills, and his divine influence over the skies. He is also associated with prophecy, being the father of the legendary musician and poet Orpheus. Mythologically, Apollo is depicted as a deity capable of punishing enemies, such as piercing Tityus with darts, showcasing his authoritative and sometimes vengeful aspect.

Furthermore, Apollo's mythological associations extend to various stories and symbolic elements, such as the comparison of his hair to beauty, and the mention of a temple where a lady walks, emphasizing his worship and cultural significance across Greek and Roman traditions. Overall, Apollo is a central figure embodying artistic inspiration, prophecy, physical prowess, and divine authority within classical mythology and religion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1af75b0ed4f2d647b015eaa8b0eac561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ae1d708e454e0e6bf3e72d6ca49fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b49e205c56dbae8d49f65c119fc9144a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf1f7f24935168c94381fb8b884628f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c9d540a7609b6ba4c69dd1651e5329e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-714b6c3790dfcbb71e477248f2e13f46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e879d2a9ee2a96c5c34084b6728bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbda8e1999ba84d769ed820fc5af83a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610590</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hercules">
      <data key="d0">Hercules</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Hercules is a mythological hero who broke the horns of Achelous.&lt;SEP&gt;Hercules is a hero associated with Deianira, mentioned in classical epistles and literature.&lt;SEP&gt;Hercules is a classical hero, mentioned in the context of Deianira's epistle and mythological stories involving jewelry.&lt;SEP&gt;Hercules is a mythological hero involved in a contest for Deianira.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af6145f9b5523efc30cc24ca7c9e03db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cc8c8a5ed910d89548c7358325c25cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79465f6fd525b49edfa758554ce7718c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610591</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Calydon">
      <data key="d0">Calydon</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Calydon was a notable region in ancient Greece, considered of less value compared to other regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Calydon is the city in Ætolia, associated with Aeneus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610591</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ætolia">
      <data key="d0">Ætolia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ætolia is an ancient region in Greece, considered of less value compared to Deianira.&lt;SEP&gt;Region where Calydon and Aeneus reigned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Deianira">
      <data key="d0">Deianira</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Deianira is a figure associated with value, mentioned as significant compared to the regions of Calydon and Ætolia.&lt;SEP&gt;Deianira is referenced in an epistle, notable in Greek mythology as associated with Hercules.&lt;SEP&gt;Deianira is a mythological figure, author of an epistle to Hercules, associated with classical jewelry references.&lt;SEP&gt;Deianira is the wife of Hercules, central to the contest depicted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af6145f9b5523efc30cc24ca7c9e03db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cc8c8a5ed910d89548c7358325c25cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Evadne">
      <data key="d0">Evadne</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Evadne is a mythological daughter of Asopus, involved in stories of love and myth.&lt;SEP&gt;Evadne is the daughter of Asopus, and her name is associated with her mother in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Salmoneus">
      <data key="d0">Salmoneus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Salmoneus is a mythological figure mentioned in relation to river waters, specifically associated with the myth involving his daughter.&lt;SEP&gt;Salmoneus is the king of Pisa in Elis, linked to Tyro and the story of Enipeus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Enipeus">
      <data key="d0">Enipeus</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Enipeus is a river god who dried up to embrace Salmoneus' daughter.&lt;SEP&gt;The Enipeus is a river that river god Neptune is said to have assumed his form in relation to, and is associated with mythological stories.&lt;SEP&gt;Enipeus is a river associated with Tyro and Neptune’s myth involving her enamor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ae80ee2d9a869de6cf9860587e59e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5cee73398d032581450496a09223dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Body">
      <data key="d0">Body</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The body is the physical structure of a person, used for attack with the right arm and protection with the left arm during boxing.&lt;SEP&gt;The body needs food and water to grow, stay healthy, and perform bodily functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The body requires fat, water, and nutrients from food to stay healthy, warm, and functional.&lt;SEP&gt;The human body processes food through various parts including the mouth, stomach, and intestines to digest and absorb nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-45e00ab8b0c8791550edf531523e2880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-416151ab22ab2af93343eed774c95e2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67b83f16f525fc1670d5a9242f36cd37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efb927db7ea2bc4669beaaff8354b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476f39fcc8c0c7619821c2b1143970d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-306d5de2eae0665c92137b63fb6d96d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c40e9d9d6bed631bcdf0022bea513804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b1925755a5c65f004018f45749c7912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a2b65dd2ccf6640dc6d501c52589484&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f6861a405786e2c037bf50463f3c97b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612171</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Right Arm">
      <data key="d0">Right Arm</data>
      <data key="d1">part</data>
      <data key="d2">The right arm is primarily used for attacking in boxing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-45e00ab8b0c8791550edf531523e2880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610593</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Left Arm">
      <data key="d0">Left Arm</data>
      <data key="d1">part</data>
      <data key="d2">The left arm is mainly used for protecting the body in boxing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-45e00ab8b0c8791550edf531523e2880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610593</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Teeth">
      <data key="d0">Teeth</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Teeth are often knocked out during boxing fights.&lt;SEP&gt;Teeth are organs made of bone covered with enamel, used for cutting and grinding food.&lt;SEP&gt;Teeth are used to chew food and must be cared for properly to help the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;Teeth are affected by tobacco, causing discoloration and sores.&lt;SEP&gt;Teeth are hard structures in the mouth made of enamel that help in biting and chewing food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-45e00ab8b0c8791550edf531523e2880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-339a04378219e12fdd299ea57c43f944&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f4c8f69196485bd4b711834dcd13e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efb927db7ea2bc4669beaaff8354b755</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612160</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ears">
      <data key="d0">Ears</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ears can become disfigured as a result of boxing.&lt;SEP&gt;Ears are parts of the head that capture sound and transmit it via the nerve of hearing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-45e00ab8b0c8791550edf531523e2880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e22299aaf0c9c5e28f9fc9822446874d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c40e9d9d6bed631bcdf0022bea513804</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612173</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Boxers">
      <data key="d0">Boxers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Boxers are athletes who engage in boxing, following specific rules that prohibit holding or tripping their opponents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-45e00ab8b0c8791550edf531523e2880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610593</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Etruria">
      <data key="d0">Etruria</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Etruria is a historical region in Italy where boxing was practiced in early times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-45e00ab8b0c8791550edf531523e2880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
