    <node id="Lyre">
      <data key="d0">Lyre</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The lyre is a traditional stringed musical instrument renowned for its harmonious sound and association with poetic and artistic expression. Typically played horizontally, the lyre features tensioned strings that are plucked with a plectrum or fingers, and it has undergone various constructional variations across different cultures and eras. Its design often includes gilded ornamentation, emphasizing its importance as a ceremonial and artistic instrument.

Historically, the lyre is deeply rooted in ancient civilizations, prominently featured in Greek, Egyptian, Hebrew, and Roman cultures. In Greek culture, the lyre is frequently depicted in art and myth, notably associated with the poet Amphion, who was said to use his lyre to build the city of Thebes. Greek variations of the lyre include types such as the _lyra_, _kithara_, _chelys_, and _phorminx_, each with unique structural features. The lyre also played a significant role in assisting poets and musicians, especially in Lesbos, where it is linked with poetic and musical themes, often symbolizing cultural identity.

In Egyptian engravings, the lyre appears as an important instrument used in musical performances, reflecting its long-standing significance throughout history. The Hebrew coins depict the lyre as a symbol of musical tradition in ancient times, with models varying in construction. The Roman adaptations of the lyre, inspired by Greek models, encompass variations such as the _lyra_, _cithara_, and _fidis_, illustrating its evolution and widespread popularity across the Mediterranean basin.

Throughout history, the lyre has appeared in Christian art from the 4th century onwards, signifying its continued cultural relevance in early European societies. Despite stylistic and structural differences among these variations, the fundamental form remains recognizable across cultures and periods, making the lyre an enduring symbol of musical and poetic heritage. Its depiction in various historical contexts underscores its importance as a instrument of artistic expression and cultural identity across civilizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ae1d708e454e0e6bf3e72d6ca49fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b49e205c56dbae8d49f65c119fc9144a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c65528b916ab8f0ecae0b5c8df37467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e481d0b0dc9178db4c1b9a451a72ab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ae2e9bd1b638782eb9d45c99ff65a68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e7feca07c634684d8111b02a41fcae1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18c204bf67b073ba131e7e3917e9d273&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f440a8129c9633e91b0a40eef56e62b1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613887</data>
