    <node id="Bacchus">
      <data key="d0">Bacchus</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Bacchus is the Roman god primarily associated with wine, celebration, and revelry. He embodies the pleasures of intoxication, festivity, and the social and cultural activities that accompany the consumption of wine. Bacchus is often depicted as a horned figure, symbolizing his connection to intoxication and the exuberance of festivities, and is frequently shown holding or associated with the thyrsus, a staff or wand that signifies his divine authority and the frenzied energy of his followers.

In addition to his role as the deity of wine, Bacchus is associated with musical celebrations and theatrical traditions, reflecting his influence on the arts and cultural expressions of joy. His worship and mythological stories also connect him to inspiration and divine madness, inspiring creativity and artistic pursuits among his devotees.

Bacchus is linked to various cultural and geographical references, including the conquest of the Gangetic land, highlighting his widespread importance beyond Italy and his association with exotic regions and their customs. Furthermore, he is sometimes depicted in stories emphasizing beauty, desire, and maternal duties, indicating the multifaceted nature of his divine persona. These attributes together illustrate Bacchus as a deity embodying not only the pleasures of wine but also the broader themes of celebration, artistic inspiration, and social cohesion in Roman culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-094a55bf6bce40e6d7aa0cbb6247afe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf1f7f24935168c94381fb8b884628f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc0c02df8cb82b091fb73a066662a32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1c921bbb2e03c67dfc84088fb4c60fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd2afa94631d5184fe4ed1acaebe619&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-714b6c3790dfcbb71e477248f2e13f46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e937ae9f431c4368aa293a51bc61c19e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613928</data>
