    <node id="Juno">
      <data key="d0">Juno</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Juno is a prominent mythological goddess with multifaceted roles and significance across various contexts. She is primarily associated with overseeing aspects related to marriage, motherhood, and protection, emphasizing her role as a protector and guardian of familial bonds. In mythological narratives, Juno is linked to divine oversight and is often depicted as vigilant and jealous, exemplified by her watchful role over Io, whom she protected yet also persecuted, symbolized by her depiction with curving horns representing divine authority and oversight.

Juno is also associated with specific symbols, such as the "bird of Juno," reinforcing her connection to motherhood and sacred imagery. Within the divine hierarchy, she is ranked below Venus but holds a high-status position, surpassing other deities such as the Daughter in the realm hierarchy, indicating her importance and authority among the gods. Juno is honored during special festivals, where she is invoked in sacred rites and ceremonies, underscoring her role in religious and cultural practices.

In Roman mythology, Juno's influence extends into mythic tales, such as her appointment of Argus, whose fate is detailed in Ovid’s "Metamorphoses." She is also believed to have concealed herself during certain mythological events, symbolizing divine refuge and the protective nature attributed to her. Overall, Juno embodies themes of divine guardianship, authority, and sacred reverence, playing a vital role in mythological stories and religious observances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2ae1d708e454e0e6bf3e72d6ca49fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29eddb27a800eedb331c3ac515bfc570&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64ce64b0a51b17a08eb9a938fb755d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfdf9595877dab30524e561a06dbcca6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-540d8a1b5f98027bff4df2b8e31b99b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3a6fd5e17721f4573f70b6578a7f23a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3acd5a0f264338faea85dbb5eea34f00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442f3a60382a33a67c2d1a47639643cf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610608</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Phæacian Land">
      <data key="d0">Phæacian Land</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Phæacian land is referenced as a place that, if it had buried Tibullus, would be considered ignoble.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29eddb27a800eedb331c3ac515bfc570</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610608</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ceres">
      <data key="d0">Ceres</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Ceres is the Roman goddess of agriculture, mentioned in allegorical comparisons within the poetry.&lt;SEP&gt;Ceres is the goddess of agriculture, honored by husbandmen and associated with fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Ceres is the goddess of agriculture who presides over rites, fertility, and the harvest in Roman mythology.&lt;SEP&gt;Ceres is the Roman goddess of agriculture, grain, and fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Ceres is an ancient Roman goddess of agriculture, honored during a festival on the Fifth of the Ides of April with specific rites and customs.&lt;SEP&gt;Ceres is the goddess of agriculture, associated with sending Triptolemus to teach farming.&lt;SEP&gt;Ceres is a celestial body, associated with violence offered by Jupiter.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29eddb27a800eedb331c3ac515bfc570&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79465f6fd525b49edfa758554ce7718c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c9855ea8a01287d6710833c29e4d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4315ba5dfb6f6d19c3b64e08d29cdc1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1c921bbb2e03c67dfc84088fb4c60fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94a58ce86fabb001a8e0add34c5408f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64676dc63a3bd3d7dc906d1feffc619e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610608</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Minos">
      <data key="d0">Minos</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Minos was a lawgiver who wished for many fruitful years and is associated with the mythological law of Crete.&lt;SEP&gt;Minos was a legendary king of Crete, father of Ariadne, and associated with the mythological labyrinth and island of Naxos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29eddb27a800eedb331c3ac515bfc570&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83b39f09bb068668fc5d13770de07db5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764610608</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cretan Ida">
      <data key="d0">Cretan Ida</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Cretan Ida is a location noted for the mythological land where certain gods and events occurred.</data>
