    <node id="Phoebus">
      <data key="d0">Phoebus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Phoebus is a prominent figure in Greek and Roman mythology, serving as an epithet and alternate name for Apollo, the god associated with numerous domains including the sun, light, prophecy, poetry, and the arts. As a solar deity, Phoebus represents the sun itself, notably the setting sun or the declining daylight, symbolizing illumination, enlightenment, and divine insight. He is frequently depicted in poetic imagery as being at risk from new challenges, reflecting the dynamic nature of mythological storytelling.

In addition to his association with the sun, Phoebus is strongly linked to prophecy and divine communication, embodying the predictive and auspicious aspects of the deity. The term is also used in context of ancestral praise, emphasizing his reverence within poetic traditions and religious practices. Phoebus is recognized as a god of poetry and arts, often regarded as the divine patron of poetic work and artistic inspiration, marking the commencement of poetic endeavors and creative expression. He is also associated with divine love and the act of gift-giving, underscoring his role in nurturing inspiration and generosity from the divine realm.

Overall, Phoebus encapsulates the multifaceted character of Apollo as a god of the sun, light, prophecy, poetry, and arts, symbolizing divine illumination and artistic verve in Mediterranean mythology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91ae32cc554dc390c60bd5c873778659&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1c921bbb2e03c67dfc84088fb4c60fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24655aa74002f9c9a1716299b6bb40bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3a6fd5e17721f4573f70b6578a7f23a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a88a2ba3aaa4f2f07b382ccde92f86d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94a58ce86fabb001a8e0add34c5408f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e937ae9f431c4368aa293a51bc61c19e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-442f3a60382a33a67c2d1a47639643cf</data>
