    </node>
    <node id="The River">
      <data key="d0">The River</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The River refers to a natural waterway that plays a central role in various contexts across the descriptions. It functions as a physical pathway along which boats, such as sampans and other vessels, travel within the narrative, facilitating transportation and escape for the characters. The river is characterized by muddy banks and diverse wildlife, contributing to its natural landscape and scenic setting. It flows towards the sea with vivid descriptions of its appearance and movement, serving as a dynamic geographical feature within the story's environment.

Additionally, the river holds symbolic significance, representing transition and freedom, exemplified by Peng throwing objects into its waters. It acts as an escape route for characters fleeing from tumultuous situations, with changing currents influencing their movement and strategy. The river also features in scenes of conflict and attack, where boats are crept closer for observational or offensive purposes, emphasizing its strategic importance. 

Furthermore, the river environment is integral to the setting’s mood and scene development, being the location of key events such as the arrival of the Hakka boat, a man emerging from marsh areas, and relics being moved from defenses by characters like Heywood and the coolies. It provides a scenic backdrop, with river scenery and foot-paths enhancing the landscape depiction. Overall, the river is depicted both as a physical conduit facilitating movement, escape, and activity, and as a meaningful symbol within the narrative, enriching the setting's complexity and depth.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural feature symbolizing rural tranquility and the destination of the old man's journey.&lt;SEP&gt;The River, specifically the North Fork, is a flowing water body that has cut through the canyon.&lt;SEP&gt;The river is a natural feature referenced in Miss Trotter's memories of walking with girl pupils.&lt;SEP&gt;The river is implied in the context of the tide changing and affecting access to the island.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-389101049259102fc576c6e995467682&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfccb23dd43d27cfb79137b943a1e1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-941c4d13af541f7963a27e1494bf443b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfe3a458e44f6e2041ed190afdf2aa9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd506e2b4ab4403262e3cf611653609b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0830112ae5a46289fc2882b6e60a848&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-056576ea60bd0b171586a0c122f845b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f2c3ab9f6fe4aa540535b1858e6332&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ce0acb7c7df540cd62c4ae48d32a1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f06afe6acd299e703bd0c0fadd159c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd990ec3d60f1ee71ebfb13a84ee7393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac3dbacd92a1b10ced2bf57a2033bac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20c2cc3543d4fe4e05c57903e7ad2413&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85a79afa2951041547dfe3b8f02f3836&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fc15155697aef464caa48945b394d83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7ff57f304f2f9a7115f81e63308f19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34009f4313fe490d580935f9ef87ff52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf86751a46c4cce678f972d8be38307</data>
