    <node id="Polaris">
      <data key="d0">Polaris</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Polaris is a star used as a reference point for celestial observations, with its position varying at different elongations and latitudes.&lt;SEP&gt;Polaris is a star whose azimuth decreases slightly each year due to increasing declination, affecting astronomical observations.&lt;SEP&gt;Polaris is a bright star near the celestial north pole, used as a navigation point, with its azimuth depending on the observer's latitude.&lt;SEP&gt;A North Star used as a reference point for altitude measurement by observers at high latitudes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542ef42b55daca6f5424b791e6bd2765&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1573d79bcfed29e4fb110ec5c56a26fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2ab7ad03b323561e72dda983ec2d3db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Alioth">
      <data key="d0">Alioth</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Alioth is a star in the handle of the Big Dipper, used as a reference in star position observations to determine Polaris's elongation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Celestial Sphere">
      <data key="d0">The Celestial Sphere</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The celestial sphere is an imaginary sphere surrounding the Earth on which celestial objects are projected for positional measurements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Star Alioth">
      <data key="d0">The Star Alioth</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Alioth is a star whose relative position to Polaris helps determine the star's elongation and the observer's latitude.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Star Polaris">
      <data key="d0">The Star Polaris</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Polaris is a star whose position and elongation are used to determine the true meridian and the observer's latitude.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The North Pole">
      <data key="d0">The North Pole</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The North Pole is the celestial point around which the celestial sphere appears to rotate, and its position relates to Polaris's azimuth.&lt;SEP&gt;The North Pole is referenced metaphorically as the endpoint for preparing provisions, indicating a remote or challenging destination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe99e4059d4a83bdd69b8d710ad9ca66</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615475</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Celestial Horizon">
      <data key="d0">The Celestial Horizon</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The celestial horizon is the intersection of the celestial sphere with the plane passing through Earth's center, used as a reference in observation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Meridian">
      <data key="d0">The Meridian</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The meridian is the imaginary line passing through the celestial poles and the observer's zenith, used as a reference point in celestial observations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The True Meridian">
      <data key="d0">The True Meridian</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The true meridian is the great circle passing through the celestial poles and the zenith, indicating the actual north-south line at the observer's location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611617</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The North Celestial Pole">
      <data key="d0">The North Celestial Pole</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The North Celestial Pole is the point in the sky around which all the stars appear to rotate and is close to Polaris.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611617</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Azimuth of Polaris">
      <data key="d0">The Azimuth of Polaris</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The azimuth of Polaris varies with latitude and is used to determine the observer's position by measuring the angle between the star and the meridian.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38361c9228288e8fce93f69ba0277336</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611617</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Southern New Mexico">
      <data key="d0">Southern New Mexico</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Southern New Mexico is a region known for prospecting and mining activities, particularly around Lake Valley and the Perche district.</data>
