    <node id="Gas Engine">
      <data key="d0">Gas Engine</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The universal gas engine is a device described with multiple figures, indicating technological innovation in energy conversion.&lt;SEP&gt;A machine that converts fuel gases into mechanical energy, with this specific design being innovative.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of internal combustion engine that is single-acting, uses a gas and air mixture, and is designed for low-cost operation without compression of the explosive charge.&lt;SEP&gt;The gas engine is a 2-horsepower Otto gas-engine used to drive the coke breaker at Old Kent Road.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f30cb2bffa149e8d3e9c6f156dd6bbc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2ab7ad03b323561e72dda983ec2d3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b0e534bc4c65f3e841cfc12aa1c3144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a92b8aff8b2407fa2e62ff3ec614ae5a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rotary Ignition Valve">
      <data key="d0">Rotary Ignition Valve</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A novel valve system that uses a ratchet plate with radial slots to ignite the gas charge in the engine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f30cb2bffa149e8d3e9c6f156dd6bbc9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ratchet Plate">
      <data key="d0">Ratchet Plate</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A component of the rotary ignition valve, featuring radial slots to successively pass the ignition slot.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f30cb2bffa149e8d3e9c6f156dd6bbc9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cylinder">
      <data key="d0">Cylinder</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A major component of the gas engine where combustion occurs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f30cb2bffa149e8d3e9c6f156dd6bbc9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ignition">
      <data key="d0">Ignition</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of initiating combustion in the gas engine, achieved through the rotary ignition valve.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f30cb2bffa149e8d3e9c6f156dd6bbc9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ocelli">
      <data key="d0">Ocelli</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ocelli are markings affected by the similarity of tail and subcaudal feathers in Gallinaceans, with specific structural features related to tail feathers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectrices">
      <data key="d0">Rectrices</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectrices are tail feathers that are all flattened, spread horizontally, increasing in length from exterior to middle, forming a graceful curve when held slightly elevated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subcaudals">
      <data key="d0">Subcaudals</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Subcaudals are feathers located beneath the tail feathers, distinguished from rectrices but similar in certain markings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gallinaceans">
      <data key="d0">Gallinaceans</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Gallinaceans are a group of birds including the species discussed, characterized by specific feather structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ocellated Pheasant">
      <data key="d0">Ocellated Pheasant</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ocellated Pheasant is a bird species depicted with one figure.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ocellated Pheasant (_Rheinardius ocellatus_) is a bird with distinctive markings, tail feathers, and beak features, found in Tonkin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2ab7ad03b323561e72dda983ec2d3db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Buih-Dinh">
      <data key="d0">Buih-Dinh</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Buih-Dinh is a locality in Tonkin, south of Hue, where the Ocellated Pheasant was captured.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611626</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tonkin">
      <data key="d0">Tonkin</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Tonkin is a region in northern Vietnam, where the Buih-Dinh locality is situated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Maidenhair Tree">
      <data key="d0">The Maidenhair Tree</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Maidenhair Tree (Gingkgo biloba) is an ornamental deciduous tree, related to conifers, with fan-shaped leaves and a history dating back to fossil periods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gingkgo biloba">
      <data key="d0">Gingkgo biloba</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Gingkgo biloba, known as the Maidenhair Tree, is a unique ornamental tree noted for its deciduous, fan-shaped leaves and historical significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kaempfer">
      <data key="d0">Kaempfer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Kaempfer was a historian who first documented the Gingkgo biloba in 1690.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Loudon">
      <data key="d0">Loudon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Loudon was an author who related the story of the 'arbre a quarante écus,' highlighting the historical story of the Gingkgo biloba.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Andre Thouin">
      <data key="d0">Andre Thouin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Andre Thouin was a botanist who related the story of the Gingkgo, illustrating its historical significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="French Amateur">
      <data key="d0">French Amateur</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A French amateur bought five Gingkgo plants from England, which were propagated in France and led to the tree's nickname.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="English Nurseryman">
      <data key="d0">English Nurseryman</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The English nurseryman was the owner of five Gingkgo plants from Japanese seeds, sold to the French amateur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gingkgo">
      <data key="d0">Gingkgo</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Gingkgo, or Ginkgo biloba, is a dioecious tree with separate male and female plants, known for its fan-shaped leaves and historical presence since the coal period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dioecious plants">
      <data key="d0">Dioecious plants</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ginkgo biloba is dioecious, meaning it has separate male and female trees, with a rare occurrence of female trees in Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grafting">
      <data key="d0">Grafting</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Grafting is a propagation method used for Ginkgo biloba, especially for producing female trees from male trees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Broadlands">
      <data key="d0">Broadlands</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Broadlands is a location in Hants where a figure, a tree, is growing and measured.&lt;SEP&gt;Broadlands, Hants, is the place where a Ginkgo tree about 40 feet tall is growing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9bbc68e971d8dbaaccac8129cd9f19d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pursers Cross">
      <data key="d0">Pursers Cross</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Pursers Cross is the location where a Ginkgo tree was measured at 60 feet tall in 1837.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hants">
      <data key="d0">Hants</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Hants is a county in England, mentioned as the place where Broadlands is situated.&lt;SEP&gt;Hants (Hampshire) is the county where Broadlands is situated, with notable Ginkgo trees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8c3f67ad6c5ab11a1aa1c43bc0fc53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9bbc68e971d8dbaaccac8129cd9f19d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Horizontal Engine">
      <data key="d0">Horizontal Engine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The horizontal engine is a type of machinery commonly used for driving large powers, known for its durability, lower first cost, and ability to operate at higher speeds compared to the beam engine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Beam Engine">
      <data key="d0">Beam Engine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The beam engine is a type of machinery that is considered less efficient and durable than the horizontal engine, with higher initial costs and foundations requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vertical Engines">
      <data key="d0">Vertical Engines</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Vertical engines are among the best forms of engine design, closely approaching the advantages of horizontal engines, and are used in machinery driving.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Condensing Engine">
      <data key="d0">Condensing Engine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A condensing engine is a type of steam engine that uses water to condense exhaust steam, offering better efficiency when water is available at moderate cost.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Condensing Engine">
      <data key="d0">Non-Condensing Engine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-condensing engine does not use water for exhaust steam condensation and can be made to approximate the economy of a condensing engine with high steam pressure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Automatic Engine">
      <data key="d0">Automatic Engine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An automatic engine is a more expensive, high-class engine with features such as automatic operation, requiring less fuel, repairs, and depreciation costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Slide-Valve Engine">
      <data key="d0">Slide-Valve Engine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A slide-valve engine is a basic type of engine that costs less but doesn’t offer the advanced features of automatic engines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Horsepower">
      <data key="d0">Horsepower</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The engine has a capacity of one hundred horsepower and consumes a specified amount of coal per hour.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Steam Pressure">
      <data key="d0">Steam Pressure</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">High steam pressure allows non-condensing engines to approach the economy of condensing engines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Steam Engine">
      <data key="d0">Steam Engine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A steam engine is a machine that converts steam power into mechanical work, used historically for driving various machinery including potential considerations for efficiency and economy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ae48c64d3b36581a7e6806915748d3e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="North Latitude">
      <data key="d0">North Latitude</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">North latitude refers to the geographic coordinate specifying a location's distance north of the Equator, used in azimuth and astronomical calculations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Azimuth">
      <data key="d0">Azimuth</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Azimuth is the angle between the star Polaris and a reference direction (north), which increases with latitude and decreases slightly annually.&lt;SEP&gt;Azimuth is an angular measurement in a spherical coordinate system, indicating the direction of celestial objects like Polaris relative to the observer's location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1573d79bcfed29e4fb110ec5c56a26fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Star Declination">
      <data key="d0">Star Declination</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Star declination relates to the star's position relative to the celestial equator, affecting the azimuth observed at different latitudes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Refraction Table">
      <data key="d0">Refraction Table</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The refraction table provides corrections for apparent altitude minus refraction at various altitudes within the latitude of the United States.&lt;SEP&gt;A table used to correct observed altitude values for atmospheric refraction to determine precise latitude.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542ef42b55daca6f5424b791e6bd2765</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transit">
      <data key="d0">Transit</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The transit method involves using a device, such as a transit with a vertical arc, to observe celestial objects for establishing the meridian and exact location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Meridian">
      <data key="d0">Meridian</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The meridian is an imaginary line that passes through the celestial pole and the observer's location, used as a reference line in astronomical observations.&lt;SEP&gt;An imaginary line passing through the celestial poles and the observer's location, used as a reference in observational astronomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542ef42b55daca6f5424b791e6bd2765</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Altimeter">
      <data key="d0">Altimeter</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An altimeter, such as a sextant or a hand lantern, is used to measure the altitude of a star by observing its position relative to the cross wires.&lt;SEP&gt;An instrument or technique used to measure the altitude or elevation of celestial objects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542ef42b55daca6f5424b791e6bd2765</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Star Observation">
      <data key="d0">Star Observation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Star observation involves measuring the altitude or zenith distance of Polaris to determine latitude and establish the meridian.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Horizon">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Horizon</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An artificial horizon, such as a silvered reflector, is used in star observations to measure altitude accurately when natural horizons are not accessible.&lt;SEP&gt;A device sometimes used alongside a sextant to assist in measuring the altitude of celestial objects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542ef42b55daca6f5424b791e6bd2765</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Star Polaris">
      <data key="d0">Star Polaris</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Polaris, the North Star, is used for establishing a true meridian in astronomical applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Polaris is a star whose azimuth decreases slightly each year due to increasing declination, affecting astronomical observations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2ab7ad03b323561e72dda983ec2d3db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transit with Vertical Arc">
      <data key="d0">Transit with Vertical Arc</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A transit with a vertical arc is an astronomical instrument used to measure celestial altitudes and establish the meridian.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sextant">
      <data key="d0">Sextant</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A sextant is a navigational instrument used to measure the altitude of stars for determining latitude.&lt;SEP&gt;A navigational instrument used to measure the angular distance between a star and the horizon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac7ddc2e019049a9912f840df02ad08a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542ef42b55daca6f5424b791e6bd2765</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tank">
      <data key="d0">Tank</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A tank is located on the roof and filled by a force pump; it allows thorough washing and cleansing of machinery articles via connected hoses.&lt;SEP&gt;The water tank used for modern construction purposes is near the camp.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Force Pump">
      <data key="d0">Force Pump</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A force pump is used to fill the tank with liquid, enabling cleaning of machinery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hose">
      <data key="d0">Hose</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hoses connect with the tank and other machinery articles to facilitate washing and cleansing processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Defecator">
      <data key="d0">Defecator</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The defecator is a copper pan used to clarify cider by removing impurities through heating and floating scum.&lt;SEP&gt;The defecator is used to scald and purify substances by sending hot water through it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Evaporator">
      <data key="d0">Evaporator</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The evaporator consists of copper tubes used to concentrate sirup by evaporation, adjusting its consistency for jelly production.&lt;SEP&gt;An evaporator is used to produce boiled cider or jelly by controlling steam pressure and heating.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Saccharometer">
      <data key="d0">Saccharometer</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A saccharometer shows the amount of reduction in the cider, guiding regulation for desired results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Steam Pressure Valve">
      <data key="d0">Steam Pressure Valve</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A valve in the steam supply pipe regulates steam pressure in the evaporator to control the process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Boiled Cider">
      <data key="d0">Boiled Cider</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Boiled cider is produced by reducing steam pressure and can be used for making pies, sauces, and other culinary products.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mixing Tub">
      <data key="d0">Mixing Tub</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The mixing tub holds about fifty gallons of jelly, where variations in reduction, sweetness, and sourness are equalized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jelly">
      <data key="d0">Jelly</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Jelly is produced from fruit, transferred from evaporators, and packaged for market; quality depends on the fruit and process.&lt;SEP&gt;A food item being fed to the hermit, representing nourishment and kindness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c686441b3937cff685f4451d4cd9685</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613385</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wooden Bucket">
      <data key="d0">Wooden Bucket</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A wooden bucket with cover and bail, used for family packaging of jelly, available in two sizes holding five and ten pounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fruit">
      <data key="d0">Fruit</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Fruit is processed to produce jelly; late-season and crab apples yield the best quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d170d1426917b3639e7243e4aade782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67b83f16f525fc1670d5a9242f36cd37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pomace">
      <data key="d0">Pomace</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Pomace is the residue of crushed fruit, which, after grinding and washing, is separated into pulp and seeds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Apple Seeds">
      <data key="d0">Apple Seeds</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Apple seeds are detached during processing, drop into still water, and are collected for sale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mill">
      <data key="d0">Mill</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The mill is a large establishment that employed engines to power its operations, specifically a flouring mill.&lt;SEP&gt;The mill is a processing building equipped with machinery such as a water-powered press, an endless chain elevator, a grater, and evaporator systems used for processing fruit and extracting juice.&lt;SEP&gt;The mill employs approximately six workers including a superintendent, sawyer, cider maker, jelly maker, helper, and packer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-855ee3c61d64dd76f89504f38e2a403d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Superintendent">
      <data key="d0">Superintendent</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The superintendent manages purchasing, measuring apples, bookkeeping, and overall mill operation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sawyer">
      <data key="d0">Sawyer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The sawyer manufactures lumber and saws slabs for furnace use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cider Maker">
      <data key="d0">Cider Maker</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The cider maker grinds apples and operates presses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jelly Maker">
      <data key="d0">Jelly Maker</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The jelly maker manages the defecator, evaporator, and mixing operations, also acting as fireman and engineer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Helper">
      <data key="d0">Helper</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The helper attends to apple seed troughs and assists in general tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Packer">
      <data key="d0">Packer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The packer handles packing, shipping, and assisting in operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Apple Crop">
      <data key="d0">Apple Crop</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">In 1880, the apple crop produced about forty-five tons of jelly; in 1881, the future goal is 100,000 bushels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Price of Apples">
      <data key="d0">Price of Apples</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">In 1880, apples cost six to eight cents per bushel; in 1881, fifteen cents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Improved Grape Bags">
      <data key="d0">Improved Grape Bags</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Grape bags, invented by M. Pelletier, are designed to protect and hasten the ripening of grapes using concentrated sunlight via curved glass.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="M. Pelletier">
      <data key="d0">M. Pelletier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">M. Pelletier is the inventor of the improved grape bag with a glass cover to enhance grape ripening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grape Bag">
      <data key="d0">Grape Bag</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The grape bag invented by G. Pelletier features a curved glass cover that concentrates sunlight to hasten fruit ripening and improve quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="G. Pelletier">
      <data key="d0">G. Pelletier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">G. Pelletier is the inventor of the improved grape bag with a glass cover designed to enhance grape ripening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-04ccd4dad326e3c82f9a671aff50b17e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rutland">
      <data key="d0">Rutland</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Rutland, Vermont, yields the largest variety and most choice specimens of marble suitable for statuary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8daf6c6c70a3c63c2e7f57dbc969f7c7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Berkshire">
      <data key="d0">Berkshire</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Berkshire, Massachusetts, supplies marble known for its reputation, second to Vermont.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8daf6c6c70a3c63c2e7f57dbc969f7c7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="New England">
      <data key="d0">New England</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The region where granite is particularly abundant and extensively quarried for construction purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;New England region provides marble of inferior quality compared to Vermont and other states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8daf6c6c70a3c63c2e7f57dbc969f7c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59ad55bacf8e991f7ec32f1bd324e6cb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pennsylvania">
      <data key="d0">Pennsylvania</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Pennsylvania is part of the marble belt and has marble quarries, notably near the state's borders.&lt;SEP&gt;Pennsylvania's farms increased by 22.7% since 1870.&lt;SEP&gt;Pennsylvania produces high-quality lime from burned limestone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8daf6c6c70a3c63c2e7f57dbc969f7c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a09a3405b807f7a33064172d2e463326</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="District of Columbia">
      <data key="d0">District of Columbia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The District of Columbia is included in the marble belt, using marble in buildings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8daf6c6c70a3c63c2e7f57dbc969f7c7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Georgia">
      <data key="d0">Georgia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Georgia is within the marble belt region.&lt;SEP&gt;Georgia is a U.S. state with granite deposits, though less extensively quarried.&lt;SEP&gt;Georgia is a U.S. state with almost 100% increase in farms, from 69,956 to 138,626.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8daf6c6c70a3c63c2e7f57dbc969f7c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71098a84f862320370cd4b30b1083858&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a09a3405b807f7a33064172d2e463326</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Alabama">
      <data key="d0">Alabama</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Alabama is within the marble belt region.&lt;SEP&gt;Alabama is a U.S. state with over 100% increase in farms, from 67,382 to 135,864.&lt;SEP&gt;Alabama experienced over 100% growth in farms, from 67,382 to 135,864.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8daf6c6c70a3c63c2e7f57dbc969f7c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71098a84f862320370cd4b30b1083858</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611634</data>
