      <data key="d0">Pelletier</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelletier is associated with developing and experimenting with methods of protecting grapes using glass bags and mechanical innovations to enhance grape ripening and prevent mildew.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be7e9aaf5247158341224d42d0080e17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="J. Cobnhill">
      <data key="d0">J. Cobnhill</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">J. Cobnhill is credited with inventing glass-fronted bags for grapes, aiming to improve grape ripening and protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be7e9aaf5247158341224d42d0080e17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tuileries Gardens">
      <data key="d0">Tuileries Gardens</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Tuileries Gardens in France hosted a fete where an apparatus for concentrating solar heat was demonstrated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be7e9aaf5247158341224d42d0080e17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="French Public">
      <data key="d0">French Public</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The French public was introduced to an experimental device for utilizing solar heat at a fete in the Tuileries Gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be7e9aaf5247158341224d42d0080e17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="J. Mouchon">
      <data key="d0">J. Mouchon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Professor Mouchon of Tours conducted initial experiments on utilizing solar rays for energy applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be7e9aaf5247158341224d42d0080e17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tours">
      <data key="d0">Tours</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Tours is the city in France where Professor Mouchon began constructing solar energy apparatus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be7e9aaf5247158341224d42d0080e17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="M. De Rouga">
      <data key="d0">M. De Rouga</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">M. De Rouga is associated with the demonstration of a solar heat concentration apparatus at a fete in the Tuileries Gardens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be7e9aaf5247158341224d42d0080e17</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Water Power">
      <data key="d0">Water Power</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Water power is used to operate the machinery in the mill, including the press and other devices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cider">
      <data key="d0">Cider</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Cider is a liquid derived from apples that undergoes clarification, concentration, and purification processes in the mill.&lt;SEP&gt;Cider is a beverage poured into Roy’s whisky jug by Cæsar to trick him into refusing to work.&lt;SEP&gt;A beverage made from pressed apple juice that ferments over time, producing alcohol and bubbles.&lt;SEP&gt;Cider is a drink made from crushed apples that begins to develop alcohol as it sours, which is considered a poison and does not provide real strength.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d5b1a3d2da2bcf2bd68dce9ef1b9e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38509fbb14988b3d2417deb93ee40233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d170d1426917b3639e7243e4aade782</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612161</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Steam">
      <data key="d0">Steam</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Steam is a vapor generated by boiling water, used as a medium for delivering energy in various processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Steam is employed to heat the copper tubes in the defecator and evaporator to facilitate impurity removal and concentration of cider.&lt;SEP&gt;Steam refers to the vaporized water used as a power source for the propulsion in the described machine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b50f15854f0d7a894e3abd39a81c4ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58817a44207e082297ea3faa165db2bf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611965</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Copper Tubes">
      <data key="d0">Copper Tubes</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Copper tubes are used in the defecator and evaporator systems to distribute steam and process cider.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pump">
      <data key="d0">Pump</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A water pump supplies water to cleanse machinery and helps in controlling fermentation during processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sugar Syrup">
      <data key="d0">Sugar Syrup</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The sugar syrup is the concentrated form of cider produced through evaporation, with a specific gravity suitable for jelly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fermentation">
      <data key="d0">Fermentation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Fermentation is a biological process affecting cider, leading to variations in sugar reduction and affecting processing parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;The process by which sugars in fruit-juice or grains are changed into alcohol and gas, producing wine or cider.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d170d1426917b3639e7243e4aade782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31526a8f34a79f3f95e4cc8ce251e822</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612169</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Apples">
      <data key="d0">Apples</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Apples are a fruit processed in the mill, undergoing cleaning, grinding, and pressing to produce cider and jelly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Strainer">
      <data key="d0">Strainer</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The strainer is a device used in the evaporator system to remove impurities from the sugar syrup during processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Trough">
      <data key="d0">Trough</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The trough is a channel used to carry away scum removed from the cider in the defecator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f7506d04f954ae2d1cb0ed256f5c0e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The digestive system in humans is adapted to process various foods, with the ability to accommodate changes in feeding modes and digest a wide range of substances, including water and air, which are essential for nutrition but not considered traditional foods.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system includes organs like the stomach and intestines, responsible for processing food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efb927db7ea2bc4669beaaff8354b755</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612177</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Human Body">
      <data key="d0">Human Body</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The human body contains tissues and fluids such as muscles, bones, blood, and fat, which require nutrients for growth, repair, and energy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Albumin">
      <data key="d0">Albumin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Albumin is a pure form of nitrogenous substance derived from eggs, classified as an albuminate, essential for tissue building and repair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fibrin">
      <data key="d0">Fibrin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Fibrin is a nitrogenous organic substance found in muscles, involved in blood clotting and tissue repair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myosin">
      <data key="d0">Myosin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Myosin is a nitrogenous substance from muscles, playing a crucial role in muscle contraction and energy production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Casein">
      <data key="d0">Casein</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Casein is a nitrogenous substance from milk, important for growth, repair, and nutrition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vegetable Fibrin">
      <data key="d0">Vegetable Fibrin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Vegetable fibrin, or glutin, is a nitrogen-free organic constituent found in grains like wheat, barley, and oats, contributing to nourishment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Legumin">
      <data key="d0">Legumin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Legumin is a nitrogen-free vegetable casein present in peas and beans, serving as an energy source.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fat">
      <data key="d0">Fat</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Fats are organic constituents that do not contain nitrogen, involved in energy storage and heat production.&lt;SEP&gt;Fat supports warmth and comfort in the body, made from specific foods and sometimes improperly by alcohol.&lt;SEP&gt;Fat is a substance needed in the body to keep warm and comfortable, and it can be made from various foods such as fat meat, butter, oil, and sugar.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67b83f16f525fc1670d5a9242f36cd37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efb927db7ea2bc4669beaaff8354b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20452cd28a7a0227706cdf094ea3c581</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612170</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Starch">
      <data key="d0">Starch</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Starch is a carbohydrate that does not contain nitrogen, contributing to energy and animal heat.&lt;SEP&gt;Starch is a carbohydrate found in grains that can be converted into sugar through sprouting and biological processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Starch is a carbohydrate found in foods like potatoes, rice, and corn that can be converted into sugar to feed the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Starch is a complex carbohydrate found in foods like wheat and crackers that can be converted into sugar by saliva.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67b83f16f525fc1670d5a9242f36cd37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31526a8f34a79f3f95e4cc8ce251e822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476f39fcc8c0c7619821c2b1143970d6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612171</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Air">
      <data key="d0">Air</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A gas with weight that exerts pressure, used in industrial ventilation and heating processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Air is a natural substance that has a specific weight per volume, involved in discussions about fans and air resistance.&lt;SEP&gt;Air is vital for respiration and nutrition, supporting life processes without being recognized as food.&lt;SEP&gt;Air is essential for lung function, providing fresh oxygen and removing waste gases; impure air poisons the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The air is described as gritty, swirling particles, and the atmosphere’s darkness, influencing the environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31d0ac870e71dbc7c06985412703656b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-837e785c96036c45397f4d6d019a9eba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b1925755a5c65f004018f45749c7912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-839ff46f67f18fe6822ebc6d38b389d6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613018</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Energy">
      <data key="d0">Energy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Energy in humans derives from oxidation of food substances, primarily fats, starches, and sugars, which can be converted into heat and useful work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Modern Science">
      <data key="d0">Modern Science</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Modern science studies the transformation of food energy into heat and work, explaining the roles of various nutrients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Joule">
      <data key="d0">Joule</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Joule is a unit of energy measurement used in scientific studies of energy derived from food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lyon Playfair">
      <data key="d0">Lyon Playfair</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lyon Playfair was a scientist contributing to the understanding that heat is a form of energy capable of doing work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clausius">
      <data key="d0">Clausius</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Clausius was a scientist associated with the development of principles in thermodynamics relating to energy and heat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tyndall">
      <data key="d0">Tyndall</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Tyndall made contributions to the understanding of energy and heat transfer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Helmholtz">
      <data key="d0">Helmholtz</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Helmholtz was a scientist who contributed to the understanding of energy as a form of motion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Human Body as a Machine">
      <data key="d0">Human Body as a Machine</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The human body is compared to a machine where food acts as fuel, and the work done corresponds to the energy produced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Work and Energy">
      <data key="d0">Work and Energy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The amount of physical work correlates with the quantity of food energy, such as coal in a boiler, needed to perform tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Alcohol">
      <data key="d0">Alcohol</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Alcohol is a chemical and psychoactive substance commonly found in beverages such as beer, cider, wine, brandy, and spirits. It is produced through the fermentation of sugars present in fruit juices and grains, often followed by distillation. While alcohol has been historically used to induce intoxication and alter mental states, it is also recognized as a poison that can cause significant harm to the human body.

As a poison, alcohol damages the brain and nerves, impairing message transmission and reducing overall proper functioning. It affects the nervous system responsible for touch, taste, sight, smell, and hearing, leading to loss of judgment, decreased control, and increased carelessness. Excessive consumption can damage the stomach lining and interfere with digestion, and it has been linked to improper fat formation, such as artificially inserting fat into muscles, which weakens muscle tissue. Alcohol can also poison the blood and impair heart and brain functions, further contributing to health risks.

Moreover, alcohol impacts the body's thermoregulation. Although it temporarily induces a feeling of warmth, it does not keep the body warm; instead, it promotes faster heat loss, increasing the risk of hypothermia or freezing in cold environments. Due to its toxic nature, alcohol can be deadly if consumed excessively and may harm or kill drinkers. Some descriptions also note its use in preservation, such as preventing the decay of dead bodies, highlighting its flammability and chemical properties.

Overall, alcohol is a substance with complex effects—ranging from social and cultural associations to significant health hazards—emphasizing its potential to impair bodily functions, mental acuity, and physical health, while also acknowledging its controversial role in diets and social life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6fb1afd3f1137ef2464bcbf8e6ca0880&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b50f15854f0d7a894e3abd39a81c4ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-339a04378219e12fdd299ea57c43f944&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38509fbb14988b3d2417deb93ee40233&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71a4a564b49b7f7ab5fc34553849e9c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d170d1426917b3639e7243e4aade782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e22299aaf0c9c5e28f9fc9822446874d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20452cd28a7a0227706cdf094ea3c581&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ec816dcf44e2fe3a4afba7c6692bba3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31526a8f34a79f3f95e4cc8ce251e822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efb927db7ea2bc4669beaaff8354b755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c99f53308a3eca211c2bfef8bf90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c40e9d9d6bed631bcdf0022bea513804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4ecf42e13f0e671845a3cd5496327f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd1e306f99fc132d43ccbfdb378c54ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a2b65dd2ccf6640dc6d501c52589484</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612166</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pulley System">
      <data key="d0">Pulley System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The pulley system involves interconnected pulleys and gears transmitting motion, including components like wheels, pins, and belts, to achieve mechanical movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b411b4e86a09df53ef8da9d8229000b8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gear">
      <data key="d0">Gear</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A gear, such as the toothed wheel K or the pinion, is a mechanical component with specified diameter and revolutions per minute, used to transmit torque and rotational movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b411b4e86a09df53ef8da9d8229000b8</data>
