      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pure Caustic Potash">
      <data key="d0">Pure Caustic Potash</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Pure caustic potash is a highly soluble chemical that can be dissolved in water to make soap and should be stored carefully to avoid deterioration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4e3baf81033bb4ebaf7b3fd82e4c171e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Old-fashioned Soap Making">
      <data key="d0">Old-fashioned Soap Making</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The traditional method of making potash or soft soap involved boiling with wood ashes or impure potash, leading to unneutralized soaps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4e3baf81033bb4ebaf7b3fd82e4c171e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Modern Soap Making">
      <data key="d0">Modern Soap Making</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Modern soap making with pure caustic potash involves dissolving it in water, melting fats such as tallow or oils, and mixing to produce neutral, high-quality soap.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4e3baf81033bb4ebaf7b3fd82e4c171e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wool">
      <data key="d0">Wool</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Wool is a fiber obtained from sheep, which benefits from washing with potash soap to clean and preserve its condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4e3baf81033bb4ebaf7b3fd82e4c171e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611668</data>
