    <node id="Headquarters">
      <data key="d0">Headquarters</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Headquarters is a social and drinking spot for local men, including Brindle Bill and other community figures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-029587962a0e769bf41beb4d2b181466</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611957</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Alonzo Phelps">
      <data key="d0">Alonzo Phelps</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Alonzo Phelps is a person whose confession contains strange elements and discusses papers taken from a murdered man in Europe.&lt;SEP&gt;A man tried for murder, who was sentenced to be hanged, wrote a confession of his crimes, and had previously murdered a man in Europe before fleeing to America.&lt;SEP&gt;Alonzo Phelps is a character whose confession is included in a pamphlet found by Don Carlo, illustrating themes of bravery and law defiance.&lt;SEP&gt;Alonzo Phelps was condemned to die and confessed to concealing family papers, which helped identify the family estate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-029587962a0e769bf41beb4d2b181466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6de2fcc61f99e91491ef81fa40580ae4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d6ba5697f9f6ac8decb2198a361839d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f402c259d546a1b4619eed5b798473c5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611957</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chicago">
      <data key="d0">Chicago</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Chicago is a city that features prominently across various contexts, both historical and narrative. Historically, Chicago was initially settled by P. J. Marquette and John Kinzie, and its name derives from a wild onion called "Chikago" by Native American tribes. It was a young, developing city at its inception, earning the description of an infant city during its early years of growth. 

In the narrative context, Chicago serves as a central setting where significant scenes of travel, social interaction, and major events unfold. It is the place where Roxie and Daymon moved to after leaving Louisville, indicating its role as a relocation or new beginning. Roxie Daymon's family history is associated with Chicago, and she resides there in later contexts, suggesting the city as her home or base of operations. 

During wartime, Chicago is depicted as a loyal and fashionable city, characterized by social gatherings such as fashionable parties and a vibrant social scene. It is also portrayed as the home of notable characters like Governor Morock, a reputed fashionable lawyer involved in estate dealings, further emphasizing its status as a hub of social and professional activity. Additionally, Chicago appears in the thoughts of characters like Van Bibber, who reflect on themes such as racing and deception, highlighting its role as a backdrop for various personal and social reflections. 

Overall, Chicago is depicted as a dynamic city with deep historical roots and a lively social fabric, serving both as a setting for personal narratives and as a symbol of loyalty and modernity during significant historical moments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5978182ffc54131ad703e871e79dc392&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58817a44207e082297ea3faa165db2bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789d217af2b17a7981ff4341de893334&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1788abe5b20f786a0ee4558b6fb02743&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c11f042f8e301d4f16b51e506f72228&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08b9d6f937baa20d8292bac71db403dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdabf392a8dc10b75e57591c9eaef5d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764612528</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="P. J. Marquette">
      <data key="d0">P. J. Marquette</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">P. J. Marquette was a French missionary from Canada and the first white man to settle temporarily on the site of Chicago before the American Revolution.&lt;SEP&gt;P. J. Marquette is recognized as the first white settler on the site of Chicago, originally from Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5978182ffc54131ad703e871e79dc392&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08b9d6f937baa20d8292bac71db403dc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="John Kinzie">
      <data key="d0">John Kinzie</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">John Kinzie is regarded as the first permanent settler of Chicago, establishing a home there in 1804.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5978182ffc54131ad703e871e79dc392</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="San Domingo">
      <data key="d0">San Domingo</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">San Domingo (now Santo Domingo) is where a landholder made improvements at the same site as Chicago after P. J. Marquette.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5978182ffc54131ad703e871e79dc392</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Daymon">
      <data key="d0">Daymon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Daymon is a boat hand and laborer in Louisville who was suspected of drowning his wife and was tried for her murder.&lt;SEP&gt;Daymon is the wife of a prisoner who enters the court room wildly and mellowness as a dramatic event.&lt;SEP&gt;Daymon is a day laborer who worked in and around Chicago, eventually becoming wealthy through land sales.&lt;SEP&gt;Daymon is Roxie's husband, who worked as a day laborer, left Louisville for Chicago, and is associated with her life story.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5978182ffc54131ad703e871e79dc392&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6de2fcc61f99e91491ef81fa40580ae4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a9445e78fa0e5990fbfc82fd7af0897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08b9d6f937baa20d8292bac71db403dc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764611958</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Roxie">
      <data key="d0">Roxie</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Roxie is the eldest daughter of Tom Fairfield, who finds wood and makes fire, taking part in caring for the family during the snowstorm.&lt;SEP&gt;Roxie is the elder sister, capable of service, who was taken in by Aunt Fillis and later ran away with her lover.&lt;SEP&gt;Roxie is a character who reflects on her childhood, wealth, and social status, and interacts with Aunt Patsy and others.&lt;SEP&gt;Roxie is a woman who died of cholera, left an only daughter, and was buried unaware of her sister’s fate.&lt;SEP&gt;Roxie is a character involved in a conversation about an estate, testifying, and making decisions with a soft tone of voice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5978182ffc54131ad703e871e79dc392&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c11f042f8e301d4f16b51e506f72228&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d6ba5697f9f6ac8decb2198a361839d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08b9d6f937baa20d8292bac71db403dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab396e5290a1af8b37332288d9ad1b47</data>
