      <data key="d0">Syrinx</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The syrinx, or Pandean pipe, is an ancient instrument resembling a pipe or flute, possibly of Hebrew origin.&lt;SEP&gt;The syrinx, or Pandean pipe, is an ancient instrument with three to nine tubes, commonly seven, used in Greek music.&lt;SEP&gt;The _syrinx_ is a Roman double pipe instrument, consisting of two tubes with either a shared or separate mouth-piece.&lt;SEP&gt;A musical instrument called _huayra-puhura_, made of cane or stone, with reed pipes tied in sets, used by Peruvians.&lt;SEP&gt;The _syrinx_ is a pipe instrument shown in medieval representations, played by performers.&lt;SEP&gt;The syrinx is a wind instrument shown among medieval sculptures of musical instruments.&lt;SEP&gt;Syrinx is a musical instrument, with Greek and Roman associations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001c1f1b3eee1ad8a7fbb7cf02acea78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e7feca07c634684d8111b02a41fcae1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18c204bf67b073ba131e7e3917e9d273&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fea727469cc8526b5cd257ed6247a09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5adf09848918f402d340b581ef777eb1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613874</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fistula">
      <data key="d0">Fistula</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _fistula_ is a Roman double pipe instrument, with tubes of different types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613874</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bagpipe, _Tibia Utricularis_">
      <data key="d0">Bagpipe, _Tibia Utricularis_</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _tibia utricularis_ is a Roman bagpipe, favored by Emperor Nero.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613874</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cornu">
      <data key="d0">Cornu</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The cornu is a Roman lyre with side horns, shaping its appearance and name, used in musical performances.&lt;SEP&gt;The _cornu_ is a large, curved bronze horn used in warfare and possibly for ceremonial purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e7feca07c634684d8111b02a41fcae1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tuba">
      <data key="d0">Tuba</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The _tuba_ is a straight brass trumpet employed for signals in warfare.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Buccina">
      <data key="d0">Buccina</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The _buccina_ is a horn, possibly a conch shell originally, used for signaling in military or ceremonial contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lituus">
      <data key="d0">Lituus</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The _lituus_ is a bent straight trumpet used in Roman musical and military practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tympanum">
      <data key="d0">Tympanum</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _tympanum_ resembled a tambourine and was played by hand.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scabillum">
      <data key="d0">Scabillum</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _scabillum_ was a percussion instrument with two plates hinged together, used to produce rhythmical effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cymbalum">
      <data key="d0">Cymbalum</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The cymbalum is an early percussion instrument from the ninth century.&lt;SEP&gt;The _cymbalum_ consisted of two metal plates similar to modern cymbals.&lt;SEP&gt;A percussion instrument consisting of metal plates suspended on cords, clashed together, with alternative versions using bells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c92bbb1fd6ed4d39b3fbf9331923c3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Crotala">
      <data key="d0">Crotala</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _crotala_ and _crusmata_ were types of castanets, with the _crotala_ being larger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Crusmata">
      <data key="d0">Crusmata</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _crusmata_ were castanet-like percussion instruments, smaller than the _crotala_.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Triangulum">
      <data key="d0">Triangulum</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _triangulum_ was a musical triangle used in Roman music.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sistrum">
      <data key="d0">Sistrum</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The sistrum was a percussion instrument made of a bronze or brass frame with metal bars, often shaped like snakes or geese, used mainly by females in religious performances.&lt;SEP&gt;The sistrum is a percussion instrument, possibly referred to as _menaaneim_ in biblical texts, similar to cymbals.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sistrum is an ancient musical percussion instrument, possibly synonymous with Hebrew _toph_ and related to cymbals, used in religious and ceremonial contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;The _sistrum_ was derived from Egypt and associated with the worship of Isis.&lt;SEP&gt;The sistrum is an ancient Egyptian musical instrument shaken to produce noise, similar to a type of rattling sistrum.&lt;SEP&gt;Ancient Egyptian percussion instrument shaken to produce a rattle-like sound.&lt;SEP&gt;Sistrum is a percussion instrument from Hebrew and Roman cultures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18c204bf67b073ba131e7e3917e9d273&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5adf09848918f402d340b581ef777eb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aef18eb73774c5f975cff80e5f2ce51f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tintinnabula">
      <data key="d0">Tintinnabula</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _tintinnabula_ were metal bells arranged in a frame, used to produce musical sounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Crepitaculum">
      <data key="d0">Crepitaculum</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _crepitaculum_ was a device resembling a hoop with a handle, used to produce rattling sounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Phœnicians">
      <data key="d0">Phœnicians</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Phœnicians were an ancient civilization known for colonizing Sardinia and introducing musical instruments to Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sardinia">
      <data key="d0">Sardinia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Sardinia is an island colonized by the Phœnicians, where traces of their presence and instruments are found.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613875</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arabs">
      <data key="d0">Arabs</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">The Arabs introduced several musical instruments into Spain, which then spread to France, Germany, and England.&lt;SEP&gt;Arabs participated in the plundering of Mogador alongside Berbers and Moors.&lt;SEP&gt;People who took control of Ceuta in the eighth century and used it as a departure point for expeditions into Spain.&lt;SEP&gt;Arab populations in Morocco, including those who are pure and mixed, totaling approximately 3,550,000.&lt;SEP&gt;Arab inhabitants of the region are depicted with their households and customs, including house habits and social behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;The Arab inhabitants of the region, described as hardy, sun-burnt, and living in encampments outside the town.&lt;SEP&gt;Inhabitants known for burying treasure in the region, especially in Morocco.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4115555de86c1bb7160cf84f291006f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01fb77c42a6cfd618f0d29c057ae2768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4996254e95ccc6b78afba2bb6b96949&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c196bdb46661c725027c238489650eb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8afc94ee00703bc5476d62e021e1994c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-232907dd57f71debadc62dcfe71c8543&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e87160352b07ec76435ae0e7abc28b28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1030072f07138e17f0700081a9158b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614547</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Foung-Hoang">
      <data key="d0">Foung-Hoang</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The foung-hoang is a mythical bird from Chinese tradition believed to have provided the musical scale to the Chinese civilization.&lt;SEP&gt;Foung-Hoang is a legendary bird, likened to a phoenix, which Chinese tradition attributes as the source of their musical scale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613876</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Europe">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Europe</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient Europe is the region where Roman and Greek instruments were introduced from Asia through conquests and cultural exchanges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613876</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Implements of the _tibia_">
      <data key="d0">Implements of the _tibia_</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Various shapes and types of _tibia_ (flutes) were used in ancient Roman music, including _tibia dextra_, _tibia sinistra_, and _tibia longa_.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d034b0ebc70d115fe140d386b94caa9e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613876</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Female Singers">
      <data key="d0">Female Singers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of female singers demonstrated a particular hand gesture at a performance, which is culturally associated with specific regions and celebratory singing practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613877</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Syria">
      <data key="d0">Syria</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Syria is a region where women traditionally perform a specific hand gesture during musical or celebratory occasions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613877</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arabia">
      <data key="d0">Arabia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Arabia is a region where women also adopt a similar hand gesture characteristic of producing shrill sounds during festivities.&lt;SEP&gt;Arabia is the region of origin for the Arab conquerors who invaded parts of North Africa and established influential cities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Persia">
      <data key="d0">Persia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Persia is another region associated with the same hand gesture used in celebratory singing.&lt;SEP&gt;Persia is a historical region included among the vast plains suitable for travel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e87160352b07ec76435ae0e7abc28b28</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614608</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dulcimer">
      <data key="d0">Dulcimer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The dulcimer is a stringed instrument, known as _santir_ in Arab and Persian traditions, and as _cimbal_ or _cymbaly_ or _cimbalom_ in European languages.&lt;SEP&gt;The Assyrian dulcimer is depicted but its construction details are unclear due to damage on the relief.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dulcimer is a musical instrument depicted on bas-reliefs, but its construction details are unclear due to damage and repair.&lt;SEP&gt;The dulcimer is a stringed instrument, similar in construction to the Persian _tschenk_, played with a plectrum, and historically used in Persia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95a9160a14d7266e2100e2d55ebcd715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d5c85cef72f59528c1fcf81f532ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aef18eb73774c5f975cff80e5f2ce51f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613877</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Trigonon">
      <data key="d0">Trigonon</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Trigonon is an ancient musical instrument held horizontally, twanged with a long plectrum, frequently appearing on bas-reliefs.&lt;SEP&gt;The trigonon is an ancient Greek stringed instrument with a triangular frame, later modified for strength and elegance.&lt;SEP&gt;Trigonon is an ancient musical instrument, possibly a stringed or percussion type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613878</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Assyrian Trumpet">
      <data key="d0">Assyrian Trumpet</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Assyrian trumpet resembles the Egyptian trumpet and was used in ancient musical performances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613878</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Drums">
      <data key="d0">Drums</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient drums from the mound of Nimroud have distinct shapes, including a sugar-loaf shape, and are made of copper, with some having holes at the top for fastening.&lt;SEP&gt;Percussion instruments known since early centuries, soft in comparison to shrill instruments like cymbalum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613878</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bells">
      <data key="d0">Bells</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Tiny bells, made of copper mixed with tin, with a hole at the top for fastening, have been found, and they withstood time's devastation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bells were brought to the church, and their sounds are linked to the shipwreck legend.&lt;SEP&gt;The lost bells are heard mournfully from the sea depths on winter nights, symbolizing the shipwreck's victims.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f14dbef413cc17a6b6cd1e7d1b659eee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c40a925861c89aa50e0fdf703fdaf56f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615467</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Musical Instruments">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Musical Instruments</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Various musical instruments such as the dulcimer, trigonon, lyre, trumpet, drums, cymbals, and bells were used by the Assyrians and Babylonians in religious and secular contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Ancient musical instruments of American Indians, often made of pottery or bone, revealing characteristics of their musical system and cultural history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93589ec60a74676e9a13006653f3b90f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613878</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="King Nebuchadnezzar">
      <data key="d0">King Nebuchadnezzar</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">King Nebuchadnezzar commanded the playing of various musical instruments such as cornet, flute, harp, sackbut, psaltery, and dulcimer during religious worship of the golden image.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613879</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="King Darius">
      <data key="d0">King Darius</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">King Darius is noted for fasting and not having musical instruments played before him during a specific night, indicating a custom involving music at his court.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613879</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jerusalem">
      <data key="d0">Jerusalem</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Jerusalem's royal court maintained private musical bands and performed sacred music with a large number of singers and instrumentalists during biblical times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613879</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Harp">
      <data key="d0">Harp</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The harp was a common stringed instrument among the Hebrews, similar to those used by Egyptians and Assyrians, but specific Hebrew names are uncertain.&lt;SEP&gt;The harp is a stringed musical instrument used in ancient Greece, with various forms and names such as kinyra and kinnor, influenced by Asiatic origins.&lt;SEP&gt;The harp is a string instrument played by crowned musicians, with a portion of the medieval orchestra scene showing its use.&lt;SEP&gt;Harp is an ancient stringed instrument from Egypt, with references to its historical and cultural importance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5adf09848918f402d340b581ef777eb1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613879</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nebel">
      <data key="d0">Nebel</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nebel is an ancient stringed instrument, comparable to the Asor, used in biblical and historical contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;The nebel is considered a Hebrew instrument, possibly a type of dulcimer, depicted in biblical and ancient contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Nebel is thought to be an instrument of the lute or guitar type, possibly a stringed instrument.&lt;SEP&gt;Nebel was a traveler who examined Mexican instruments and provided descriptions of their construction and use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-863ecd34d59b90a44bfad0a61b39cd89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18c204bf67b073ba131e7e3917e9d273</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613879</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Psanterin">
      <data key="d0">Psanterin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The psanterin may be a Hebrew or ancient instrument, potentially synonymous with the psalterion, related to the dulcimer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613880</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Asor">
      <data key="d0">Asor</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Asor is a ten-stringed ancient musical instrument played with a plectrum, possibly resembling the nebel.&lt;SEP&gt;The asor is a ten-stringed Hebrew instrument played with a plectrum, resembling the nebel, used in ancient music.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613880</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Judas Maccabeus">
      <data key="d0">Judas Maccabeus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Judas Maccabeus was a historical figure associated with the Hebrew revolt, and images of his time depict lyres on coins.&lt;SEP&gt;Judas Maccabeus is associated with Hebrew coins depicting a lyre, indicating the instrument's cultural significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613880</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Instruments">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Instruments</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The text describes various ancient musical instruments such as the dulcimer, trigonon, lyre, trumpet, drums, cymbals, and bells used by Assyrians and Babylonians in religious and secular music.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613880</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nimroud">
      <data key="d0">Nimroud</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Nimroud is an archaeological site where many small copper and tin bells with holes at the top have been found, surviving through time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613880</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Old Hebrew Musical Instruments">
      <data key="d0">Old Hebrew Musical Instruments</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hebrews possessed several musical instruments including the harp, dulcimer, lyre, and the likely presence of others based on biblical and ancient Egyptian and Assyrian representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613880</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Judas Maccab">
      <data key="d0">Judas Maccab</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Judas Maccab is depicted on Hebrew coins associated with the lyre, emphasizing the instrument's cultural and historical significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-210eaafae8dea701b360369916174fd0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peruvian Festivals">
      <data key="d0">Peruvian Festivals</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Peruvian festivals are annual celebrations characterized by music, dancing, and special ceremonies honoring the dead and notable figures such as monarchs, often involving musical performances and processions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inca">
      <data key="d0">Inca</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient civilization in South America with complex military and ceremonial musical practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Ancient civilization in South America; their musical instruments included the _tinya_.&lt;SEP&gt;The Inca was the ruler of the Incan Empire, celebrated through solemn feast ceremonies and associated with noble deeds and achievements.&lt;SEP&gt;The Inca civilization cultivated songs with the word _hailli_, meaning “Triumph,” used to glorify the Inca ruler.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82544a13bf6799b0efc4d2f2a5e62661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73ed7c284b3a9d9caa2237f4d2af971d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prescott">
      <data key="d0">Prescott</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Prescott is an historian who described Peruvian festival observances, including processions, banners, and songs honoring the dead.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Haravi">
      <data key="d0">Haravi</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Haravi refers to the Peruvian elegiac songs performed during festivals, lamenting the dead and celebrating noble deeds, though their originals have been altered or tampered with.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Haravecs">
      <data key="d0">Haravecs</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Haravecs were Minstrels in Peru who composed and recited haravis, the indigenous elegiac songs associated with festivals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nezahualcoyotl">
      <data key="d0">Nezahualcoyotl</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nezahualcoyotl was a Mexican monarch who founded the Council of Music, an institution for promoting arts, sciences, and education.&lt;SEP&gt;Nezahualcoyotl was a wise monarch of the Tezcucans who opposed human sacrifices and dedicated a temple to an unknown god with musical instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a423779aeb8c30b26de6eff15931f31c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chinese Board of Music (_Yo Poo_)">
      <data key="d0">Chinese Board of Music (_Yo Poo_)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The _Yo Poo_ was a Chinese governmental organization responsible for regulating ceremonies, sacrifices, festivals, court solemnities, military reviews, and social events.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient American Indians">
      <data key="d0">Ancient American Indians</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the indigenous peoples of the Americas, with some hypotheses suggesting they originated from regions such as Mongolia, Thibet, Hindustan, Phoenicia, or the lost ten tribes of Israel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tezcuco">
      <data key="d0">Tezcuco</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Tezcuco is an ancient city in Mexico where the Council of Music was founded by Nezahualcoyotl to promote arts and sciences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613882</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mongolia">
      <data key="d0">Mongolia</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Mongolia is hypothesized as one of the regions from which the ancestors of the American Indians may have originated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613882</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thibet">
      <data key="d0">Thibet</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Thibet is suggested as a possible homeland for the ancestors of the American Indians according to some hypotheses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613882</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hindustan">
      <data key="d0">Hindustan</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Hindustan refers to the Indian subcontinent where various traditional musical instruments are possessed and used.&lt;SEP&gt;Hindustan is hypothesized as a potential origin region for the ancestors of the American Indians.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d5c85cef72f59528c1fcf81f532ec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613882</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ten Lost Tribes of Israel">
      <data key="d0">Ten Lost Tribes of Israel</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">The ten lost tribes of Israel are hypothesized to be ancestors of the American Indians, according to some theories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc505eeb3cd53b0544dd308adf0587ad</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613882</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Latin">
      <data key="d0">Latin</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Latin is a classical language in which older works on music were generally written, used for naming musical instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613883</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Psalterium">
      <data key="d0">Psalterium</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The psalterium is an early stringed instrument or musical device.&lt;SEP&gt;The psalterium is an oblong, square-shaped lyre used in Roman music, played with a large plectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;Psalterium is a simple stringed musical instrument of triangular shape, named from Latin origins, with historical significance in medieval music.&lt;SEP&gt;Psalterium is a musical instrument or concept related to musical notation or performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c92bbb1fd6ed4d39b3fbf9331923c3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e7feca07c634684d8111b02a41fcae1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613883</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nablum">
      <data key="d0">Nablum</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Nablum is a musical instrument of uncertain acoustics, engrained from a 9th-century manuscript, possibly a type of stringed instrument.&lt;SEP&gt;The _nablum_ is a small stringed instrument, shown in medieval musical groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5adf09848918f402d340b581ef777eb1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613883</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Salterio">
      <data key="d0">Salterio</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Salterio (English: psaltery) is a trapezium-shaped dulcimer used from the 14th century onwards.&lt;SEP&gt;The salterio is a musical instrument, similar to a dulcimer, played with fingers and depicted in medieval sculptures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001c1f1b3eee1ad8a7fbb7cf02acea78</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613883</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Harpa">
      <data key="d0">Harpa</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Harpa is an almost obsolete Hindu stringed instrument with a triangular frame, similar in construction and shape to the Assyrian harp.&lt;SEP&gt;Harpa is a stringed instrument with a triangular shape, used by Anglo-Saxons and popular among German, Celtic, and Scandinavian musicians.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb8822d3dfa29a3bb48da5205e0808c6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613883</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ullard Harp">
      <data key="d0">Ullard Harp</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ullard harp is a small, ancient harp from Ireland believed to date before the year 800, with sculptures found in the old church of Ullard.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sculptured Cross">
      <data key="d0">Sculptured Cross</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The sculptured cross at Ullard depicts a small harp, possibly older than the monument at Monasterboice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Finnish Harpa">
      <data key="d0">Finnish Harpa</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The Finnish harpa (_harpu_, _kantele_) is a small harp with a frame without a front pillar, used until the early 20th century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rotta">
      <data key="d0">Rotta</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Rotta is a medieval stringed instrument, sounded by plucking or bowing, likely of German or Celtic origin, similar to a lyre.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Suroda">
      <data key="d0">Suroda</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _suroda_ is a Hindu stringed instrument used as a lute or fiddle, exemplifying the use of stringed instruments across cultures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pope Gregory">
      <data key="d0">Pope Gregory</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pope Gregory is associated with the transmission of ancient manuscripts, including a psalter that shows David playing the _rotta_.&lt;SEP&gt;Pope Gregory was a pope who sent an instrument resembling a lyre to St. Augustine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e481d0b0dc9178db4c1b9a451a72ab0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="St. Blasius">
      <data key="d0">St. Blasius</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">St. Blasius is a monastery whose manuscript depicts a _rotta_, illustrating medieval musical instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="King David">
      <data key="d0">King David</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">King David is associated with solemn processions involving Hebrew bands and their musical accomplishments.&lt;SEP&gt;King David is a biblical figure often portrayed with a harp or _rotta_ in religious and historical manuscripts.&lt;SEP&gt;King David is depicted as playing the _rotta_ in an illuminated manuscript.&lt;SEP&gt;A biblical figure depicted holding hammers to strike bells of different sizes in a fourteenth-century manuscript.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6073fe712a13c2a8fa88a32fa74f3062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4839098cd46c452a68e1941bee57dab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e481d0b0dc9178db4c1b9a451a72ab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0658b2c968fe6ee5f181166477508b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Assyrian harp">
      <data key="d0">Assyrian harp</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Assyrian harp is an ancient stringed instrument with tuning-pegs placed on the sound-board, used as a reference for similar instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613884</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Greek harp">
      <data key="d0">Greek harp</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Greek harp is an ancient stringed instrument likely imported from Asia, differing from the Assyrian harp and associated with Greek musical traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kinyra">
      <data key="d0">Kinyra</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The kinyra is a Greek name for the harp, possibly linked etymologically to the Hebrew _kinnor_, and associated with King David's instrument.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kinnor">
      <data key="d0">Kinnor</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Kinnor is a biblical stringed instrument, likely a lyre or small harp, associated with King David and considered the oldest stringed instrument.&lt;SEP&gt;The _kinnor_ is a Hebrew stringed instrument, considered the favorite of King David, translated as "harp" in English Bible versions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lyra">
      <data key="d0">Lyra</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The lyra generally refers to stringed instruments, often held on the lap or in the arms, different from the _kithara_.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ithara">
      <data key="d0">Ithara</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The ithara is a type of lyre, differing in construction and possibly in size from other lyres.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chelys">
      <data key="d0">Chelys</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The chelys is a small lyre with a body made from tortoise shell or imitation, used in ancient Greek music.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Phorminx">
      <data key="d0">Phorminx</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The phorminx is a large early lyre used for solo performances and accompanying recitations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kithara">
      <data key="d0">Kithara</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The kithara is a stringed instrument with a square body held against the chest, used for solo play since at least 700 BC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sinew">
      <data key="d0">Sinew</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Sinew is used for making strings of ancient Greek lyres, twanged with a _plektron_.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Plektron">
      <data key="d0">Plektron</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The plektron is a short stem of ivory or metal used for plucking the strings of ancient Greek lyres.&lt;SEP&gt;Plektron is a plectrum or pick used for stringed instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Greek lyre">
      <data key="d0">Greek lyre</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Greek lyre is an ancient stringed instrument, with variations in design, used for musical performances and associated with the muses Polyhymnia, Kalliope, and Erato.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Magadis">
      <data key="d0">Magadis</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Magadis is a Greek musical instrument of uncertain type, possibly stringed or wind, with origins linked to Asia.&lt;SEP&gt;The magadis, also called pektis, is an ancient Greek instrument with twenty strings tuned in octaves, possibly a dulcimer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sambyke">
      <data key="d0">Sambyke</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The sambyke is an ancient stringed instrument, said to have been invented by Ibykos around 540 BC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613887</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Simmikon">
      <data key="d0">Simmikon</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The simmikon is an ancient Greek instrument with thirty-five strings, likely a dulcimer, named after its inventor Simos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613887</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nabla">
      <data key="d0">Nabla</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nabla is a type of ancient musical instrument mentioned among the foreign and Greek instruments, though its exact nature is uncertain.&lt;SEP&gt;The nabla is a stringed instrument with only two strings, probably similar to the Hebrew nebel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613887</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pandoura">
      <data key="d0">Pandoura</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The pandoura is a Greek lute-like instrument with three strings, used in ancient Greece.&lt;SEP&gt;Pandoura is an ancient stringed musical instrument.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613887</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Monochord">
      <data key="d0">Monochord</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The monochord is a simple musical string instrument used historically for tuning and studying acoustics.&lt;SEP&gt;The monochord is a simple stringed instrument with a single string, used for teaching acoustics and singing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c92bbb1fd6ed4d39b3fbf9331923c3b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613887</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aulos">
      <data key="d0">Aulos</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The aulos is an ancient Greek wind instrument, similar to an oboe or clarionet, with many varieties, held like a flageolet with a vibrating reed.&lt;SEP&gt;Aulos is an ancient wind instrument resembling an oboe or clarionet, with a vibrating reed and distinct construction, used by the Greeks and possibly by the Etruscans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e7feca07c634684d8111b02a41fcae1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613887</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ibykos">
      <data key="d0">Ibykos</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ibykos is traditionally credited with inventing the _sambyke_ around 540 BC, a stringed instrument.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613887</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Simos">
      <data key="d0">Simos</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Simos is credited with inventing the _simmikon_ around 600 BC, a stringed instrument with thirty-five strings, likely a dulcimer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c32645e26c49665a71ca04b6c2eea85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613888</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bagpipe">
      <data key="d0">Bagpipe</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The bagpipe, possibly called _sumphonia_ in ancient texts, is a wind instrument consisting of a bag and pipes.&lt;SEP&gt;The bagpipe is a wind instrument depicted in medieval sculptures, associated with traditional musical practices.&lt;SEP&gt;The bagpipe is an ancient musical instrument favored by Celtic and Slavonic nations.&lt;SEP&gt;The bagpipe is a musical instrument that appears fully retained when expanded with air, featuring a head with horns, and is historically associated with Irish culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4839098cd46c452a68e1941bee57dab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7452f67f575388f5344a993822f4062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001c1f1b3eee1ad8a7fbb7cf02acea78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18c204bf67b073ba131e7e3917e9d273</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613888</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kosa">
      <data key="d0">Kosa</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Kosa is a type of bagpipe made from the whole skin of a goat, used in Poland and Ukraine historically.&lt;SEP&gt;Kosa signifies a goat and is the name given to a bagpipe in the context described.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4839098cd46c452a68e1941bee57dab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7452f67f575388f5344a993822f4062</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613888</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Woodcut">
      <data key="d0">Woodcut</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A woodcut illustration shows varieties of ancient wind instruments with detailed descriptions of their construction and historical significance.&lt;SEP&gt;A carved or engraved picture or design made in wood, possibly used as an illustration for instruments or historical artifacts described in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;A woodcut illustration representing a Scotch bagpipe from the last century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4839098cd46c452a68e1941bee57dab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e7feca07c634684d8111b02a41fcae1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
