    <node id="African Tribes">
      <data key="d0">African Tribes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Various African tribes utilize simple and improved musical instruments similar to the harp and bow, often with sound-boards made from natural materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Some tribes in western and central Africa create percussion instruments with principles similar to the American Indian teponaztli.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95a9160a14d7266e2100e2d55ebcd715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82544a13bf6799b0efc4d2f2a5e62661</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613917</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Damaras">
      <data key="d0">Damaras</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">The Damaras are a native tribe in South-western Africa that use bows as musical instruments by tightening strings with a leathern thong to produce sounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95a9160a14d7266e2100e2d55ebcd715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613917</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gourd">
      <data key="d0">Gourd</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A gourd, hollowed and open at the top, is attached to a bow in some African tribes to serve as a sound-board for musical instrument construction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95a9160a14d7266e2100e2d55ebcd715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613917</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Musical Instrument">
      <data key="d0">Musical Instrument</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Musical instruments exhibit a gradual development through civilizations and serve as insights into the heart of man, reflecting feelings and racial characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Term applied to a percussion instrument of wood and a harmonica-like contrivance called "regal" or "regals."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95a9160a14d7266e2100e2d55ebcd715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5adf09848918f402d340b581ef777eb1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613918</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ethnologist">
      <data key="d0">Ethnologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An ethnologist studies collections of musical instruments to gain insights into the history, health, and civilization of different peoples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95a9160a14d7266e2100e2d55ebcd715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613918</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Santar">
      <data key="d0">Santar</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The _santir_ is an ancient stringed instrument known in Arab and Persian cultures, related historically to the development of the dulcimer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95a9160a14d7266e2100e2d55ebcd715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613918</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Katzenstein">
      <data key="d0">Katzenstein</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Katzenstein was an organ builder in St. Petersburg who was inspired by the Chinese _cheng_ to incorporate the _free reed_ into organ stops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95a9160a14d7266e2100e2d55ebcd715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613918</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Santir">
      <data key="d0">Santir</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Santir is possibly a derived or variant name associated with ancient musical instruments, referenced in historical texts and biblical contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Santir is a stringed Middle Eastern instrument.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613918</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jubal">
      <data key="d0">Jubal</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jubal is mentioned in the Bible as the inventor of the kinnor, the oldest known stringed instrument.&lt;SEP&gt;Jubal is mentioned as "the father of all who handle the harp and organ" in Genesis, representing an early musical instrument inventor or practitioner.&lt;SEP&gt;Jubal is a biblical figure associated with musical instruments, specifically mentioned among ancient instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-662dfab4657790a73298dc9f0446a502</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613918</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Joseph">
      <data key="d0">Joseph</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Joseph, mentioned in biblical history, might be depicted with Hebrew lyres in Egyptian paintings from about 1700 B.C.&lt;SEP&gt;Joseph is a historical figure cited for mentioning a city of Mauritania and contributing to debates on ancient Moroccan history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1b63bc45a96581a48ba223c8d48c07</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Beni Hassan">
      <data key="d0">Beni Hassan</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Beni Hassan is an Egyptian site where ancient paintings potentially depict Hebrew lyres and musical customs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613919</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hieroglyphics">
      <data key="d0">Hieroglyphics</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Hieroglyphics are used for interpreting ancient Egyptian inscriptions that might depict Hebrew musical instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613919</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Osirtasen">
      <data key="d0">Osirtasen</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Osirtasen I was an Egyptian Pharaoh potentially linked with the period of Joseph and the depiction of Hebrew musical instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613919</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Egyptian Scribe">
      <data key="d0">Egyptian Scribe</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An Egyptian scribe is depicted as recording the arrival of Hebrew ancestors, possibly including their musical instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68ba8bec314d618fd6ba2fced04ef111</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613919</data>
      <data key="d6" />
