    </node>
    <node id="Gittith">
      <data key="d0">Gittith</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Gittith is likely a stringed instrument, possibly a harp or similar, referenced in psalm headings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613921</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jobel">
      <data key="d0">Jobel</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Jobel is a trumpet or horn, possibly a loud and cheerful blast used for particular occasions, or a symbol for the instrument's sound.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613921</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Greeks">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Greeks</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ancient Greeks are a civilization known for their contributions to music, with many instruments derived from or influenced by earlier cultures, and depicted in art and artifacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Ancient Greeks are referenced in relation to the _magadis_, a double-pipe musical instrument possibly similar to the Poongi.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04d5c85cef72f59528c1fcf81f532ec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613921</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Greek Instruments">
      <data key="d0">Greek Instruments</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Greeks possessed various musical instruments, many of which are derived from Asia, such as the lyre, kithara, and others like the Magadis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613921</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polyhymnia">
      <data key="d0">Polyhymnia</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyhymnia is one of the Greek muses associated with sacred poetry and music, depicted with a harp on Greek artwork.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613921</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Greek Harp">
      <data key="d0">Greek Harp</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Greek harp, similar to the Assyrian harp, has thirteen strings and was likely imported from Asia, illustrating cultural exchange in musical instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613921</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Strabo">
      <data key="d0">Strabo</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Strabo was an ancient Greek geographer who provided descriptions of musical instruments and their origins in different regions, including Asia and Greece.&lt;SEP&gt;Strabo was an ancient Greek geographer who cataloged North African oasis and peoples like the Nasamones, Getulians, and Garamantes.&lt;SEP&gt;Strabo was a Greek geographer who described Mauritania as fertile, well-watered, and abundant in trees and resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Strabo was an ancient geographer who described the richness and fertility of Mauritania.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dac59a77b85c004bd29ffff6b594c7f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026c31ea51debe67089624a49cd283a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sambuka">
      <data key="d0">Sambuka</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sambuka is an ancient musical instrument of foreign origin, mentioned by classical sources as part of the Greek or Asian musical systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613922</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Barbiton">
      <data key="d0">Barbiton</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Barbiton is an ancient stringed instrument associated with Greek music, possibly imported or derived from Asian traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7da70d39d1688e8eb35f0af080c5cf6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613922</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kouei">
      <data key="d0">Kouei</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Kouei, known as the Orpheus of the Chinese, was a performer on the _king_, an ancient Chinese instrument capable of drawing animals and subjugating them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613922</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fohi">
      <data key="d0">Fohi</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Fohi was the legendary founder of the Chinese empire, attributed with the invention of some of their early stringed instruments, living around B.C. 3000.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613922</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Niuva">
      <data key="d0">Niuva</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Niuva was a supernatural female credited with inventing the most important Chinese instruments and systematic sound arrangements during Fohi's era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613922</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pien-King">
      <data key="d0">Pien-King</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The _pien-king_ is an advanced Chinese musical instrument with tones tuned according to _lu_ intervals, and is regarded as a modern evolution of older _king_ instruments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613923</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tse-King">
      <data key="d0">Tse-King</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Tse-king is a solitary sonorous stone used as a rhythmical instrument in Chinese music, similar to drums.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613923</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tchung">
      <data key="d0">Tchung</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The _tchung_ is an ancient Chinese bell, made of copper and tin alloy, used to indicate time and in musical performances, with various forms like _té-tchung_ and _pien-tchung_.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613923</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hiuen-Tchung">
      <data key="d0">Hiuen-Tchung</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The _hiuen-tchung_ is an oval-shaped ancient Chinese bell with symbolic ornamentation, used during Confucius worship and in the Han dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0d3d720b9e8db10b828dd12d97de70e</data>
