      <data key="d0">Melozzo da Forli</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An artist who painted a picture featuring a regal with keys resembling pistons.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613933</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Percussion Instrument">
      <data key="d0">Percussion Instrument</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes drums, cymbalum, bunibulum, and other metal or wood instruments producing rhythm or sound effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bunibulum">
      <data key="d0">Bunibulum</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An instrument with an angular frame and metal plates, shaken to produce sound similar to a sistrum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Glass Harmonica">
      <data key="d0">Glass Harmonica</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A toy instrument with slips of glass arranged to produce harmonies, resembling a musical toy of the past.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Theodosius">
      <data key="d0">Theodosius</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Theodosius the Great was a Roman emperor under whom the sculptured obelisk depicting the pneumatic organ was erected in Constantinople towards the end of the fourth century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Beverley Minster">
      <data key="d0">Beverley Minster</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A church in England that contains a sculptured figure of a man playing a regal organ.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melrose Abbey">
      <data key="d0">Melrose Abbey</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">An old Scottish church with a sculptured figure of an angel holding a double regal organ.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grassineau">
      <data key="d0">Grassineau</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Grassineau described the "Rigols," a percussion instrument, in historical context and detailed its structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bombulom">
      <data key="d0">Bombulom</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An instrument made of an angular frame with metal plates, shaken like a sistrum to produce sound, also called bunibulum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-242f71a68c104bb1e671b3e7c72468fa</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Figures in the Roof">
      <data key="d0">Figures in the Roof</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Figures depicted on the roof of Peterborough Cathedral, some of which resemble modern instruments like the violin, providing evidence of medieval craftsmanship and iconography.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03c5035205433abd8b211b1d1712aea6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Instrument">
      <data key="d0">The Instrument</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The instrument depicted in figures on the roof bears a surprising resemblance to the modern violin, especially in its incurvations and sound-holes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03c5035205433abd8b211b1d1712aea6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Restorers">
      <data key="d0">Restorers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Restorers attempted to preserve and restore the roof and its figures, with debates over whether alterations have been made that affected the historical authenticity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03c5035205433abd8b211b1d1712aea6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The _Organistrum_">
      <data key="d0">The _Organistrum_</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The _organistrum_ is a medieval musical instrument that required two persons to sound it, involving a wheel mechanism and keys to produce different tones, considered a precursor to the hurdy-gurdy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03c5035205433abd8b211b1d1712aea6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Monochord">
      <data key="d0">The Monochord</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The monochord is a simple stringed musical instrument with a single string stretched over two bridges, used mainly by theorists to study musical scales.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03c5035205433abd8b211b1d1712aea6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The _Chorus_ or _Choron_">
      <data key="d0">The _Chorus_ or _Choron_</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Medieval wind instrument with one or two tubes, sometimes resembling a bagpipe or horn, possibly derived from the Hebrew _Keren_, Greek _Keras_, or Latin _cornu_.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03c5035205433abd8b211b1d1712aea6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613934</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Flutes">
      <data key="d0">The Flutes</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Medieval flutes blown at the end, similar to the flageolet, with some tubes tied together like the Pandean pipe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03c5035205433abd8b211b1d1712aea6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613935</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The _Syrinx_">
      <data key="d0">The _Syrinx_</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient instrument consisting of multiple tubes inserted into a bowl-shaped base, with illustrations from the ninth and tenth centuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03c5035205433abd8b211b1d1712aea6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764613935</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
