    <node id="Fez">
      <data key="d0">Fez</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Fez is a major city and imperial capital in Morocco, renowned for its rich history, cultural significance, and political influence. Historically, Fez served as the capital of the Edristees dynasty, recognized as the first Moroccan dynasty mentioned in historical records, and was once a powerful kingdom that continues to hold importance in Morocco’s heritage. It has been a royal city within the Moroccan Empire, celebrated for its arts, learning, and industry, and has often been associated with Moorish doctors of Spain. 

The city is characterized by its ancient fortifications, including seven gates, two castles, and a decaying wall, reflecting its historical and architectural significance. Its old and new parts are densely built, illustrating successive transformations from its ancient origins to the present day. Fez has been a cultural hub, especially noted for its prominence as a center of arts and learning. The city’s history includes notable figures such as Muley Suleiman, who was celebrated as an emperor, underscoring its political and historical importance.

Geographically, Fez is an inland city situated within a region marked by significant mountain heights within the 'Systeme Atlantique,' and it has experienced various political and commercial interactions over its long history. In the 19th and early 20th centuries, Fez played a role in international influence dynamics; for example, British influence sought to prevent French invasion of the region. The city remains a key regional hub, with populations varying from about ten or twelve thousand in some parts engaged in commerce to approximately fifty thousand in broader contexts. Merchants from Rabat frequently conduct business in Fez, especially in the interior regions, and the city historically engaged in trade relations with Sahara communities. Overall, Fez embodies a blend of historical grandeur, cultural depth, and ongoing regional significance within Morocco.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73c170ba2d6f65788ae52ee29483e59d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02e1fb091464c03ab00842920bc21f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97d99746fa60d96d3fbab1f89af585c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb3f38fdfb7168489cd1fb4adcb2515&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284546e32da930f297907ef0f48339cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d2e2382323fa2c2ced43dc3587ea2a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3032c2aaf4f583ac650213201f7041d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ee1dd105b3b5398ba93ec7c92e6dc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45af3d0999432adf74bdb708ed276f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a0feaae427cf9b154a9a737a238afba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f075dca5ffdfa89a91a9f3f686330e0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4d805a74697d9faea388c9f6f2958a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dac59a77b85c004bd29ffff6b594c7f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78c973a4a4c99e07c3571a6d29d1779d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fd702921e8d3a990c6c7e84369cba36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-203f252ae42306fa9f42171f8e2f0f23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
