    </node>
    <node id="Muley Ismail">
      <data key="d0">Muley Ismail</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Muley Ismail, died in 1727, a prominent ruler.&lt;SEP&gt;Muley Ismail is referred to as "the Numidian Tiger," a Moroccan ruler noted for his strength and reputation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-feec165bb57c6356de7d23684253d46b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a0feaae427cf9b154a9a737a238afba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f075dca5ffdfa89a91a9f3f686330e0f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614542</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muley Abdullah">
      <data key="d0">Muley Abdullah</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Muley Abdullah, died in 1757, a ruler in the Moroccan dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-feec165bb57c6356de7d23684253d46b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614542</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muley Yezeed">
      <data key="d0">Muley Yezeed</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Muley Yezeed, assumed the surname El-Mahdee in 1792.&lt;SEP&gt;Muley Yezeed assumed the surname El-Mahdee in 1792; known as a historical figure associated with the throne.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-feec165bb57c6356de7d23684253d46b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f075dca5ffdfa89a91a9f3f686330e0f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614542</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muley Hisham">
      <data key="d0">Muley Hisham</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Muley Hisham, died in 1794, a ruler in Morocco.&lt;SEP&gt;Muley Hisham was proclaimed Sultan in 1794 and a significant prince of the Shereefian lineage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-feec165bb57c6356de7d23684253d46b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f075dca5ffdfa89a91a9f3f686330e0f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614542</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arab Tribes">
      <data key="d0">Arab Tribes</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Arab tribes are described as burying large sums of money in different parts of Barbary, particularly in Morocco, following ancient customs without fear from sovereigns or sheikhs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sidi Bou Aly">
      <data key="d0">Sidi Bou Aly</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Sidi Bou Aly is a religious site in Nefta, associated with a marabet (religious place) within the town.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sidi Mustapha Azouz">
      <data key="d0">Sidi Mustapha Azouz</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sidi Mustapha Azouz is a well-known saint and religious figure in Nefta, respected for his benevolence and wisdom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Amour Abeda">
      <data key="d0">Amour Abeda</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Amour Abeda is mentioned as a fanatic, contrasting with Sidi Mustapha Azouz’s benevolent character.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sheikh">
      <data key="d0">Sheikh</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sheikh governs some fifteen hundred Shelouhs in the district of Shtouka.&lt;SEP&gt;A sheikh is a leader or elder in the Saharan villages, governing the community.&lt;SEP&gt;A religious or community leader who has been installed recently, likely a figure of authority among the Moors or local inhabitants.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sheikh is a religious leader in Nefta, highly venerated and involved in charitable activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4115555de86c1bb7160cf84f291006f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01fb77c42a6cfd618f0d29c057ae2768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fd702921e8d3a990c6c7e84369cba36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86994d5a5ba62d67eaecd5aaeeb5aab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sgougou">
      <data key="d0">Sgougou</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sgougou is a seed called by Arabs, eaten with honey, resembling dried apple-seed, and distributed by religious figures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wad Nefta">
      <data key="d0">Wad Nefta</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Wad Nefta is a spring in Nefta that supplies water to gardens and forests of date, orange, pomegranate, and fig trees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Shereefs">
      <data key="d0">Shereefs</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Shereefs, also called Oulad Ali, are the current Moroccan dynasty founded by Hasein, with roots in Medina, Arabia.&lt;SEP&gt;Shereefs are descendants of the Prophet, claiming a right to the Moroccan throne and involved in the political history of Tafilett.&lt;SEP&gt;Shereefs are royal or noble families of the Empire, belonging to a dynasty of Sufi descent, with African blood, holding the dynasty of the current ruler.&lt;SEP&gt;Shereefs are descendants of the Prophet and inhabit the castle town of Kesar, and the area around Tafilett.&lt;SEP&gt;Shereefs are notable inhabitants of Tednest, historically residing there.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shereefs are noble landowners and traditional nobles of Nefta, from whom the Bey appoints governors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcfd21521e87c975db82bce68bfa5b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97d99746fa60d96d3fbab1f89af585c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d2e2382323fa2c2ced43dc3587ea2a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67512ea13edfd2cab9973f3ae5f8c75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-203f252ae42306fa9f42171f8e2f0f23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rebot">
      <data key="d0">Rebot</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Rebot is a central market area in Nefta situated on the banks of Wad Nefta.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jewish Artisans">
      <data key="d0">Jewish Artisans</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Jewish artisans are tolerated in Nefta under certain conditions, such as wearing a black handkerchief and restrictions on riding horses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Braun">
      <data key="d0">Braun</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Braun is the name of the Kaed (leader) of the Jereed (a group or vessel), associated with the local Arab community in Nefta.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ghadumes">
      <data key="d0">Ghadumes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ghadumes is a town from which Nefta is fifteen days' journey away, part of the Tunisian territories.&lt;SEP&gt;Ghadumes is a town where Turkish-established restrictions have negatively impacted trade between the Jereed and the Desert.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86994d5a5ba62d67eaecd5aaeeb5aab4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sidi Ahmed">
      <data key="d0">Sidi Ahmed</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sidi Ahmed is a town named in the surrounding villages of Nefta, reflecting local settlements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Safer">
      <data key="d0">Safer</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Safer is a region or landscape area associated with the local geography of parts of Nefta and the Sahara.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-54d2a15489fc52241d68e4fa40e99203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
