    <node id="Berbers">
      <data key="d0">Berbers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Berbers are an indigenous ethnic group of North Africa, comprising a diverse array of tribes with distinct origins, histories, and cultural characteristics. They have traditionally inhabited regions across North Africa, including areas such as Rif, Garet, Azgar, Amayeeghs, and Tednest. Notable tribes among the Berbers include the Beni-Arous, Masmuda, Zeneta, Sanhagha, and others, each contributing to the rich tapestry of Berber identity and history.

Historically, the Berbers have played significant roles in regional conflicts and trade. They are known to have aided in plundering Mogador during specific conflicts, and they have been involved in various battles and interactions with invading and ruling civilizations throughout history. Their resistance to external influences has been a defining feature, enabling them to maintain a degree of cultural independence despite external pressures, including Arab and other foreign influences. This resilience is reflected in their efforts to preserve their traditions, language, and social structures.

The Berbers have also been associated with terms such as Moors and Barbarians, reflecting their long history and interactions with various civilizations over centuries. Historically, some Berber tribes engaged in herding, grazing herds and flocks, and exporting products like skins. In regions such as Tetuan, the Berber population was documented to number around eight thousand, highlighting their significant presence in local demographics. These tribes have historically been semi-barbarous, involved in trade, conflicts, and cultural exchanges that have shaped their societal development.

Overall, the Berbers represent a resilient and ancient population that has preserved its unique cultural identity and influence in North Africa over millennia, maintaining their traditions amidst changing political and social landscapes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4115555de86c1bb7160cf84f291006f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284546e32da930f297907ef0f48339cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4996254e95ccc6b78afba2bb6b96949&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67512ea13edfd2cab9973f3ae5f8c75d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbd510e5f844be534e59040af85f777&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c196bdb46661c725027c238489650eb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4d805a74697d9faea388c9f6f2958a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8d3ba708724915d4c95066561027635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026c31ea51debe67089624a49cd283a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11883b983a6b12419bdd608231cd321f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614553</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="European Jews">
      <data key="d0">European Jews</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">European Jews are a community residing in Mogador who are subjected to violence and depredation during the chaos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614553</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ruffians">
      <data key="d0">Ruffians</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">The ruffians are marauding bands engaging in violence, pillage, and depredation in Mogador during the chaos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614553</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Water-Port">
      <data key="d0">Water-Port</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The water-port is a key point for escape, where Mr. Robertson and others coordinate their escape out of Mogador.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614553</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Lucas">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Lucas</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mr. Lucas is a member of the fleeing party who devises a plan to communicate with the fleet using planks and grass cordage.&lt;SEP&gt;Mr. Lucas is a young man who demonstrated self-devotion, sagacity, and indefatigable exertions by constructing a raft to communicate with a fleet, and was recognized with some mark of favor by the British Government.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea2d548d08b4129bfc47eab34719160b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614554</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fleet">
      <data key="d0">Fleet</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The fleet refers to the naval squadron offshore capable of aiding the beleaguered and fleeing civilians.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea2d548d08b4129bfc47eab34719160b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614555</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Marauding Band">
      <data key="d0">Marauding Band</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">The marauding band is a violent group attacking and pillaging Mogador, threatening the fleeing families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614555</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="British Jews">
      <data key="d0">British Jews</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">European Jews in Mogador are subjected to violence, depredation, and destruction during the chaos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614555</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Native Moors">
      <data key="d0">Native Moors</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">The Moors are the local inhabitants who fought fiercely during the bombardment and later fled Mogador.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614555</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arab Marauders">
      <data key="d0">Arab Marauders</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">The marauding bands led by their chiefs are violent groups attacking the city during the chaos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c246ad7923d08e2ef2366af23b4c19f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614555</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Professor Lee">
      <data key="d0">Professor Lee</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Professor Lee of Cambridge translated English texts into Arabic concerning the abolition of slavery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614555</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="British Merchants of Mogador">
      <data key="d0">British Merchants of Mogador</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">British merchants in Mogador received an anti-slavery circular authored by the narrator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="El-Jereed">
      <data key="d0">El-Jereed</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">El-Jereed, or Belad-el-Jereed, is a part of the Sahara known as the country of dates and palm branches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Salt Lake (El-Sibhah)">
      <data key="d0">Salt Lake (El-Sibhah)</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">El-Sibhah, or Salt Lake, is a large saltplain divided into parts, with specific geographic and historic significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Palus Tritonis">
      <data key="d0">Palus Tritonis</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Palus Tritonis is a historic name for part of the salt lake, associated with the ancient river Triton.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Palus Pallas">
      <data key="d0">Palus Pallas</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Palus Pallas is a section of the salt lake named after the tradition of Pallas accompanying Sesostris.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Palus Libya">
      <data key="d0">Palus Libya</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Palus Libya is another part of the salt lake, historically distinguished among the lake's divisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biledulgerid">
      <data key="d0">Biledulgerid</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Biledulgerid is a name used by geographers for the region including El-Jereed, borrowed from Arabians meaning the dry country.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arabians">
      <data key="d0">Arabians</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Arabians are a group of people described as pure in the context of their ethnicity or culture.&lt;SEP&gt;Arabian peoples invaded North Africa in the 7th century CE, originating from various provinces of Arabia, and were divided into groups such as Ismaelites, Amalekites, Koushites, etc.&lt;SEP&gt;Arabians are credited with naming the region "Biledulgerid" indicating the dry country.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbd510e5f844be534e59040af85f777&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8d3ba708724915d4c95066561027635</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grand Salt Lake">
      <data key="d0">Grand Salt Lake</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The salt lake, measuring about seventy miles, contains dry islands and salt deposits that vary seasonally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dates">
      <data key="d0">Dates</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Dates are a staple food in El-Jereed, harvested from date palms, and form a significant resource.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary trade resource of the country, exported in large quantities and widely known for their fine quality.&lt;SEP&gt;Dates are fruits harvested in the region, with several varieties including Degalah, Trungah, and Monachah.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a3a1103e1d6efafaf897eb994dbaa44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1030072f07138e17f0700081a9158b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Date-Palm">
      <data key="d0">Date-Palm</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The noble date-palm grows in the Sahara and is a significant tree in Moroccan desert regions.&lt;SEP&gt;The date-palm is an important tree in El-Jereed, used for food, beverage, baskets, poetry, and irrigation.&lt;SEP&gt;A palm tree abundant in North Africa, propagated from shoots or stones, which bears dates and produces sap used as a beverage and spirit.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a3a1103e1d6efafaf897eb994dbaa44</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arabian Poets">
      <data key="d0">Arabian Poets</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Arabian poets have described the palm's leaves as hanging gracefully, emphasizing its beauty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4bc0fa20a8bb60c2f53107ccd7d2bbec</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abd-el-Kader">
      <data key="d0">Abd-el-Kader</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Abd-el-Kader is a marabout warrior, greatly revered and idolized by Muslims across North Africa.&lt;SEP&gt;Abd-el-Kader was an Emir engaged in hostilities against the Moroccan ruler, revered as a marabout warrior.&lt;SEP&gt;Abd-el-Kader was a leader whose followers once resided in Fez, reflecting historical resistance against authority.&lt;SEP&gt;A leader seeking refuge and support among Berber tribes in Rif; resisted the Imperial government.&lt;SEP&gt;Abd-el-Kader was a prominent figure whose companions threw salt into the sea in his honor, during his passage from Oran to Toulon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcfd21521e87c975db82bce68bfa5b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d2e2382323fa2c2ced43dc3587ea2a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ee1dd105b3b5398ba93ec7c92e6dc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c196bdb46661c725027c238489650eb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d121b85033bc8ae8ffcb392dcbbcf78</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oran">
      <data key="d0">Oran</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Oran is a city in Algeria, from which Abd-el-Kader passed during his journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Toulon">
      <data key="d0">Toulon</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Toulon is a city in France, where Abd-el-Kader's passage was headed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Graeberg de Hemso">
      <data key="d0">Graeberg de Hemso</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Graeberg de Hemso is an author or source referenced concerning the Imperial Shereefian pedigree.&lt;SEP&gt;Graeberg de Hemso was a consul-general for Sweden and Sardinia who wrote about Moroccan sovereigns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcfd21521e87c975db82bce68bfa5b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muley Abd-ur-Bakliman">
      <data key="d0">Muley Abd-ur-Bakliman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Muley Abd-ur-Bakliman is a reigning Moroccan sovereign, praised and prayed for by Graeberg de Hemso.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yezeed">
      <data key="d0">Yezeed</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Yezeed was a tyrant of Moroccan origin, a son of Erin, known for his cruelty and unpredictable behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f075dca5ffdfa89a91a9f3f686330e0f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614557</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sidi-Mohammed">
      <data key="d0">Sidi-Mohammed</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sidi-Mohammed was a ruler of Morocco who had a harem with women from many regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614558</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Moroccan Sultanate">
      <data key="d0">Moroccan Sultanate</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Moroccan Sultanate was ruled by Muley Abd-ur-Bakliman and later by Yezeed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614558</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Maurici">
      <data key="d0">Maurici</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Maurici, Maurus, or Maurusci are terms derived from Latin, linked to the dark complexion of the Moors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614558</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mauri">
      <data key="d0">Mauri</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The term Mauri refers to the Moors, originally Latin for dark-complexioned peoples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614558</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bar">
      <data key="d0">Bar</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The word _bar_ signifies land or earth, used in derivations of "Barbary."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614559</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="De Haedo">
      <data key="d0">De Haedo</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">De Haedo authored a work describing the land of Barbary as a "land of Moros," emphasizing its inhuman and indolent nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614559</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Moros">
      <data key="d0">Moros</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Moros is a term used to describe the Moors, often associated with the land of Barbary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614559</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Los Moros">
      <data key="d0">Los Moros</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Los Moros, mentioned by De Haedo, refers to the Moors, a term used to describe the Islamic peoples of North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e6cf13c2e9f84e3b490bbe727b69df5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614559</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Shereefian Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Shereefian Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Shereefian Dynasty is the ruling family in Morocco, originating from Medina in Arabia and beginning in the 16th century.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shereefian Dynasty is the ruling family of Morocco, claiming descent from the Prophet and maintaining political legitimacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dcfd21521e87c975db82bce68bfa5b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d2e2382323fa2c2ced43dc3587ea2a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614559</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tripoli">
      <data key="d0">Tripoli</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Tripoli is a regency of the Port of Tunis, governed by a Pasha.&lt;SEP&gt;Tripoli is a city in North Africa, and its inhabitants are called Tripoline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dcfd21521e87c975db82bce68bfa5b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8d3ba708724915d4c95066561027635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdfdc15ee78020d5a5e9bc96f2c72f6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614559</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Algeria">
      <data key="d0">Algeria</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Algeria has become an integral part of France via the Republic.&lt;SEP&gt;Algeria is an eastern border country of Morocco.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dcfd21521e87c975db82bce68bfa5b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-680c330ee8036afcbacb673ce690b640</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614559</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imperial Shereefian pedigree">
      <data key="d0">Imperial Shereefian pedigree</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Imperial Shereefian pedigree details the genealogical lineage of the Shereefian dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dcfd21521e87c975db82bce68bfa5b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genealogical Tableau">
      <data key="d0">Genealogical Tableau</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The genealogical tableau of the Imperial Shereefian pedigree presents their lineage and descent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6dcfd21521e87c975db82bce68bfa5b9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muley Moluc">
      <data key="d0">Muley Moluc</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Muley Moluc was a figure who died very ill in a litter during a fight, but his death was kept secret to avoid discouraging the Moors.&lt;SEP&gt;Muley Moluc was a Moorish prince who died ill after the battle at El-Kesar, with his death kept secret.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284546e32da930f297907ef0f48339cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Don Sebastian">
      <data key="d0">Don Sebastian</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Don Sebastian's death in 1578 led to Ceuta passing from Portuguese to Spanish control.&lt;SEP&gt;Don Sebastian was a prince who perished along with other nobility in the battle, representing the Portuguese nobility and chivalry of the time.&lt;SEP&gt;Don Sebastian was the Portuguese king who perished at the Battle of The Three Kings in 1578.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284546e32da930f297907ef0f48339cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-026c31ea51debe67089624a49cd283a6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Battle of the Moors">
      <data key="d0">Battle of the Moors</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The battle was a significant conflict involving Moors and Portuguese nobility, marked by loss of princes and nobles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="El-Kesar">
      <data key="d0">El-Kesar</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">El-Kesar is a place in latitude 35 deg. 11' N, longitude 5 deg. 49' 30" W; associated with geographic and historical context of the region.&lt;SEP&gt;El-Kesar, also called Al-Kesar, is an imperial city in Morocco, expanded by Sultan of Fez, Almansor.&lt;SEP&gt;El-Kesar is a common name for fortified towns, often written as Alcazar by Spaniards, indicating strategic military sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284546e32da930f297907ef0f48339cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1b63bc45a96581a48ba223c8d48c07</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Edris Ben Abdalluh">
      <data key="d0">Edris Ben Abdalluh</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Edris Ben Abdalluh was a descendant of Ali and Fatima who established himself at Oualili, was venerated for sanctity, and became a leader of the Faithful.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Edris-Ben-Edris">
      <data key="d0">Edris-Ben-Edris</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Edris-Ben-Edris was the son of Edris Ben Abdalluh, the first bona-fide Mussulman sovereign of the Maroquine empire, and founder of Fez.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Foz">
      <data key="d0">Foz</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Foz, also called Fas, is a city in Morocco, historically a notable Muslim capital and second in rank among Islamism cities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mahadin">
      <data key="d0">Mahadin</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mahadin was a venerable figure, associated with the lineage of Edris Ben Abdalluh, exemplifying sanctity and leadership.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Meknasat">
      <data key="d0">Meknasat</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Meknasat was an ancient name for Miknas, a city founded by the Berbers of Zenatah in the tenth century, from which the modern name derives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Miknas">
      <data key="d0">Miknas</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Miknas is a city in Morocco, founded by Berbers in the tenth century, with extensive ancient ruins and surrounded by a formidable wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ruins of Morocco">
      <data key="d0">Ruins of Morocco</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The ruins of Morocco are ancient, possibly Roman, and have supplied building materials for royal cities, located about a mile from Miknas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Leo Africanus">
      <data key="d0">Leo Africanus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Leo Africanus was a traveler or historian who previously described Fez as a marvelous city in his accounts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Algiers">
      <data key="d0">Algiers</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Algiers is a city in North Africa founded in the 16th century by Andalusian cavaliers and pirates, becoming a significant maritime city.&lt;SEP&gt;Algiers is a city in North Africa founded in part by Andalusian cavaliers and the Barbarossas, forming the basis for modern Algiers.&lt;SEP&gt;Algiers was part of the Arab conquests during the eighth century, influencing North African history.&lt;SEP&gt;Algiers is the destination Santa Maria would proceed to after reaching Beja.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdfdc15ee78020d5a5e9bc96f2c72f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbd510e5f844be534e59040af85f777</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oualili">
      <data key="d0">Oualili</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Oualili was the city where Edris Ben Abdalluh settled and was buried, an important center of religious and political influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Arabic">
      <data key="d0">Arabic</data>
      <data key="d1">language</data>
      <data key="d2">Arabic is the language in which the name Mequinez (Miknas) and related terms are derived, and contextualizes regional history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614562</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mahometan">
      <data key="d0">Mahometan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mahometan refers to the followers of Muhammad, with Fez described as a significant Mahometan capital.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614562</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Veneration">
      <data key="d0">Veneration</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Veneration describes the high respect and sanctity attributed to figures like Edris Ben Abdalluh and Mahadin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614562</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fut">
      <data key="d0">Fut</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Fut is an ancient name associated with Fez, possibly derived from the Prophet Nahum, used in philological contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Fut is a district or city of ancient importance, mentioned in biblical and historical contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5b31b92f9b032efc61f97bef8d8b3ec2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1b63bc45a96581a48ba223c8d48c07</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614562</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Zawiat-Muley-Driss">
      <data key="d0">Zawiat-Muley-Driss</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Zawiat-Muley-Driss is a renowned city of the province of Fez, situated at the foot of mountains, with historical significance related to Edris.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97d99746fa60d96d3fbab1f89af585c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614562</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muley Edris">
      <data key="d0">Muley Edris</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Muley Edris is the founder of the city of Fez, venerated by the people, with his mausoleum inviolably preserved.&lt;SEP&gt;Muley Edris is the progenitor and founder of the dynasty of Edris, commemorated in the sanctuary at Zawiat-Muley-Driss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97d99746fa60d96d3fbab1f89af585c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45af3d0999432adf74bdb708ed276f29</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614562</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tuilet">
      <data key="d0">Tuilet</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Tuilet is an ancient site, previously known by this name, possibly the ancient city of Volubilis, located near Zawiat-Muley-Driss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97d99746fa60d96d3fbab1f89af585c9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614562</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kesar Faraoun">
      <data key="d0">Kesar Faraoun</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Kesar Faraoun, or Castle of Pharaoh, is a set of ruins near Zawiat-Muley-Driss, associated with ancient times.&lt;SEP&gt;Kesar Faraoun, also called "Castle of Pharaoh," is an ancient city with magnificent ruins, indicative of historical significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97d99746fa60d96d3fbab1f89af585c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78c973a4a4c99e07c3571a6d29d1779d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614563</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dubdu">
      <data key="d0">Dubdu</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Dubdu is an ancient city in the district of Shaous, once the residence of an independent prince, now in decay.&lt;SEP&gt;Dubdu, also known as Doubouton, is an ancient, large city in the district of Shaous, now fallen into decay, situated on the sides of a barren mountain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97d99746fa60d96d3fbab1f89af585c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78c973a4a4c99e07c3571a6d29d1779d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614563</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="North Coast">
      <data key="d0">North Coast</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The north coast region is characterized by blowing winds nine months out of twelve, with varied surface features such as hills and plains, and is considered one of the most healthy areas in Africa after Tunis, due to the absence of marshy districts that cause diseases in hot climates. It has a climate similar to Spain in the north and less rainfall with more desert heat in the south, with significant mountain ranges and rivers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614563</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Miltsin">
      <data key="d0">Miltsin</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Miltsin is the highest peak of the Atlas Mountains, over 15,000 feet above sea level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614563</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rif Coast">
      <data key="d0">Rif Coast</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Rif coast features a mountainous chain of considerable height, with the Atlantic coast mainly consisting of hills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614563</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wad Draha">
      <data key="d0">Wad Draha</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Wad Draha is a river that traverses Morocco towards the Sahara, contributing to the region’s water system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614563</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lake Debaia">
      <data key="d0">Lake Debaia</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lake Debaia is a freshwater lake in Morocco, traversed by Wad Draha.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614563</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lake Gibel-Akhder">
      <data key="d0">Lake Gibel-Akhder</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lake Gibel-Akhder is a freshwater lake in Morocco, compared by Leo to Lake Bolsena.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Shebbel">
      <data key="d0">Shebbel</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">The Shebbel, or Barbary salmon, is a fish species found abundantly in Moroccan markets, tasting like herring but more delicate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Moroccan River">
      <data key="d0">Moroccan River</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Various rivers originating from the Atlas feed into the Sahara, including Wad Draha, but generally are not navigable far inland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wheat and Barley">
      <data key="d0">Wheat and Barley</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Wheat and barley are the primary cultivated crops on Moroccan plains and open countries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gums, Almonds, Ostrich-Feathers, Wax, Wool, Skins">
      <data key="d0">Gums, Almonds, Ostrich-Feathers, Wax, Wool, Skins</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">These products are significant exports from Morocco’s southern regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Eloeondenron Argan">
      <data key="d0">Eloeondenron Argan</data>
      <data key="d1">plant</data>
      <data key="d2">The Argan tree produces fruit similar to olives with hard stones enclosing white almonds that produce rancid oil, found in southern Morocco, particularly around Mogador.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Argan Forests">
      <data key="d0">Argan Forests</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Several Argan forests are located near Mogador in Morocco.&lt;SEP&gt;The Argan Forests are a region known for argan trees, located near Mogador.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fd702921e8d3a990c6c7e84369cba36</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Forests of Dwarfish Oak">
      <data key="d0">Forests of Dwarfish Oak</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The northern plains are covered with forests of dwarf oak, some bearing sweet or bitter acorns, and cork trees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cedar of Lebanon">
      <data key="d0">Cedar of Lebanon</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The cedar of Lebanon is found in the Atlas Mountains and has also been identified in Algeria on high mountains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Palmetto">
      <data key="d0">Palmetto</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The palmetto, or dwarf-palm, grows alongside the date-palm in Morocco.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ginson, Snouber">
      <data key="d0">Ginson, Snouber</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Ginson and Snouber, or pine of Aleppo, are trees used in Morocco for preparing fine leathers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Moroccan Mines">
      <data key="d0">Moroccan Mines</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Morocco's mineral resources include gold, silver, lead, iron, tin, sulphur, copper, salt, and antimony, with several rich and ancient mines.&lt;SEP&gt;Morocco has mineral mines of gold, silver, lead, iron, tin, sulfur, salt, and antimony, though most are neglected or unexploited.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb3f38fdfb7168489cd1fb4adcb2515</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Elala and Stouka">
      <data key="d0">Elala and Stouka</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Elala and Stouka are towns in the province of Sous, known for several rich silver mines.&lt;SEP&gt;Elala and Stouka are provinces in Morocco's Sous region, mentioned in the context of mineral resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb3f38fdfb7168489cd1fb4adcb2515</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Elala">
      <data key="d0">Elala</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Elala is a province in the Moroccan Sous region, mentioned as a location with mineral resources such as mines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614564</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stouka">
      <data key="d0">Stouka</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Stouka is a province in the Moroccan Sous region, referenced alongside Elala as a location known for mineral resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f69d89c3a43b14f48ca5e3c600b7a494</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614565</data>
