    <node id="Camel">
      <data key="d0">Camel</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">The camel is a desert animal that subsists on coarse grass, weeds, or thorns without carrying provender.&lt;SEP&gt;Camel is a beast of burden, with a load capacity of about five cantars, used in desert trade and transport.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39746a374c565c0e58462818e02e77f9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chiefs">
      <data key="d0">Chiefs</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Some chiefs have recently joined the party, bringing their wives and riding camels in a palanquin-like contrivance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Camel Palanquin">
      <data key="d0">Camel Palanquin</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A palanquin or shut-up machine with a mast and shrouds, used to carry women riding camels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mineral Springs">
      <data key="d0">Mineral Springs</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The hot, sulphurous, mineral springs are noted for containing fish and snakes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Snake">
      <data key="d0">Snake</data>
      <data key="d1">creature</data>
      <data key="d2">The snake nourishes itself entirely on the fish in the hot springs, living together in the springs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8afc94ee00703bc5476d62e021e1994c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mr. Honneger">
      <data key="d0">Mr. Honneger</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mr. Honneger made a sketch of the fish living in the hot springs.&lt;SEP&gt;Mr. Honneger is a person who observes a snake that nourishes itself upon fish near hot springs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8afc94ee00703bc5476d62e021e1994c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prince Puekler Muskau">
      <data key="d0">Prince Puekler Muskau</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Prince Puekler Muskau traveled in Tunis and mentioned a warm spring near the ruins of Utica with hot waters.&lt;SEP&gt;Prince Puekler Muskau traveled in Tunis and narrates about warm springs, turtles inhabiting basins, and archaeological sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8afc94ee00703bc5476d62e021e1994c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Feriana">
      <data key="d0">Feriana</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Feriana is a spring-filled area with hot and mineral waters, noted for containing fish and snakes in its hot springs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Utica">
      <data key="d0">Utica</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Utica is an ancient site near which Prince Puekler Muskau mentioned a warm spring with hot waters.&lt;SEP&gt;Utica is an ancient city near warm springs, mentioned as the location where turtles inhabit a basin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712990bb2ebe5d62b532d770bfe4b95c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8afc94ee00703bc5476d62e021e1994c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ptolemy">
      <data key="d0">Ptolemy</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ptolemy was an ancient Greek geographer who divided Mauritania, including Algeria and Morocco, among tribes such as Gaetuliae and Melanogaeluti.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Libyans">
      <data key="d0">Libyans</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Libyans are indigenous inhabitants of North Africa, first occupied by these tribes according to historical sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Libyans are among the earliest peoples occupying North Africa, according to historical accounts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Getulians">
      <data key="d0">Getulians</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">Getulians are tribes in North Africa, resisting alliances with invaders and forming part of the native tribes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Garamantes">
      <data key="d0">Garamantes</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">Garamantes are a people who colonized Ghadames and Fezzan oases in North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canaanites">
      <data key="d0">Canaanites</data>
      <data key="d1">people</data>
      <data key="d2">Canaanites are ancient inhabitants said to have been expelled from Palestine, according to Byzantine historian Procopius.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Procopius">
      <data key="d0">Procopius</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Procopius was a Byzantine historian who documented that Canaanites fled Palestine and settled in North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tigisis">
      <data key="d0">Tigisis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Tigisis was an ancient site where Procopius claimed a column inscribed with the Canaanite expulsion was located; its exact location is uncertain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Phoenicians">
      <data key="d0">Phoenicians</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Phoenicians established a presence in North Africa around 820 years before the common era, founding Carthage.&lt;SEP&gt;Phoenicians are an ancient people who colonized various cities and provinces of Europe, linked to the Caucasian race.&lt;SEP&gt;The Phoenicians, also called Carthaginians, established the city of Carthage and engaged in early relations with indigenous peoples in North Africa.&lt;SEP&gt;The Phoenicians were an ancient maritime civilization linked historically to Cornwall's distant history.&lt;SEP&gt;Phoenicians were ancient traders and miners known to have worked with early colonists in Cornwall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbd510e5f844be534e59040af85f777&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8d3ba708724915d4c95066561027635&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2a4525b14048f3353d702b98f67ccda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9567484ec2e94de37ab803e0df4f01d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764615485</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Carthage">
      <data key="d0">Carthage</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Carthage was an ancient city-state founded by Phoenicians in North Africa, serving as a major hub before Roman conquest.&lt;SEP&gt;Carthage was an ancient city founded approximately 820 years before the current era, known for its historical significance in early North African civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-73f52bfe54d47067a25b5b0c44706763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cbd510e5f844be534e59040af85f777</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sidi Amour Abeda">
      <data key="d0">Sidi Amour Abeda</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sidi Amour Abeda is a saint venerated and visited during the journey, associated with sanctity.&lt;SEP&gt;A saint of particular sanctity, visited and honored by the travel party.&lt;SEP&gt;Sidi Amour Abeda is a revered Moorish saint known for exceeding sanctity, whose remains are to be deposited in a future burial-place and is often worshiped with devout obeisance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-232907dd57f71debadc62dcfe71c8543&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25c04a34bdc315c8eeefbaa0ff20978</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kaed">
      <data key="d0">Kaed</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Kaed's house is a notable place where the saint accompanies visitors, and the old Kaed is depicted as ill and weeping due to his son's arrival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-232907dd57f71debadc62dcfe71c8543</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764614600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
