    <node id="Sir W. Batten">
      <data key="d0">Sir W. Batten</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Sir W. Batten was a prominent figure associated with the early 17th-century naval and political spheres, as well as a social host and acquaintance of Samuel Pepys. In the historical context, Sir W. Batten was involved in naval affairs, notably participating in discussions about the Navy's financial condition alongside figures such as Sir W. Pen. He is referenced in Pepys's writings as borrowing money from him, indicating some degree of financial interaction and personal familiarity.

In addition to his political and naval engagements, Sir W. Batten was a gentleman who frequently hosted social gatherings, entertaining Pepys and others at his residence. These visits included dinners, social companionship, and even viewing unusual creatures, suggesting he played a significant role in his social circle as a convivial host. He was also involved in visits to ships and discussions about the Navy, further emphasizing his connection to maritime affairs.

Throughout Pepys's accounts, Sir W. Batten appears as a person of social standing, engaged in both official naval matters and personal interactions. His involvement spanned financial exchanges, social visits on Sundays, and hosting gatherings that contributed to the social and professional networks of the period. Overall, Sir W. Batten was a notable figure intertwined with naval politics, social life, and personal relationships in 17th-century England as documented by Samuel Pepys.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac1ce8602395a115fb7f15acd4fc13c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986db80f0b248cae0ba30adbca747f75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846325a7078b3d463558889100c878b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4aff12629bd3d15955833b8aa9864691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63be821a7049668cd27a886c0bcf0d2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bcea781bcee54c12362d64f503a3692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acb7515cd29b4382b802b389628bd15c</data>
