    <node id="Brampton">
      <data key="d0">Brampton</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Brampton is a locale that holds significance across various contexts. It is primarily a land location that has been discussed in relation to purchase and land transactions, indicating its potential as a site of real estate or property interest. The name also refers to a place frequently visited by the author's father, suggesting familial or personal ties to the area and its local affairs. In another account, Brampton is associated with Mrs. Norbury's house and land, which the narrator contemplates acquiring, highlighting its role as a residential or estate location.

Additionally, Brampton is linked to family connections, as it is where the narrator's family and uncle reside. The references to the uncle's death and family matters imply that Brampton may serve as a significant family or ancestral home. The narrator's narrative journey includes returning to Brampton after visiting Cambridge and Gravely, further emphasizing its importance within personal and regional geography.

Historically or narratively, Brampton is identified as a town visited by Samuel Pepys, who stays overnight at his father's house there, indicating its relevance in historical accounts or personal visits. Overall, Brampton is a multifaceted location, embodying land, family residence, and a point of personal or historical significance within the broader context of the narrative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac1ce8602395a115fb7f15acd4fc13c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986db80f0b248cae0ba30adbca747f75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4aff12629bd3d15955833b8aa9864691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80d293af5e7df136bc3f73cb98ae3fad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2923fc0c238f18810f053d6e390e261d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bcea781bcee54c12362d64f503a3692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ed7144a6153903011bff75ac1d5f586&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df7930a888490c21dbb03d3e8332e3f8</data>
