    <edge source="Sterne" target="Goethe">
      <data key="d7">11.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The relationship between Johann Wolfgang von Goethe and Laurence Sterne encapsulates a profound literary influence and admiration that spanned much of Goethe's life. Goethe’s association with Sterne primarily revolves around Sterne's significant impact on German literary circles, as evidenced by Goethe's acknowledgment of Sterne’s influence and his appreciation for how Sterne shaped his literary development. Goethe was notably inspired by Sterne’s works and ideas, particularly those related to sentimentalism and humor, which contributed to the evolution of his own literary style. Throughout his life, Goethe frequently referenced and re-read Sterne’s writings, demonstrating his deep admiration for Sterne’s literary genius.

This influence extended beyond mere inspiration; Goethe incorporated Sterne’s themes and stylistic elements into his own works and discussions. He drew upon Sterne’s writings to sharpen his powers of observation and to critique false ideals prevalent in society. His references to Sterne often served to deepen his understanding of human nature and literary expression, solidifying Sterne’s role as a key intellectual influence. Goethe’s conversations and writings reveal a recognition of Sterne’s literary significance, and towards the end of his life, Goethe explicitly appreciated Sterne’s genius.

Further, Goethe’s engagement with Sterne’s work is reflected in his satirical piece, “Der Triumph der Empfindsamkeit,” which mocks the sentimental movement and imitators of Sterne’s “Werther,” indicating a nuanced and complex relationship with Sterne’s influence—both admiring and critically engaging with Sterne’s legacy. Overall, Goethe’s relationship with Sterne is characterized by profound admiration, ongoing reference, and a lasting influence on his literary and philosophical outlook.</data>
      <data key="d9">admiration,appreciation,connection,cultural,imitation,influence,inspiration,literary admiration,literary influence,observation,plagiarism,recognition,references,relationship,satire</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-458f12b07f966802c9353e65d5abc548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-073c32eb7cfcec94cf8952d9901d1c9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58047f97c3f5cb05bd0a35cf0a18f86d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c29ec3cd76c13c7da9bd30bfc253b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc9514ef0c01ef6384fdf0e7549eeccb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-559b00cb6dc756396bfc420ba8cc5cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c128264faa77a054077d4c6bfd355759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58d87bc060205995efa9baf4bddb1a07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-835b2b68b00b2bd674ad666036ecbc29</data>
      <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
