    <edge source="Goethe" target="Herder">
      <data key="d7">14.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The relationship between Goethe and Herder encompasses their significant roles in shaping German cultural and literary history. Herder played an influential role in guiding Goethe's early reading, notably introducing him to Johann Georg Sulzer and Sterne's works, which profoundly impacted Goethe's literary imagination and development. Beyond their intellectual influence, Herder was an active participant in Goethe's circle, exchanging numerous letters and ideas that fostered a dynamic scholarly and personal connection.

Both Goethe and Herder are widely recognized as major figures in German literature and philosophy, frequently discussed and studied together within academic and cultural contexts. They are often considered leading representatives of the German literary movement, contributing to the development of German cultural identity and literary tradition. Their joint prominence has led scholars to frequently pair them as central figures in German literary and cultural history, highlighting their influence on each other's work and the broader intellectual landscape of 18th and early 19th centuries Germany.

Overall, the relationship between Goethe and Herder is characterized by intellectual exchange, mutual influence, and their shared status as foundational figures in Germany's literary and philosophical development. They continue to be regarded as central to German cultural history, often associated within scholarly discourse as key contributors to the shaping of German literary tradition and cultural identity.</data>
      <data key="d9">Person,correspondent,influence,mentorship,philosopher</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-2dc64252e1565a88b1d7729211672d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-003097ce0b9615e69c58849744d9a337&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c128264faa77a054077d4c6bfd355759</data>
